window.ROADMAP = {"tiers":[{"id":0,"name":"Fundamentals","blurb":"Everyone starts here. What AI is, how to talk to it, and where it breaks."},{"id":1,"name":"Foundational","blurb":"The base skills each track builds on: prompting well, coding, math, and basic concepts."},{"id":2,"name":"Intermediate","blurb":"You start building: APIs, classic ML, neural nets, RAG, automations, strategy."},{"id":3,"name":"Advanced","blurb":"Production-grade work: agents, evals, transformers, fine-tuning, security."},{"id":4,"name":"Expert","blurb":"Specialist depth: inference optimization, alignment, multi-agent systems, AI-native orgs."},{"id":5,"name":"Frontier","blurb":"Open research questions. Read papers, follow labs, contribute."}],"lanes":[{"id":"tools","name":"Using AI","hue":200},{"id":"biz","name":"Business & Product","hue":35},{"id":"safety","name":"Safety & Ethics","hue":350},{"id":"foundations","name":"Math & Theory","hue":265},{"id":"code","name":"Code & Data","hue":150},{"id":"ml","name":"Machine Learning","hue":95},{"id":"dl","name":"Deep Learning","hue":315},{"id":"llm","name":"LLMs","hue":230},{"id":"apps","name":"LLM Apps & RAG","hue":180},{"id":"agents","name":"Agents & Automation","hue":15},{"id":"ops","name":"Production & Ops","hue":55}],"roles":[{"id":"aie","name":"AI Engineer","short":"AI Eng","blurb":"Builds products on top of LLMs: RAG, agents, tool use, evals, shipping to users."},{"id":"mle","name":"ML Engineer","short":"ML Eng","blurb":"Trains, tunes, and serves models. Lives between data, GPUs, and production."},{"id":"ds","name":"Data Scientist","short":"Data Sci","blurb":"Turns data into decisions with statistics, classic ML, and clear communication."},{"id":"rs","name":"Research Scientist","short":"Research","blurb":"Pushes the frontier: new architectures, training methods, alignment, interpretability."},{"id":"pm","name":"AI Product Manager","short":"PM","blurb":"Decides what AI should build, how it feels to users, and how to measure it."},{"id":"biz","name":"Business Owner / Leader","short":"Business","blurb":"Finds where AI makes or saves money, picks vendors, and leads adoption safely."},{"id":"nc","name":"No-code Automator","short":"No-code","blurb":"Wires AI into workflows with Zapier, Make, n8n, and custom assistants. No heavy coding."},{"id":"pu","name":"Everyday Power User","short":"Power user","blurb":"Uses AI daily to write, research, analyse, and create faster and better."}],"nodes":[{"id":"what-is-ai","title":"What AI Is (and Isn't)","tier":0,"lane":"tools","prereqs":[],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"AI is software that learns patterns from data instead of following rules a programmer wrote by hand. Today's chat assistants are large language models (LLMs): systems trained on huge amounts of text to predict what comes next, which turns out to let them write, summarize, reason through problems, and use tools.","why":"Everything else on this map builds on a clear picture of what these systems are. If you know what is really happening under the hood, you can tell when to trust AI, when to double-check it, and when not to use it at all.","depth":"Aim for a solid mental model, not technical detail. You don't need to know how neural networks are trained; you do need to know that AI predicts likely answers rather than looking up true ones, and that it can be confidently wrong.","hours":4,"outcomes":["You can explain in plain words the difference between AI, machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI.","You can describe what an LLM does when it answers a question, and why that means it can make things up.","You can name tasks AI is reliably good at today and tasks where it still struggles.","You can spot hype in an AI claim and ask the right follow-up question."],"project":"Write a one-page explainer called 'What AI is, in our house (or company)' for a friend or coworker who has never used it. Include three things it does well, three it gets wrong, and one real example you tested yourself where it confidently made a mistake.","resources":[{"title":"Elements of AI","url":"https://www.elementsofai.com/","type":"course","source":"University of Helsinki","free":true},{"title":"AI For Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true},{"title":"Large Language Models explained briefly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LPZh9BOjkQs","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"AI Fluency: Framework & Foundations","url":"https://anthropic.skilljar.com/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","type":"course","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Intro to Large Language Models","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zjkBMFhNj_g","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"What Is Artificial Intelligence (AI)?","url":"https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/artificial-intelligence","type":"article","source":"IBM","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"AI, machine learning, deep learning, generative AI","desc":"These terms nest inside each other. AI is the broad goal, machine learning is learning from data, deep learning uses large neural networks, and generative AI creates new text, images, or audio.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Elements of AI","url":"https://www.elementsofai.com/","type":"course","source":"University of Helsinki","free":true}]},{"title":"How a language model produces an answer","desc":"An LLM breaks text into tokens and repeatedly predicts the next one. Training on vast text gives it broad knowledge and skills, but it has no built-in fact checker.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Large Language Models explained briefly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LPZh9BOjkQs","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"What AI is good and bad at","desc":"AI ability is 'jagged': it can ace a hard exam question and then fail a simple counting task. Learn the rough shape of that frontier so you pick the right jobs for it.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Centaurs and Cyborgs on the Jagged Frontier","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/centaurs-and-cyborgs-on-the-jagged","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Assistants, reasoning models, and agents","desc":"A chat assistant answers a message. A reasoning model 'thinks' step by step before answering, which helps on hard problems. An agent can take actions on its own, like searching, running code, or editing files, over many steps.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Training vs. using a model","desc":"Building a model costs huge amounts of data and compute and happens once. Using it (called inference) is what you do every time you send a prompt. Most people and businesses only ever use models.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Intro to Large Language Models","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zjkBMFhNj_g","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Separating hype from reality","desc":"Claims like 'AI will replace all jobs' or 'it's just autocomplete' both miss the mark. Learn to ask what was measured, on which task, and compared to what.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"AI as Normal Technology (newsletter)","url":"https://www.normaltech.ai/","type":"article","source":"Arvind Narayanan & Sayash Kapoor","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-landscape","title":"The AI Tool Landscape","tier":0,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["what-is-ai"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"a","ds":"a","rs":"a","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"The AI tool landscape is the map of products you can actually use: general assistants like ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and Copilot, plus specialist tools for search, writing, coding, meetings, images, audio, video, and automation.","why":"New tools appear every week, and most are thin wrappers around a few underlying models. Knowing the categories lets you pick the right tool quickly, avoid paying twice for the same thing, and ignore most of the noise.","depth":"Know the main assistant families and the categories of specialist tools well enough to choose. You don't need to track every launch or benchmark; you do need a simple way to compare tools on your own tasks.","hours":5,"outcomes":["You can name the major general-purpose assistants and what each is known for.","You can sort a new AI product into a category and tell whether it adds real value over a general assistant.","You can compare two tools on a task you care about with a fair side-by-side test.","You can explain the difference between free, paid, and business/enterprise plans, including what happens to your data."],"project":"Pick three real tasks from your week (for example: drafting an email, summarizing a PDF, planning a trip). Run each through two different assistants with the identical prompt. Score the outputs in a simple table and write two sentences on which tool you'll default to and why.","resources":[{"title":"A Guide to Which AI to Use in the Agentic Era","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/a-guide-to-which-ai-to-use-in-the","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"How I use LLMs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EWvNQjAaOHw","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"The best AI productivity tools","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/best-ai-productivity-tools/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true},{"title":"Artificial Analysis: model comparisons","url":"https://artificialanalysis.ai/","type":"tool","source":"Artificial Analysis","free":true},{"title":"AI Index Report","url":"https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index","type":"article","source":"Stanford HAI","free":true},{"title":"Simon Willison's Weblog","url":"https://simonwillison.net/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"General-purpose assistants","desc":"ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini (Google), and Microsoft Copilot are the big four. They overlap a lot, but differ in writing style, tool integrations, file handling, and which apps they live inside.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Guide to Which AI to Use in the Agentic Era","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/a-guide-to-which-ai-to-use-in-the","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Models vs. products","desc":"A model (like a specific GPT, Claude, or Gemini version) is the engine. A product (the chat app, a writing tool, a meeting recorder) wraps a model with an interface and features. Many products let you pick which model runs underneath.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Specialist tool categories","desc":"AI search and research (Perplexity, deep research modes), meeting notes, writing aids, coding assistants, image/audio/video generators, and automation platforms. Each solves a narrower job, usually with better workflow fit.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The best AI productivity tools","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/best-ai-productivity-tools/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true}]},{"title":"Plans, pricing, and data handling","desc":"Free tiers have usage limits and may use your chats for training unless you opt out. Business and enterprise plans usually exclude your data from training and add admin controls. Check the settings before pasting anything sensitive.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Reading leaderboards and benchmarks","desc":"Sites like Artificial Analysis compare models on quality, speed, and price. They are useful for a shortlist, but your own tasks are the real test.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Artificial Analysis","url":"https://artificialanalysis.ai/","type":"tool","source":"Artificial Analysis","free":true}]},{"title":"Open-weight and local models","desc":"Some models (like Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma) can be downloaded and run on your own computer or servers. That trades some convenience and capability for privacy and control.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"prompting-basics","title":"Prompting Basics","tier":0,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["what-is-ai"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"Prompting is how you ask AI for what you want. Good basic prompts give context, a clear task, and a description of what a good answer looks like, then you refine through conversation.","why":"The same model gives weak or excellent results depending on how you ask. A few simple habits get most people from 'AI is underwhelming' to 'AI saves me hours a week'.","depth":"Master the everyday habits: context, clear asks, examples, and iterating. You don't need special 'magic words' or long templates; modern models respond best to plain, specific instructions, like briefing a smart new colleague.","hours":5,"outcomes":["You can write a prompt that includes the context, the task, and the format you want.","You can improve a weak answer by giving feedback in the same chat instead of starting over.","You can use examples and documents in a prompt to steer the result.","You can recognize when to start a fresh chat because the conversation has gone off track."],"project":"Take one task you do every week. Write a one-line prompt for it and save the result. Then rewrite the prompt with context, audience, format, and an example, and compare. Keep the improved version in a notes file as the start of your personal prompt library.","resources":[{"title":"Prompt engineering overview","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Google Workspace with Gemini Prompt Guide","url":"https://workspace.google.com/learning/content/gemini-prompt-guide","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"AI prompt guide: How to write effective AI prompts","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/ai-prompt/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true},{"title":"How I use LLMs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EWvNQjAaOHw","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Learn Prompting","url":"https://learnprompting.org/","type":"course","source":"Learn Prompting","free":true},{"title":"Google AI Essentials","url":"https://grow.google/ai-essentials/","type":"course","source":"Google","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Give context like you're briefing a colleague","desc":"Say who you are, who the output is for, and why you need it. The model can't read your mind, and missing context is the most common cause of generic answers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Prompting best practices: be clear and direct","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices#be-clear-and-direct","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Be specific about the output","desc":"State length, tone, structure, and audience. 'Three bullet points for a busy CFO' beats 'summarize this'.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Show examples","desc":"Pasting one or two examples of what good looks like (a past email, a sample format) is often the fastest way to get the style you want.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Iterate in conversation","desc":"Treat the first answer as a draft. Tell the model what to change, ask it to try three alternatives, or ask what information it needs from you.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Working with AI: Two paths to prompting","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/working-with-ai-two-paths-to-prompting","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Attach files and sources","desc":"Upload the document, spreadsheet, or screenshot instead of describing it. Answers grounded in your material are far more accurate than answers from the model's memory.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Know when to start fresh","desc":"Long chats fill up with old instructions and mistakes that keep influencing answers. A clean chat with a better first prompt often beats ten corrections.","level":"deep","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ai-for-business-101","title":"AI for Business: Where It Fits","tier":0,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["what-is-ai"],"roles":{"aie":"a","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"r","pu":"r"},"summary":"This is the business owner's overview of where AI fits: which kinds of work it helps with, what it costs, what risks it brings, and how other companies are using it today.","why":"Leaders face constant pressure to 'do something with AI'. A clear picture of where AI actually creates value helps you avoid both wasted pilots and missed opportunities.","depth":"Understand the main categories of business value and the main risks well enough to lead a conversation. You don't need technical knowledge of models; you do need to know that AI works best on specific tasks inside processes, not as a vague 'transformation'.","hours":6,"outcomes":["You can describe the main ways AI creates business value: saving time, improving quality, growing revenue, and enabling new offerings.","You can explain the difference between tasks AI can fully automate and tasks where it assists a person.","You can name the key risks (accuracy, data privacy, legal, reputational) and basic mitigations.","You can point to real examples of AI use in your industry."],"project":"Write a one-page 'AI in our business' brief: list your five biggest areas of work, mark which tasks in each AI could assist or automate, note one competitor or peer example, and list the top three risks with a one-line guardrail for each.","resources":[{"title":"Generative AI for Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/generative-ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true},{"title":"AI For Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true},{"title":"AI in the Enterprise","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/ai-in-the-enterprise.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Real-world gen AI use cases from leading organizations","url":"https://cloud.google.com/transform/101-real-world-generative-ai-use-cases-from-industry-leaders","type":"article","source":"Google Cloud","free":true},{"title":"Co-Intelligence: Living and Working with AI","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/741805/co-intelligence-by-ethan-mollick/","type":"book","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":false},{"title":"Work Trend Index","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index","type":"article","source":"Microsoft","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Tasks, not jobs","desc":"AI rarely replaces a whole role. It takes on specific tasks: drafting, summarizing, classifying, answering routine questions, extracting data. Think about work as a list of tasks to find the fit.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Anthropic Economic Index","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/economic-index","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Where the value shows up","desc":"Common wins are customer support, sales and marketing content, internal knowledge search, document processing, coding, and analysis. Look at what peers in your industry have already shipped.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Real-world gen AI use cases from leading organizations","url":"https://cloud.google.com/transform/101-real-world-generative-ai-use-cases-from-industry-leaders","type":"article","source":"Google Cloud","free":true}]},{"title":"Augment vs. automate","desc":"Augmentation helps a person do the job faster or better and keeps them in charge. Automation lets AI do it end to end. Most companies should start with augmentation, then automate what proves reliable.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Costs","desc":"Costs include subscriptions or usage fees, setup and integration work, training staff, and the time spent checking output. Seat-based tools are predictable; usage-based APIs scale with volume.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Risks and guardrails","desc":"AI can be wrong, leak sensitive data if used carelessly, and create legal exposure. A simple acceptable-use policy and business-grade plans address most early risks.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"How to Implement AI Responsibly","url":"https://hbr.org/2024/05/how-to-implement-ai-responsibly","type":"article","source":"Harvard Business Review","free":false}]},{"title":"Agents in business","desc":"AI agents can run multi-step work like processing an order or researching leads. They are powerful but need clear limits, logging, and human sign-off on important actions.","level":"deep","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ai-limitations","title":"Hallucinations & Limits","tier":0,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["what-is-ai"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"AI models can sound confident and still be wrong. A hallucination is when a model produces something plausible but false, like a fake citation, a wrong number, or an API function that does not exist.","why":"Every AI user hits this on day one. Knowing where models fail tells you when to trust an answer, when to check it, and when not to use AI at all.","depth":"Everyone must master the practical habits: spot risky tasks, check facts that matter, and give the model sources to work from. You only need to know that the technical causes exist (next-token prediction, training that rewards guessing); you don't need to study them.","hours":4,"outcomes":["You can explain in plain words why a language model can invent facts while sounding sure of itself.","You can tell which tasks are high-risk for hallucination (citations, niche facts, exact numbers, recent events) and which are low-risk.","You can use grounding, \"say I don't know\" instructions, and source checks to cut errors.","You can describe other common failure modes: sycophancy, stale knowledge, weak arithmetic, and losing track of long inputs."],"project":"Pick a topic you know well. Ask an AI assistant 15 questions about it: 5 common facts, 5 obscure facts, and 5 requests for sources. Check every answer and log which kinds of questions failed. Then repeat the obscure ones with a pasted source document and \"say I don't know if unsure,\" and compare the error rate.","resources":[{"title":"Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT (hallucination section)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7xTGNNLPyMI","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Why language models hallucinate","url":"https://openai.com/index/why-language-models-hallucinate/","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Reduce hallucinations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Centaurs and Cyborgs on the Jagged Frontier","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/centaurs-and-cyborgs-on-the-jagged","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"Simon Willison on hallucinations","url":"https://simonwillison.net/tags/hallucinations/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Extrinsic Hallucinations in LLMs","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2024-07-07-hallucination/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Why models hallucinate","desc":"A model predicts likely text. It has no built-in fact database, and training and benchmarks have often rewarded a confident guess over admitting uncertainty.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Why language models hallucinate","url":"https://openai.com/index/why-language-models-hallucinate/","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"The jagged frontier","desc":"AI can be excellent at a hard task and fail at an easy-looking one right next to it. You can't guess its skill from how hard a task feels to a human, so you learn it by testing.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Centaurs and Cyborgs on the Jagged Frontier","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/centaurs-and-cyborgs-on-the-jagged","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Practical ways to reduce hallucinations","desc":"Give the model the source documents, ask it to quote them, let it say \"I don't know,\" turn on web search for current facts, and verify anything you will act on.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Reduce hallucinations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Sycophancy","desc":"Models tend to agree with you, praise your idea, or change a correct answer when you push back. Ask neutral questions and invite criticism to get honest output.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13548","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true}]},{"title":"Knowledge cutoffs, long context, and counting","desc":"Models know little after their training cutoff unless they search. They can miss details buried in the middle of long inputs. Exact counting and arithmetic are unreliable without a tool like code execution.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Reasoning failures and odd blind spots","desc":"Reasoning models are much better at multi-step problems but still make logic slips. Some gaps are strange. For example, a model trained on \"A is B\" may not know \"B is A.\"","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"The Reversal Curse","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.12288","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"math-intuition","title":"Math Intuition for AI","tier":0,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":[],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"a"},"summary":"Math intuition for AI means understanding the few big ideas that make machine learning work (vectors, functions, slopes, probability, and averages) without needing to do the calculations by hand.","why":"Almost every AI concept, from embeddings to training loss to temperature, is a math idea in disguise. A little intuition turns jargon into something you can reason about.","depth":"How much math you need depends on your role. AI engineers building apps need only this intuition level: what a vector and similarity are, what training minimizes, and what a probability distribution is. PMs need even less. ML engineers, data scientists, and researchers should treat this as a warm-up before the deeper math nodes.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can explain an embedding as a list of numbers where similar things end up close together.","You can explain training as \"adjusting numbers to make the error smaller, one small step at a time.\"","You can explain an LLM's output as a probability distribution over next tokens, and what temperature does to it.","You can read a simple formula with a sum or a dot product and say what it computes."],"project":"Get embeddings for 20 short sentences from any embedding API (or a spreadsheet of made-up 3-number vectors). Compute cosine similarity between every pair and confirm that sentences with similar meaning score higher. Then, in the TensorFlow Playground, train a tiny network and write three sentences explaining what the loss curve and learning rate are doing.","resources":[{"title":"Neural networks series","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDNU6R1_67000Dx_ZCJB-3pi","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"A Neural Network Playground","url":"https://playground.tensorflow.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Seeing Theory","url":"https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/","type":"interactive","source":"Brown University","free":true},{"title":"Mathematics for Machine Learning and Data Science","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/specializations/mathematics-for-machine-learning-and-data-science","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":false},{"title":"BetterExplained","url":"https://betterexplained.com/","type":"article","source":"Kalid Azad","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Vectors and similarity","desc":"A vector is a list of numbers that can represent a word, image, or user. Cosine similarity measures how closely two vectors point in the same direction, which is how semantic search works.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Essence of linear algebra (chapter 1)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Functions and models","desc":"A model is a function that turns inputs into outputs, controlled by adjustable numbers called parameters. An LLM is the same idea with billions of parameters.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Slopes, loss, and gradient descent","desc":"Loss measures how wrong a model is. The gradient tells you which way to nudge each parameter to reduce it. Training repeats that nudge millions of times.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Neural networks series","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDNU6R1_67000Dx_ZCJB-3pi","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"A Neural Network Playground","url":"https://playground.tensorflow.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Probability and sampling","desc":"Models output probabilities, not certainties. Sampling picks from those probabilities, and temperature makes the choice more or less random.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Seeing Theory","url":"https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/","type":"interactive","source":"Brown University","free":true}]},{"title":"Averages, spread, and noise","desc":"Means, variance, and sample size explain why one test run proves little and why eval results need enough examples to be trusted.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Reading math notation","desc":"Learn to read Σ (sum), subscripts, vectors in bold, and function notation. That small step lets you follow docs and blog posts that use formulas.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"python-basics","title":"Python Basics","tier":0,"lane":"code","prereqs":[],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"a","nc":"a"},"summary":"Python is the main programming language of AI. This node covers the core language: variables, types, control flow, functions, data structures, files, and running code in a proper environment.","why":"Nearly every AI library, SDK, notebook, and tutorial assumes Python. Even if an AI assistant writes most of your code, you need to read it, run it, and fix it when it breaks.","depth":"Master the basics until you can write a 100-line script without looking things up: lists, dicts, loops, functions, imports, reading files, and handling errors. Classes, type hints, and packaging only need a working understanding at this stage. Decorators, generators, and async are things to recognize, not master yet.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can write and run a Python script that reads a file, processes it, and writes results.","You can choose the right built-in data structure (list, dict, set, tuple) for a job.","You can create a virtual environment and install packages without breaking your system Python.","You can read a traceback and find the line and cause of an error.","You can read code an AI assistant wrote and explain what each part does."],"project":"Write a command-line script that reads a folder of text files, counts word frequencies, ignores a list of stopwords, and saves the top 20 words per file to a JSON file. Run it inside a virtual environment.","resources":[{"title":"CS50's Introduction to Programming with Python","url":"https://cs50.harvard.edu/python/","type":"course","source":"Harvard","free":true},{"title":"The Python Tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true},{"title":"Automate the Boring Stuff with Python","url":"https://automatetheboringstuff.com/","type":"book","source":"Al Sweigart","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Python","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/python","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Python Tutor (visualize code execution)","url":"https://pythontutor.com/","type":"interactive","source":"Python Tutor","free":true},{"title":"Learn Python - Full Course for Beginners","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rfscVS0vtbw","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp","free":true},{"title":"Exercism Python track","url":"https://exercism.org/tracks/python","type":"interactive","source":"Exercism","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Values, types, and control flow","desc":"Numbers, strings, booleans, None, if/elif/else, for and while loops. This is the grammar every other topic builds on.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Python Tutorial: More Control Flow Tools","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Built-in data structures","desc":"Lists, dicts, sets, and tuples, plus comprehensions. Dicts in particular show up everywhere in AI work because JSON maps directly onto them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Python Tutorial: Data Structures","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Functions, modules, and imports","desc":"Writing functions with arguments and return values, splitting code into files, and importing from libraries. This is how you keep scripts readable as they grow.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Files, errors, and exceptions","desc":"Reading and writing text, CSV, and JSON files, and using try/except so a script fails clearly instead of mysteriously.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Python Tutorial: Errors and Exceptions","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Environments and packages (venv, pip, uv)","desc":"A virtual environment is an isolated folder of packages for one project. Learn to create one, install dependencies, and pin them in a requirements or pyproject file. uv is the fast modern tool most new projects use.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Virtual Environments and Packages","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true},{"title":"uv documentation","url":"https://docs.astral.sh/uv/","type":"tool","source":"Astral","free":true}]},{"title":"Classes and type hints","desc":"Classes bundle data with behavior. Type hints (like def f(x: int) -> str) document what a function expects and power tools like Pydantic, which LLM SDKs use heavily.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The Python Tutorial: Classes","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/classes.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true},{"title":"typing: Support for type hints","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Generators, decorators, and async","desc":"Generators produce values lazily, decorators wrap functions, and async lets one program wait on many network calls at once. You will see all three in LLM SDKs and agent code. Recognize them now, learn them properly when you need them.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"prompt-engineering","title":"Prompt Engineering","tier":1,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["prompting-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","ds":"r","rs":"a","pm":"c","biz":"r","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"Prompt engineering is the more systematic version of prompting: structuring instructions, using examples and delimiters, asking for step-by-step reasoning, controlling output format, and testing prompts so they work reliably, not just once.","why":"Once a prompt is reused by a team, a custom assistant, or an automation, 'it worked once' isn't enough. Structured, tested prompts give consistent results and are the foundation for assistants, agents, and LLM apps.","depth":"Master clear structure, examples, output formats, and testing against a handful of real cases. Know that tricks like 'you are a world-class expert' or bribes matter much less with current models; reasoning models also need less step-by-step coaching than older ones.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can write a reusable prompt template with sections for role, context, instructions, examples, and output format.","You can get reliable structured output (tables, JSON, fixed headings) from a model.","You can break a complex task into a chain of smaller prompts.","You can test a prompt against several real inputs and fix the failures you find.","You can explain how prompting differs for reasoning models."],"project":"Build a reusable prompt for a recurring job, such as turning messy meeting notes into a standard action-item summary. Collect 10 real inputs, run the prompt on all of them, log every failure, and revise until at least 9 of 10 come out right. Save the final prompt with a short note on what you changed and why.","resources":[{"title":"Claude prompting best practices","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Interactive prompt engineering tutorial","url":"https://github.com/anthropics/prompt-eng-interactive-tutorial","type":"interactive","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Prompt engineering guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-engineering","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Prompt design strategies","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/prompting-strategies","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"AI prompt engineering: A deep dive","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T9aRN5JkmL8","type":"video","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Prompt Engineering whitepaper","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/whitepaper-prompt-engineering","type":"paper","source":"Google / Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Prompt Engineering Guide","url":"https://www.promptingguide.ai/","type":"article","source":"DAIR.AI","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Structure: roles, sections, and delimiters","desc":"Separate instructions from data with clear headings or XML-style tags like <document>. This stops the model from confusing your material with your instructions.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Structure prompts with XML tags","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices#structure-prompts-with-xml-tags","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Few-shot examples","desc":"Include a few input/output pairs that show exactly what you want. Vary them so the model learns the pattern rather than copying one example.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Use examples effectively","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices#use-examples-effectively","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Reasoning and thinking","desc":"Asking the model to think before answering improves hard tasks. Reasoning models do this automatically, so with them you focus on the goal and constraints rather than spelling out every step.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Reasoning models guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Output formatting","desc":"Specify the exact shape you want: a table, a JSON object, a fixed set of headings. Consistent formats make results easy to paste into documents, spreadsheets, or automations.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Prompt chaining","desc":"Split big jobs into steps (extract, then analyze, then write) where each prompt's output feeds the next. Each step is easier to check and fix.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Testing prompts","desc":"Collect 5 to 20 real inputs, including tricky ones, and run your prompt on all of them whenever you change it. This is the small-scale version of the evals that AI engineers use.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Define success criteria and build evaluations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"From prompts to context engineering","desc":"In agents and apps, the prompt is only part of what the model sees. Context engineering is deciding which documents, tool results, and history go into the model's limited context window.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-productivity","title":"AI in Daily Workflows","tier":1,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["prompting-basics","ai-landscape"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"a","ds":"r","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"This is about weaving AI into the work you already do: email, documents, research, meetings, spreadsheets, planning, and learning. The goal is steady time savings and better quality, not occasional novelty.","why":"Most of AI's value for individuals and small teams comes from many small daily uses that add up. People who build these habits get hours back each week; people who don't often conclude AI 'isn't that useful'.","depth":"Master a handful of repeatable workflows in the tools you already use, plus the habit of checking outputs. You don't need to adopt every new app; you do need to know which tasks to hand off, which to do together with AI, and which to keep for yourself.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can map your weekly tasks and pick the ones where AI saves the most time.","You can use AI for writing, summarizing, research, and data analysis with a reliable checking step.","You can use built-in AI features in your email, docs, and spreadsheet tools.","You can use deep research and file analysis features to get sourced answers.","You can measure how much time a new AI habit actually saves."],"project":"Track your work for one week and list every recurring task. Pick the three best AI candidates, design a workflow for each (prompt, tool, and how you'll check the output), and use them for two weeks. Record minutes saved per task and decide which to keep.","resources":[{"title":"How I use LLMs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EWvNQjAaOHw","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"15 Times to use AI, and 5 Not to","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/15-times-to-use-ai-and-5-not-to","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Academy","url":"https://academy.openai.com/","type":"course","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Anthropic Courses (AI Fluency and more)","url":"https://anthropic.skilljar.com/","type":"course","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Google Workspace with Gemini Prompt Guide","url":"https://workspace.google.com/learning/content/gemini-prompt-guide","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Co-Intelligence: Living and Working with AI","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/741805/co-intelligence-by-ethan-mollick/","type":"book","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Auditing your work for AI fit","desc":"List your recurring tasks and rate each one: how often, how long, how risky if wrong. Frequent, time-consuming, low-risk tasks with easy checking are the best starting points.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"15 Times to use AI, and 5 Not to","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/15-times-to-use-ai-and-5-not-to","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Writing and editing","desc":"Use AI for first drafts, rewrites for different audiences, tightening long text, and getting critical feedback. Keep your own judgment and voice in the final version.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Research and deep research","desc":"Search-connected assistants and deep research modes read many web pages and return a sourced report. Always open the key sources yourself before relying on a claim.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Working with your own files and data","desc":"Upload PDFs, spreadsheets, and slides to get summaries, comparisons, charts, and analysis. Tools like Gemini Notebook (formerly NotebookLM) and Claude or ChatGPT Projects keep a set of sources handy.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"AI inside the tools you already use","desc":"Microsoft 365 Copilot, Gemini in Google Workspace, and assistants connected to your email, calendar, and drives can act on your real work. Connectors (often built on MCP) let one assistant reach many apps.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Microsoft 365 Copilot","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot","type":"tool","source":"Microsoft","free":false}]},{"title":"Verification habits","desc":"Decide in advance how you'll check each type of output: open the cited source, re-run the numbers, or ask a second model. The check should take much less time than doing the task yourself.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Handing off to agents","desc":"Agent features can now browse, fill forms, and run multi-step tasks for you. Start with low-stakes tasks and review what they did before trusting them with more.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"multimodal-tools","title":"Image, Audio & Video Generation","tier":1,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["ai-landscape"],"roles":{"aie":"a","pm":"a","biz":"a","nc":"r","pu":"r"},"summary":"Multimodal tools create or understand images, audio, and video, not just text. They cover image generation and editing, voice and speech, music, and short video clips.","why":"Visuals, voice, and video used to need specialists and budgets. Now a small business or individual can make product images, voiceovers, explainer clips, and transcripts in minutes, if they understand the quality limits and the legal risks.","depth":"Get hands-on with at least one image, one voice, and one video tool, and learn the rules on rights and disclosure. You don't need to understand diffusion models; you do need to know about likeness, copyright, and labeling AI-made media.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can generate and edit images with text prompts and reference images to match a specific look.","You can produce a voiceover or transcription and fix common errors.","You can create a short AI video clip and know its current length and consistency limits.","You can explain the copyright, likeness, and disclosure rules that apply to AI-generated media."],"project":"Make a 30-second promo for a real or imaginary product: three AI-generated images or clips, an AI voiceover reading a script you wrote with AI help, and simple editing to combine them. Add a note listing every tool used, its commercial-use terms, and how you labeled the result as AI-made.","resources":[{"title":"Image generation guide","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Image generation guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Runway Academy","url":"https://academy.runwayml.com/","type":"course","source":"Runway","free":true},{"title":"Veo","url":"https://deepmind.google/models/veo/","type":"tool","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true},{"title":"ElevenLabs documentation","url":"https://elevenlabs.io/docs/overview/intro","type":"docs","source":"ElevenLabs","free":true},{"title":"Copyright and Artificial Intelligence","url":"https://www.copyright.gov/ai/","type":"docs","source":"U.S. Copyright Office","free":true},{"title":"Content Authenticity Initiative","url":"https://contentauthenticity.org/","type":"article","source":"Content Authenticity Initiative","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Image generation and editing","desc":"Tools built into ChatGPT, Gemini, Midjourney, Adobe Firefly, and Canva can create images from text or edit existing ones by instruction. Describe subject, style, composition, and lighting, and use reference images for consistency.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Image generation guide","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/image-generation","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Voice, speech, and transcription","desc":"Text-to-speech can produce natural voiceovers in many languages; speech-to-text turns meetings and calls into searchable transcripts. Voice cloning exists but requires the speaker's clear consent.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"ElevenLabs documentation","url":"https://elevenlabs.io/docs/overview/intro","type":"docs","source":"ElevenLabs","free":true}]},{"title":"Video generation","desc":"Models like Veo, Sora, and Runway create short clips from text or images. They are great for b-roll, concepts, and social content, but long scenes with consistent characters still take work.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Runway Academy","url":"https://academy.runwayml.com/","type":"course","source":"Runway","free":true}]},{"title":"Understanding images and documents","desc":"Assistants can read screenshots, charts, receipts, handwriting, and photos. This 'vision' input is often more useful day to day than generating new images.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Copyright, likeness, and commercial use","desc":"Purely AI-generated work may not be protected by copyright, tool terms differ on commercial rights, and using someone's face or voice without permission is risky or illegal. Check before you publish.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Copyright and Artificial Intelligence","url":"https://www.copyright.gov/ai/","type":"docs","source":"U.S. Copyright Office","free":true}]},{"title":"Provenance and labeling","desc":"Content Credentials (the C2PA standard) and watermarks like SynthID mark media as AI-made. Some platforms and laws now require you to label synthetic media.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"C2PA: Verifying Media Content Sources","url":"https://c2pa.org/","type":"docs","source":"C2PA","free":true}]},{"title":"Music generation","desc":"Tools like Suno generate full songs from a prompt. Useful for background tracks and drafts; check licensing terms before commercial use.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ai-use-cases","title":"Finding AI Use Cases","tier":1,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-for-business-101"],"roles":{"aie":"a","ds":"a","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"r","pu":"a"},"summary":"Finding AI use cases means systematically spotting the tasks in your business where AI would save time, cut cost, or improve quality, and ranking them so you start with the ones most likely to pay off.","why":"Most failed AI projects picked the wrong problem: too vague, too risky, or not valuable enough. A simple discovery and scoring process gets you to quick wins and builds the confidence to tackle bigger ones.","depth":"Master a repeatable method: interview people, map tasks, score by value and feasibility, and run a small test. You don't need to estimate ROI to the dollar at this stage; a rough high/medium/low is enough to rank ideas.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can run a discovery session with a team to surface AI opportunities.","You can break a process into tasks and spot the ones that suit AI.","You can score and rank use cases by value, feasibility, and risk.","You can design a small, time-boxed test to validate a top use case."],"project":"Interview three people in your business (or three roles you play yourself) about their weekly tasks. Build a list of at least 10 AI use cases, score each on value, feasibility, and risk in a spreadsheet, pick the top one, and write a one-page plan for a two-week test with a clear success measure.","resources":[{"title":"Identifying and Scaling AI Use Cases","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/identifying-and-scaling-ai-use-cases.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Real-world gen AI use cases from leading organizations","url":"https://cloud.google.com/transform/101-real-world-generative-ai-use-cases-from-industry-leaders","type":"article","source":"Google Cloud","free":true},{"title":"Making AI Work: Leadership, Lab, and Crowd","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/making-ai-work-leadership-lab-and","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"Economic Index: Uneven AI adoption","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/anthropic-economic-index-september-2025-report","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Generative AI for Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/generative-ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true},{"title":"How to Capitalize on Generative AI","url":"https://hbr.org/2023/11/how-to-capitalize-on-generative-ai","type":"article","source":"Harvard Business Review","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Discovery: ask the people doing the work","desc":"Frontline staff know which tasks are repetitive, slow, or frustrating. Ask what eats their time, what they copy and paste, and what they'd hand to an assistant.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Identifying and Scaling AI Use Cases","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/identifying-and-scaling-ai-use-cases.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Common use-case patterns","desc":"Most business uses fall into a few patterns: content creation, research and summarization, data extraction from documents, answering questions over your knowledge, coding, and analysis.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Process mapping","desc":"Draw the steps of a process and note inputs, outputs, time, and who checks the work. AI fits best on steps with clear inputs, repeatable patterns, and easy checking.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Scoring: value, feasibility, risk","desc":"Rate each idea on business value (hours saved, revenue impact), feasibility (data available, tool exists, simple integration), and risk (cost of a mistake). Plot them to find quick wins.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Small tests before big projects","desc":"Run a two to four week test with a few users, a clear success measure, and a before/after comparison. Kill or scale based on results, not enthusiasm.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Learning from what people already do","desc":"Many employees already use AI quietly. Surveying and sharing these grassroots uses is often the fastest source of proven use cases.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Making AI Work: Leadership, Lab, and Crowd","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/making-ai-work-leadership-lab-and","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-privacy","title":"Data Privacy & Safe Use","tier":1,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["ai-limitations"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","ds":"r","rs":"a","pm":"c","biz":"c","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"Safe use means knowing where your data goes when you type it into an AI tool, who can see it, and whether it can be used to train future models.","why":"Pasting a customer list, contract, or health record into the wrong tool can break the law or your company's rules. If you build AI products, you also handle other people's data and need to protect it.","depth":"Everyone must master the basics: consumer vs business plans, training opt-outs, what never to paste, and your company's AI policy. Builders should also understand PII redaction, data retention, and GDPR basics. Training-data extraction and differential privacy are 'know it exists' unless you train models.","hours":6,"outcomes":["You can check whether a given AI tool and plan uses your data for training, and how to opt out.","You can sort data into \"fine to paste,\" \"only in approved tools,\" and \"never\" categories.","You can explain when to choose an enterprise plan, a zero-retention API, or a local model.","You can strip or mask personal data (PII) before it reaches a model in an app you build."],"project":"Audit the AI tools you or your team use. For each one, record the plan, whether data is used for training, retention period, and where the opt-out lives. Turn it into a one-page \"what you can paste where\" guide. Builders: add a Presidio redaction step in front of an LLM call and show that names and emails never reach the model.","resources":[{"title":"Is my data used for model training?","url":"https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/10023580-is-my-data-used-for-model-training","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Data Controls FAQ","url":"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7730893-data-controls-faq","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Gemini Apps Privacy Hub","url":"https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/13594961","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"LLM02:2025 Sensitive Information Disclosure","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm022025-sensitive-information-disclosure/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true},{"title":"Guidance on AI and data protection","url":"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/","type":"docs","source":"UK ICO","free":true},{"title":"Presidio","url":"https://github.com/data-privacy-stack/presidio","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Consumer vs business plans","desc":"Free and consumer plans often have different training and retention terms from team, enterprise, and API plans. Read the data-use page for each tool you use and check the settings.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Is my data used for model training?","url":"https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/10023580-is-my-data-used-for-model-training","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Data Controls FAQ","url":"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7730893-data-controls-faq","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"What not to paste","desc":"Passwords, API keys, customer personal data, health or financial records, and confidential business documents stay out of unapproved tools. When in doubt, anonymize or summarize first.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Sensitive data in AI apps","desc":"Apps can leak data through prompts, logs, retrieved documents, and model outputs. Limit what goes into context, redact PII, and control who can see conversation logs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LLM02:2025 Sensitive Information Disclosure","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm022025-sensitive-information-disclosure/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true},{"title":"Presidio (PII detection and anonymization)","url":"https://github.com/data-privacy-stack/presidio","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Data protection law basics","desc":"Laws like GDPR require a lawful basis for processing personal data, data minimization, and rights such as access and deletion. These apply to data you send to AI vendors too.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Guidance on AI and data protection","url":"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/","type":"docs","source":"UK ICO","free":true}]},{"title":"Local and private deployment","desc":"Running an open model on your own machine or servers keeps data in-house. It trades some quality and convenience for control.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Ollama","url":"https://ollama.com/","type":"tool","source":"Ollama","free":true}]},{"title":"Memorization and training-data extraction","desc":"Models can memorize and repeat rare text from their training data, including personal details. This is why training on private data needs care.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Extracting Training Data from Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07805","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-ethics","title":"AI Ethics & Bias","tier":1,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["ai-limitations"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"r","ds":"r","rs":"r","pm":"c","biz":"r","nc":"a","pu":"a"},"summary":"AI ethics asks whether an AI system is fair, honest, accountable, and good for the people it affects. Bias is when a system works worse for, or treats unfairly, certain groups of people.","why":"AI now helps decide who gets hired, approved for loans, or flagged for fraud. Biased or careless systems cause real harm, lose user trust, and create legal risk.","depth":"PMs and leaders must master the main harm types and how to ask the right questions before launch. Data scientists and ML engineers should also know how to measure group fairness metrics and document a model. The formal math of fairness trade-offs is for those who build high-stakes models.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can name the main sources of bias: skewed data, proxy features, labels that encode past decisions, and feedback loops.","You can describe at least three real cases where AI caused harm and what went wrong.","You can compare outcomes across groups and explain why different fairness definitions can conflict.","You can write a short model card or impact note for an AI feature."],"project":"Take a public tabular dataset with a demographic column (for example, a credit or income dataset). Train a simple classifier, then use Fairlearn to compare accuracy and false-positive rates across groups. Write a one-page model card that states the gaps you found and where the model should not be used. Non-coders: write an impact note for an AI feature at your work covering who could be harmed and how you would check.","resources":[{"title":"Practical Data Ethics","url":"https://ethics.fast.ai/","type":"course","source":"fast.ai","free":true},{"title":"Fairness (Machine Learning Crash Course)","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/fairness","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Intro to AI Ethics","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-ai-ethics","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle Learn","free":true},{"title":"Fairness and Machine Learning","url":"https://fairmlbook.org/","type":"book","source":"Barocas, Hardt, Narayanan","free":true},{"title":"AI Safety, Ethics, and Society","url":"https://www.aisafetybook.com/","type":"book","source":"Center for AI Safety","free":true},{"title":"Machine Bias","url":"https://www.propublica.org/article/machine-bias-risk-assessments-in-criminal-sentencing","type":"article","source":"ProPublica","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Where bias comes from","desc":"Bias enters through who is in the data, how labels were made, which features act as proxies for protected traits, and how the system is used after launch.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Fairness (Machine Learning Crash Course)","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/fairness","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Case studies of real harm","desc":"Learning from documented failures, like risk scores in criminal justice and face recognition that misses darker-skinned women, is the fastest way to build judgment.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Machine Bias","url":"https://www.propublica.org/article/machine-bias-risk-assessments-in-criminal-sentencing","type":"article","source":"ProPublica","free":true},{"title":"Gender Shades","url":"http://gendershades.org/","type":"article","source":"MIT Media Lab","free":true}]},{"title":"Measuring fairness","desc":"Compare error rates and outcomes across groups. Definitions like demographic parity and equalized odds often can't all hold at once, so you must choose based on the context.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Fairlearn","url":"https://fairlearn.org/","type":"tool","source":"Fairlearn","free":true},{"title":"Fairness and Machine Learning","url":"https://fairmlbook.org/","type":"book","source":"Barocas, Hardt, Narayanan","free":true}]},{"title":"Transparency and documentation","desc":"Model cards and datasheets record what a model or dataset is for, how it was tested, and where it should not be used.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Model Cards for Model Reporting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Datasheets for Datasets","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Generative AI harms","desc":"LLMs and image models add new issues: stereotypes in generated content, misinformation, deepfakes, copyright questions, and the labor behind training data.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Accountability and human oversight","desc":"Someone must own each AI decision, people need a way to appeal, and humans in the loop need the time and information to actually catch errors.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"linear-algebra","title":"Linear Algebra","tier":1,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":["math-intuition"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"Linear algebra is the math of vectors and matrices. Every neural network layer is a matrix multiplication, and embeddings, attention, and LoRA are all linear algebra.","why":"If you train, debug, or design models, tensor shapes and matrix operations are your daily language. It also explains why GPUs matter: they are machines for multiplying matrices fast.","depth":"AI engineers building apps need only vectors, dot products, and cosine similarity; the rest is 'know it exists.' ML engineers must master matrix multiplication, shapes, broadcasting, and the geometric view of transformations. Researchers should also be fluent with eigenvectors, SVD, low-rank structure, and norms. Almost nobody needs to compute decompositions by hand; libraries do that.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can read a matrix multiplication as a transformation and predict the output shape of any layer.","You can explain dot products, norms, and cosine similarity, and implement them in NumPy.","You can explain eigenvectors and SVD and how low-rank approximation underlies PCA and LoRA.","You can debug shape mismatches and broadcasting errors in tensor code."],"project":"In NumPy, write a 2-layer neural network forward pass by hand and print every shape. Then take a grayscale image, compute its SVD, and rebuild it from the top 5, 20, and 50 singular values. Plot the results and explain how this connects to LoRA's low-rank updates.","resources":[{"title":"Essence of linear algebra","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"Mathematics for Machine Learning (Part I, ch. 2-4)","url":"https://mml-book.github.io/","type":"book","source":"Deisenroth, Faisal, Ong","free":true},{"title":"Linear Algebra (18.06)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06-linear-algebra-spring-2010/","type":"course","source":"MIT OCW (Gilbert Strang)","free":true},{"title":"Immersive Linear Algebra","url":"http://immersivemath.com/ila/index.html","type":"interactive","source":"immersivemath","free":true},{"title":"Linear Algebra (Dive into Deep Learning 2.3)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_preliminaries/linear-algebra.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"Computational Linear Algebra","url":"https://github.com/fastai/numerical-linear-algebra","type":"course","source":"fast.ai","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Vectors, dot products, and norms","desc":"Vectors are points or directions. The dot product measures alignment, the norm measures length, and together they give cosine similarity, the core of embedding search.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Linear Algebra (Dive into Deep Learning 2.3)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_preliminaries/linear-algebra.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Matrices as transformations","desc":"A matrix stretches, rotates, or projects space. Multiplying matrices chains these transformations. A neural network layer is a matrix followed by a nonlinearity.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Essence of linear algebra","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDPD3MizzM2xVFitgF8hE_ab","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Tensors, shapes, and broadcasting","desc":"Real code works with batches: 3D and 4D arrays. Knowing how shapes combine and how broadcasting stretches dimensions prevents most bugs in model code.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"NumPy linear algebra reference","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/routines.linalg.html","type":"docs","source":"NumPy","free":true}]},{"title":"Eigenvectors and SVD","desc":"Eigenvectors are directions a matrix only stretches. SVD breaks any matrix into rotate, scale, rotate, which reveals its most important directions.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Matrix Methods in Data Analysis, Signal Processing, and Machine Learning (18.065)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-065-matrix-methods-in-data-analysis-signal-processing-and-machine-learning-spring-2018/","type":"course","source":"MIT OCW","free":true}]},{"title":"Low rank and why it matters","desc":"Many large matrices are well approximated by a product of two thin ones. This is the idea behind PCA, compression, and LoRA fine-tuning.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Proof-based linear algebra","desc":"Vector spaces, linear maps, and proofs. Useful for theory research and reading dense papers, but not needed for applied work.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Linear Algebra Done Right","url":"https://linear.axler.net/","type":"book","source":"Sheldon Axler","free":true}]}]},{"id":"calculus","title":"Calculus & Gradients","tier":1,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":["math-intuition"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"Calculus studies how things change. For AI, the key idea is the gradient: it tells you how a small change in each parameter would change the loss, which is how every model learns.","why":"Backpropagation, learning rates, vanishing gradients, and exploding losses are all calculus. If you train models, you need this to understand why training works or fails.","depth":"AI engineers building apps only need the intuition: a gradient points uphill, and training steps downhill. ML engineers must master derivatives, the chain rule, partial derivatives, and how autograd applies them, since that's what backprop is. Researchers should be comfortable with matrix calculus and Jacobians. You don't need integration techniques or to derive backprop by hand for every layer; frameworks do that.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can explain a derivative as a rate of change and a gradient as the direction of steepest increase.","You can apply the chain rule to a small computation graph by hand.","You can explain how backpropagation and autograd compute gradients for a network.","You can diagnose vanishing and exploding gradients and say why they happen."],"project":"Follow Karpathy's micrograd video and build your own tiny autograd engine. Use it to train a small neural network on a toy dataset. Then check your gradients against PyTorch's autograd on the same network and confirm they match to several decimal places.","resources":[{"title":"The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMj-3S1tku0","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Essence of calculus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDMsr9K-rj53DwVRMYO3t5Yr","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"The Matrix Calculus You Need For Deep Learning","url":"https://explained.ai/matrix-calculus/","type":"article","source":"Terence Parr & Jeremy Howard","free":true},{"title":"CS231n: Backpropagation, Intuitions","url":"https://cs231n.github.io/optimization-2/","type":"article","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"Mathematics for Machine Learning (ch. 5)","url":"https://mml-book.github.io/","type":"book","source":"Deisenroth, Faisal, Ong","free":true},{"title":"Automatic Differentiation (Dive into Deep Learning 2.5)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_preliminaries/autograd.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Derivatives as rates of change","desc":"A derivative tells you how much the output moves when you nudge the input. That one idea drives all of gradient-based learning.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Essence of calculus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDMsr9K-rj53DwVRMYO3t5Yr","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"The chain rule","desc":"When functions are stacked, their derivatives multiply. A neural network is a long stack of functions, so the chain rule is how gradients flow backward through it.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Backpropagation calculus","url":"https://www.3blue1brown.com/lessons/backpropagation-calculus","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Partial derivatives and gradients","desc":"With many inputs, a partial derivative measures change along one input. The gradient collects them all into a vector that points uphill on the loss surface.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Multivariable calculus","url":"https://www.khanacademy.org/math/multivariable-calculus","type":"course","source":"Khan Academy","free":true}]},{"title":"Backpropagation and autograd","desc":"Backprop applies the chain rule efficiently from the output back to every parameter. PyTorch's autograd does this automatically by recording a computation graph.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMj-3S1tku0","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"A Gentle Introduction to torch.autograd","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/blitz/autograd_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Matrix calculus and Jacobians","desc":"Gradients of vector and matrix functions, written compactly. Needed to derive gradients for new layers or to follow derivations in papers.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The Matrix Calculus You Need For Deep Learning","url":"https://explained.ai/matrix-calculus/","type":"article","source":"Terence Parr & Jeremy Howard","free":true}]},{"title":"Integrals and the rest of calculus","desc":"Integrals show up in probability (areas under density curves) and in diffusion model theory. Applied engineers can mostly skip them.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Single Variable Calculus (18.01SC)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-01sc-single-variable-calculus-fall-2010/","type":"course","source":"MIT OCW","free":true}]}]},{"id":"probability-stats","title":"Probability & Statistics","tier":1,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":["math-intuition"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"a"},"summary":"Probability describes uncertainty, and statistics turns data into conclusions you can trust. Together they explain how models make predictions and how you know whether a result is real.","why":"LLMs literally output probability distributions, and every eval, A/B test, and metric is a statistical claim. Without this, it's easy to ship a change that only looked better because of noise.","depth":"AI engineers must master the practical parts: distributions, sampling, expected value, confidence intervals, and enough to tell if an eval improvement is real. Data scientists must master hypothesis testing, regression, experiment design, and Bayesian thinking. ML engineers and researchers also need likelihood, maximum likelihood estimation, and conditional probability fluency. Measure theory is 'know it exists' for nearly everyone.","hours":35,"outcomes":["You can explain random variables, common distributions, expectation, and variance.","You can apply Bayes' theorem and conditional probability to a real problem.","You can put a confidence interval on an eval score and decide whether two model versions really differ.","You can explain maximum likelihood and why cross-entropy loss is used to train classifiers and LLMs.","You can design a simple A/B test with an adequate sample size."],"project":"Run the same 100-question eval on two prompt versions (or two models) at least three times each. Compute the mean score, standard error, and 95% confidence interval for each, plus a paired test on the difference. Write a short verdict on whether the \"better\" version is actually better, and how many questions you'd need to detect a 2-point difference.","resources":[{"title":"StatQuest","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@statquest","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"Seeing Theory","url":"https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/","type":"interactive","source":"Brown University","free":true},{"title":"Statistics 110: Probability","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL2SOU6wwxB0uwwH80KTQ6ht66KWxbzTIo","type":"course","source":"Harvard (Joe Blitzstein)","free":true},{"title":"OpenIntro Statistics","url":"https://www.openintro.org/book/os/","type":"book","source":"OpenIntro","free":true},{"title":"Think Stats","url":"https://greenteapress.com/wp/think-stats-3e/","type":"book","source":"Allen Downey","free":true},{"title":"Adding Error Bars to Evals","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00640","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true},{"title":"Statistics and probability","url":"https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability","type":"course","source":"Khan Academy","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Probability basics and distributions","desc":"Random variables, Bernoulli, binomial, normal, and categorical distributions. An LLM's next-token output is a categorical distribution over its vocabulary.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Seeing Theory","url":"https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/","type":"interactive","source":"Brown University","free":true}]},{"title":"Conditional probability and Bayes","desc":"How beliefs should update with evidence. It underpins classifiers, explains base-rate errors, and is the core of Bayesian methods.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Bayes' theorem","url":"https://www.3blue1brown.com/lessons/bayes-theorem","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Descriptive stats and sampling","desc":"Mean, median, variance, and the central limit theorem. Why averages of many samples behave predictably, and how sample size controls noise.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"StatQuest","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@statquest","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}]},{"title":"Uncertainty in evals and experiments","desc":"Confidence intervals, standard errors, and significance tests tell you whether a score change is real. This applies directly to LLM evals and product A/B tests.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A statistical approach to model evaluations","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/statistical-approach-to-model-evals","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Evan's Awesome A/B Tools","url":"https://www.evanmiller.org/ab-testing/","type":"tool","source":"Evan Miller","free":true}]},{"title":"Likelihood and estimation","desc":"Maximum likelihood picks parameters that make the observed data most probable. Minimizing cross-entropy loss is exactly this, which is how LLMs are pretrained.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Probability and Statistics (Dive into Deep Learning 2.6)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_preliminaries/probability.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Bayesian methods","desc":"Treat parameters as uncertain and update a full distribution over them. Useful for small data, uncertainty estimates, and decision-making.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Think Bayes 2","url":"https://allendowney.github.io/ThinkBayes2/","type":"book","source":"Allen Downey","free":true}]}]},{"id":"python-data","title":"Python for Data (NumPy, pandas)","tier":1,"lane":"code","prereqs":["python-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c"},"summary":"NumPy and pandas are the Python libraries for working with numbers and tables. NumPy gives you fast arrays; pandas gives you DataFrames, which are spreadsheets you control with code.","why":"Every ML workflow starts with loading, cleaning, and reshaping data, and model inputs are NumPy arrays or tensors that behave the same way. If you can't move data around fluently, you spend your time fighting the tools instead of learning from the data.","depth":"Master pandas loading, filtering, groupby, merge, and handling missing values, plus NumPy array shapes, indexing, and broadcasting. These come up daily. Polars, DuckDB, and memory tuning are worth knowing about for large data but are not required to start.","hours":35,"outcomes":["You can load a CSV or Parquet file, inspect it, and clean obvious problems like missing values and wrong types.","You can answer questions with groupby, aggregation, and joins instead of loops.","You can predict the shape of a NumPy operation, including broadcasting, before running it.","You can work in a Jupyter notebook and keep it tidy enough for someone else to rerun."],"project":"Take a public dataset with at least 50,000 rows (for example a city's open data on 311 calls or bike trips). Clean it, then answer five concrete questions using only vectorized pandas (no Python loops), such as busiest hour by neighborhood or month-over-month change.","resources":[{"title":"Python for Data Analysis, 3rd Edition (open access)","url":"https://wesmckinney.com/book/","type":"book","source":"Wes McKinney","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Pandas","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/pandas","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Python Data Science Handbook","url":"https://jakevdp.github.io/PythonDataScienceHandbook/","type":"book","source":"Jake VanderPlas","free":true},{"title":"pandas getting started tutorials","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/intro_tutorials/index.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true},{"title":"NumPy: Learn","url":"https://numpy.org/learn/","type":"docs","source":"NumPy","free":true},{"title":"Project Jupyter","url":"https://jupyter.org/","type":"tool","source":"Jupyter","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"NumPy arrays, shapes, and indexing","desc":"An ndarray is a grid of numbers with a shape like (rows, columns). Slicing, boolean masks, and reshaping are the moves you will use constantly, and they carry over directly to PyTorch tensors.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"NumPy: the absolute basics for beginners","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/absolute_beginners.html","type":"docs","source":"NumPy","free":true},{"title":"Indexing on ndarrays","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.indexing.html","type":"docs","source":"NumPy","free":true}]},{"title":"Vectorization and broadcasting","desc":"Vectorization means operating on whole arrays at once instead of looping, which is often 100x faster. Broadcasting is the rule for combining arrays of different shapes. Shape bugs are the most common bug in ML code.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Broadcasting","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.broadcasting.html","type":"docs","source":"NumPy","free":true}]},{"title":"pandas DataFrames: load, select, filter","desc":"Reading CSV, JSON, and Parquet, selecting columns, filtering rows with conditions, and using .loc and .iloc correctly.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"10 minutes to pandas","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true}]},{"title":"Cleaning: missing values, types, duplicates","desc":"Real data has gaps, wrong types, and duplicate rows. Learn to find them, decide what to do about them, and document the choice.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Working with missing data","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/missing_data.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true}]},{"title":"Groupby, merge, and reshape","desc":"Split-apply-combine with groupby, SQL-style joins with merge, and pivot/melt to switch between wide and long tables. This is where pandas starts to save you hours.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Group by: split-apply-combine","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/groupby.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true},{"title":"Merge, join, concatenate and compare","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/merging.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true}]},{"title":"Dates and time series","desc":"Parsing dates, resampling to daily or monthly, and rolling windows. Needed for any data with a time dimension, such as sales, logs, or sensor readings.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Time series / date functionality","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/timeseries.html","type":"docs","source":"pandas","free":true}]},{"title":"Bigger data: Polars and DuckDB","desc":"When pandas gets slow or runs out of memory, Polars (a fast DataFrame library) and DuckDB (SQL on local files) handle millions of rows on a laptop. Good to know they exist.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Polars user guide","url":"https://docs.pola.rs/","type":"docs","source":"Polars","free":true},{"title":"DuckDB documentation","url":"https://duckdb.org/docs/stable/","type":"docs","source":"DuckDB","free":true}]}]},{"id":"git-cli","title":"Git, CLI & Dev Environment","tier":1,"lane":"code","prereqs":["python-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"r"},"summary":"The command line, Git, and a good editor are the everyday environment of anyone who writes code. Git tracks every change to your code so you can undo mistakes and collaborate.","why":"You can't deploy, collaborate, run a remote GPU box, or safely let an AI agent edit your code without these. Git is also your safety net when an AI coding tool makes a large change you want to roll back.","depth":"Master the daily loop: navigate the terminal, edit files, git add/commit/push/pull, branches, and pull requests. Know how to recover from a bad commit. Rebasing, SSH config, and Docker are useful to understand but you can learn them as needed.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can move around, search, and run programs from a terminal without a GUI.","You can commit work in small logical steps, push to GitHub, and open a pull request.","You can create a branch, merge it, and resolve a simple merge conflict.","You can undo a mistake with git restore, revert, or reset without losing work.","You can set up a project with an editor, a virtual environment, and a .gitignore that keeps secrets out of the repo."],"project":"Put your Python projects on GitHub. Use a feature branch for one new feature, open a pull request against your own repo, add a GitHub Actions workflow that runs Ruff on every push, and merge it once the check passes.","resources":[{"title":"The Missing Semester of Your CS Education","url":"https://missing.csail.mit.edu/","type":"course","source":"MIT","free":true},{"title":"Learn Git Branching","url":"https://learngitbranching.js.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Learn Git Branching","free":true},{"title":"Git and GitHub for Beginners - Crash Course","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGOj5yH7evk","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp","free":true},{"title":"Pro Git book","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2","type":"book","source":"git-scm.com","free":true},{"title":"Atlassian Git Tutorials","url":"https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials","type":"article","source":"Atlassian","free":true},{"title":"The Linux Command Line (free book)","url":"https://linuxcommand.org/tlcl.php","type":"book","source":"William Shotts","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Shell basics","desc":"Paths, cd, ls, pipes, redirects, environment variables, and running scripts. The terminal is where AI tools, servers, and GPUs are controlled.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Missing Semester: Course overview + the shell","url":"https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2020/course-shell/","type":"course","source":"MIT","free":true}]},{"title":"Git fundamentals","desc":"The working directory, staging area, and commits. A commit is a saved snapshot with a message; history is a chain of those snapshots.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Missing Semester: Version Control (Git)","url":"https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2020/version-control/","type":"course","source":"MIT","free":true},{"title":"Pro Git book","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2","type":"book","source":"git-scm.com","free":true}]},{"title":"Branches, merging, and pull requests","desc":"Branches let you try changes without touching main. Pull requests on GitHub are where changes get reviewed, tested, and merged.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Learn Git Branching","url":"https://learngitbranching.js.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Learn Git Branching","free":true},{"title":"GitHub Hello World","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-github/hello-world","type":"docs","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Editor and project setup","desc":"A code editor like VS Code or Cursor with Python support, a linter/formatter like Ruff, and a .gitignore and .env file for secrets. API keys should never be committed.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Getting Started with Python in VS Code","url":"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/python/python-tutorial","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"Ruff","url":"https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/","type":"tool","source":"Astral","free":true}]},{"title":"Remote machines and SSH","desc":"Training and serving often happen on a remote Linux box with a GPU. SSH lets you log in, and your editor can work on remote files as if they were local.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Remote Development using SSH","url":"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/remote/ssh","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true}]},{"title":"Automation: CI and containers","desc":"GitHub Actions runs tests on every push. Docker packages your code with its dependencies so it runs the same everywhere. Both become core when you start shipping.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"GitHub Actions documentation","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions","type":"docs","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"Docker: Get started","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/","type":"docs","source":"Docker","free":true}]}]},{"id":"apis-json","title":"APIs, HTTP & JSON","tier":1,"lane":"code","prereqs":["python-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","ds":"r","pm":"a","nc":"c"},"summary":"An API is a way for programs to talk to each other over the internet, usually by sending HTTP requests and getting back JSON. Every LLM, vector database, and SaaS tool is used through one.","why":"Calling Claude, OpenAI, or Gemini is an HTTP request with a JSON body. Tool calling, MCP, webhooks, and no-code automations are all built on the same ideas. Understanding them makes SDK errors and integration bugs easy to debug.","depth":"Master the request/response cycle: methods, URLs, headers, auth with API keys, status codes, and reading and writing JSON in Python. Learn to handle errors, timeouts, and rate limits. Building your own API with FastAPI and writing JSON Schema are the next step, not a requirement on day one.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can call a REST API from Python and from curl, with headers and an API key.","You can read an API's docs and turn them into working code.","You can explain what a 401, 404, 429, and 500 mean and how your code should react to each.","You can parse nested JSON into Python objects and validate it.","You can build a tiny API endpoint of your own that returns JSON."],"project":"Build a small FastAPI service with one endpoint that takes a city name, calls a free public weather API, validates the response with Pydantic, handles errors and timeouts cleanly, and returns a simplified JSON summary. Test it with curl.","resources":[{"title":"Python and REST APIs: Interacting With Web Services","url":"https://realpython.com/api-integration-in-python/","type":"article","source":"Real Python","free":true},{"title":"Overview of HTTP","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Overview","type":"docs","source":"MDN","free":true},{"title":"FastAPI Tutorial - User Guide","url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/","type":"docs","source":"FastAPI","free":true},{"title":"Working with JSON","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Scripting/JSON","type":"docs","source":"MDN","free":true},{"title":"curl tutorial","url":"https://curl.se/docs/tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"curl","free":true},{"title":"JSONPlaceholder (free fake API for practice)","url":"https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/","type":"tool","source":"JSONPlaceholder","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"How HTTP works","desc":"A client sends a request (method, URL, headers, optional body) and the server sends back a status code, headers, and a body. GET reads, POST creates or triggers, and so on.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Overview of HTTP","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Overview","type":"docs","source":"MDN","free":true},{"title":"HTTP response status codes","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status","type":"docs","source":"MDN","free":true}]},{"title":"JSON in and out","desc":"JSON is text made of objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null. In Python it maps to dicts and lists via the json module. LLM structured outputs and tool calls are all JSON.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Introducing JSON","url":"https://www.json.org/json-en.html","type":"docs","source":"json.org","free":true},{"title":"json: JSON encoder and decoder","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Calling APIs from Python","desc":"Using requests or httpx to send calls, pass API keys in headers, and read responses. Keep keys in environment variables, never in code.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Requests Quickstart","url":"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/quickstart/","type":"docs","source":"Requests","free":true},{"title":"HTTPX","url":"https://www.python-httpx.org/","type":"docs","source":"HTTPX","free":true}]},{"title":"Errors, retries, and rate limits","desc":"Real APIs time out, return 429 when you send too much, and fail now and then. Retry with exponential backoff, set timeouts, and log failures. This matters a lot with LLM APIs.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Validating data with Pydantic and JSON Schema","desc":"JSON Schema describes the shape JSON must have. Pydantic turns Python classes into validators and schemas. LLM SDKs use both for structured outputs and tool definitions.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Pydantic documentation","url":"https://pydantic.dev/docs/validation/latest/get-started/","type":"docs","source":"Pydantic","free":true},{"title":"JSON Schema: Creating your first schema","url":"https://json-schema.org/learn/getting-started-step-by-step","type":"docs","source":"JSON Schema","free":true}]},{"title":"Building your own API","desc":"FastAPI lets you expose a Python function as an HTTP endpoint in a few lines, with automatic docs. It is the usual way to wrap a model or an LLM workflow as a service.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"FastAPI Tutorial - User Guide","url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/","type":"docs","source":"FastAPI","free":true}]},{"title":"Webhooks, streaming, and auth flows","desc":"Webhooks are APIs calling you when something happens. Streaming (server-sent events) is how LLM responses arrive token by token. OAuth is how apps get permission to act for a user. Know what they are.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"sql","title":"SQL & Databases","tier":1,"lane":"code","prereqs":[],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"r","ds":"c","pm":"r","biz":"a","nc":"a"},"summary":"SQL is the language for asking questions of databases. Most company data lives in relational databases and warehouses, and SQL is how you get it out.","why":"Data scientists spend a large share of their time writing SQL. AI engineers need it for app data, logs, and Postgres with pgvector. PMs and analysts use it to answer their own questions instead of waiting on someone else.","depth":"Master SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY, JOINs, subqueries, CTEs, and window functions until you can answer a business question in one query. Schema design and indexes need a working understanding. Query planners, transactions, and database internals are only for people who run databases.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can write queries that filter, aggregate, and join several tables correctly.","You can use CTEs and window functions for running totals, rankings, and period-over-period changes.","You can spot when a join is silently duplicating or dropping rows.","You can design a simple schema with primary and foreign keys for a small app.","You can query a local file or database from Python."],"project":"Load a public multi-table dataset (for example an e-commerce orders, customers, and products dataset) into SQLite or DuckDB. Write queries for monthly revenue, each customer's first and latest order, the top 3 products per category by revenue, and 30-day repeat purchase rate.","resources":[{"title":"SQLBolt","url":"https://sqlbolt.com/","type":"interactive","source":"SQLBolt","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Intro to SQL","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-sql","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Advanced SQL","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/advanced-sql","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"ThoughtSpot (formerly Mode) SQL Tutorial","url":"https://www.thoughtspot.com/sql-tutorial","type":"course","source":"ThoughtSpot","free":true},{"title":"SQL Tutorial - Full Database Course for Beginners","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HXV3zeQKqGY","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp","free":true},{"title":"CS 186: Introduction to Database Systems","url":"https://cs186berkeley.net/","type":"course","source":"UC Berkeley","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Querying a single table","desc":"SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, and aggregate functions like COUNT, SUM, and AVG with GROUP BY and HAVING.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"SQLBolt interactive lessons","url":"https://sqlbolt.com/","type":"interactive","source":"SQLBolt","free":true}]},{"title":"Joins and relationships","desc":"INNER, LEFT, and FULL joins combine tables by keys. Getting joins wrong is the most common way SQL gives confidently wrong answers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Select Star SQL","url":"https://selectstarsql.com/","type":"interactive","source":"Select Star SQL","free":true}]},{"title":"CTEs and window functions","desc":"CTEs (WITH clauses) break a big query into readable steps. Window functions compute things like rank, running total, or previous value without collapsing rows. They are what separates basic from fluent SQL.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"PostgreSQL Tutorial: Window Functions","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html","type":"docs","source":"PostgreSQL","free":true}]},{"title":"Schema design and data modeling","desc":"Tables, primary keys, foreign keys, and normalization. Knowing how data is shaped tells you how to query it and where it can go wrong.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"PostgreSQL Tutorial","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PostgreSQL","free":true}]},{"title":"Indexes and performance","desc":"An index is a lookup structure that makes queries fast. Learn why some queries take milliseconds and others take minutes, and how to read an EXPLAIN plan.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Use The Index, Luke","url":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/","type":"book","source":"Markus Winand","free":true}]},{"title":"SQL for AI apps: Postgres, pgvector, DuckDB","desc":"Postgres with the pgvector extension stores embeddings next to normal data, a common choice for RAG. DuckDB runs fast SQL directly on CSV and Parquet files on your laptop.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"DuckDB documentation","url":"https://duckdb.org/docs/stable/","type":"docs","source":"DuckDB","free":true}]}]},{"id":"algorithms-ds","title":"Data Structures & Algorithms","tier":1,"lane":"code","prereqs":["python-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"r","ds":"a","rs":"r"},"summary":"Data structures are ways to organize data in memory (arrays, hash maps, trees, graphs). Algorithms are step-by-step methods to solve problems with them. Big-O notation describes how their cost grows as inputs grow.","why":"It helps you write code that stays fast when data grows from 1,000 rows to 100 million, and it is the vocabulary of technical interviews. Many AI ideas are built on these basics: vector search is nearest-neighbor search, beam search is a search algorithm, and tokenizers use tries and merges.","depth":"Implement yourself and understand deeply: arrays vs linked lists, hash maps, stacks and queues, binary search, sorting (know how merge sort works), recursion, BFS and DFS on trees and graphs, and Big-O analysis. Know how to use but not implement: heaps and priority queues, balanced trees, Dijkstra, tries, and dynamic programming patterns (practice these if you are interviewing). Just know they exist: red-black trees, B-trees, union-find, advanced graph algorithms, and approximate nearest-neighbor indexes like HNSW (covered later under vector databases).","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can state the Big-O time and memory of common operations and spot an accidental O(n^2) loop.","You can pick a list, dict, set, heap, or deque for a task and justify it.","You can implement binary search, BFS, and DFS from scratch without bugs.","You can solve typical easy and medium coding interview problems in Python.","You can explain in plain words why approximate nearest-neighbor search exists."],"project":"Build a tiny search engine over a folder of text files: an inverted index using dicts, top-k results with heapq, and a brute-force nearest-neighbor search over word-count vectors. Time each part as the corpus grows 10x and explain the results in Big-O terms.","resources":[{"title":"MIT 6.006 Introduction to Algorithms (Spring 2020)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/","type":"course","source":"MIT OCW","free":true},{"title":"Algorithms and Data Structures Tutorial - Full Course for Beginners","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8hly31xKli0","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp","free":true},{"title":"Grokking Algorithms, Second Edition","url":"https://www.manning.com/books/grokking-algorithms-second-edition","type":"book","source":"Manning","free":false},{"title":"Problem Solving with Algorithms and Data Structures using Python","url":"https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/pythonds/index.html","type":"book","source":"Runestone Academy","free":true},{"title":"VisuAlgo","url":"https://visualgo.net/en","type":"interactive","source":"NUS","free":true},{"title":"NeetCode Roadmap","url":"https://neetcode.io/roadmap","type":"interactive","source":"NeetCode","free":true},{"title":"Algorithms by Jeff Erickson","url":"https://jeffe.cs.illinois.edu/teaching/algorithms/","type":"book","source":"UIUC","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Big-O and complexity (implement and master)","desc":"How time and memory grow with input size: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n^2). You should be able to analyze any code you write this way.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Big-O Cheat Sheet","url":"https://www.bigocheatsheet.com/","type":"article","source":"Eric Rowell","free":true}]},{"title":"Core structures: arrays, hash maps, sets, stacks, queues (implement and master)","desc":"Know what Python's list, dict, set, and collections.deque do under the hood and what each operation costs. Build a stack and a queue yourself once. Hash maps are the single most useful structure in practice.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"collections: Container datatypes","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/collections.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Searching, sorting, and recursion (implement and master)","desc":"Write binary search and merge sort by hand and understand why sorting is O(n log n). Learn recursion well enough to trace it on paper. In real work you call sorted(), but you should know what it costs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Problem Solving with Algorithms and Data Structures using Python","url":"https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/pythonds/index.html","type":"book","source":"Runestone Academy","free":true}]},{"title":"Trees and graphs: BFS and DFS (implement and master)","desc":"Trees and graphs model hierarchies, networks, and dependencies. Implement breadth-first and depth-first search yourself. Agent workflows, knowledge graphs, and computation graphs in deep learning all use these ideas.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"VisuAlgo","url":"https://visualgo.net/en","type":"interactive","source":"NUS","free":true}]},{"title":"Heaps, tries, Dijkstra, and dynamic programming (use and understand)","desc":"Use heapq for top-k and priority queues, know what a trie is (prefix trees used in autocomplete and tokenizers), know Dijkstra finds shortest paths, and recognize dynamic programming (solving big problems by caching subproblems). Practice implementing these only if you are preparing for interviews.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"heapq: Heap queue algorithm","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html","type":"docs","source":"Python.org","free":true}]},{"title":"Interview practice patterns","desc":"Two pointers, sliding window, hashing, BFS/DFS, and DP cover most interview problems. A structured problem list beats random grinding.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"NeetCode Roadmap","url":"https://neetcode.io/roadmap","type":"interactive","source":"NeetCode","free":true},{"title":"LeetCode","url":"https://leetcode.com/","type":"interactive","source":"LeetCode","free":true}]},{"title":"Advanced structures (just know they exist)","desc":"B-trees power database indexes, union-find groups connected items, and graph-based approximate nearest-neighbor indexes (like HNSW) power vector search. Know the names and what problem each solves; you will almost never implement them.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ml-concepts","title":"How Machine Learning Works","tier":1,"lane":"ml","prereqs":["what-is-ai","math-intuition"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"r","biz":"a"},"summary":"Machine learning is getting a computer to learn patterns from examples instead of following hand-written rules. This node covers the core ideas: data, features, labels, models, loss, training, and generalization.","why":"These concepts are the shared language of all AI work, from a spreadsheet-sized regression to training an LLM. Once they click, you can read any ML docs or paper intro and judge whether a model's results can be trusted.","depth":"Master the vocabulary and intuitions: supervised vs unsupervised, training vs inference, loss functions, gradient descent as 'adjust weights to reduce error', overfitting, and why we hold out test data. You don't need the math in detail yet. You should be able to explain each idea in plain words and sketch it on a whiteboard.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can explain the difference between supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning with an example of each.","You can describe what happens during training, from loss to gradient to weight update, in plain words.","You can explain overfitting and underfitting and why train, validation, and test splits exist.","You can frame a real problem as an ML task: inputs, target, success metric, and data needed.","You can tell when a problem does not need ML at all."],"project":"Pick three problems from your work or life. For each, write a one-page ML framing: input features, label, type of task, success metric, where the data would come from, a simple non-ML baseline, and the biggest risk of overfitting or bad data.","resources":[{"title":"Google Machine Learning Crash Course","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Specialization","url":"https://www.coursera.org/specializations/machine-learning-introduction","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI / Stanford","free":false},{"title":"StatQuest: Machine Learning playlist","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLblh5JKOoLUICTaGLRoHQDuF_7q2GfuJF","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"MLU-Explain: visual essays on ML concepts","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Intro to Machine Learning","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-machine-learning","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"An Introduction to Statistical Learning (Python edition)","url":"https://www.statlearning.com/","type":"book","source":"James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani, Taylor","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Types of learning","desc":"Supervised learning learns from labeled examples (spam or not). Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels (clusters). Reinforcement learning learns from rewards. Self-supervised learning, which trains LLMs, makes labels from the data itself.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Google: Introduction to Machine Learning","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/intro-to-ml","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Features, labels, and models","desc":"Features are the inputs, the label is what you want to predict, and a model is a function with adjustable parameters that maps one to the other. Regression predicts numbers; classification predicts categories.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Gentle Introduction to Machine Learning","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gv9_4yMHFhI","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}]},{"title":"Loss and gradient descent","desc":"A loss function scores how wrong the model is. Gradient descent nudges each parameter in the direction that lowers the loss, repeated many times. The same loop trains neural networks and LLMs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Linear regression","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/linear-regression","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Generalization, overfitting, and the bias-variance trade-off","desc":"The goal is to do well on new data, not memorize the training set. Too simple a model underfits, too flexible a model overfits. Holding out data is how you find out which is happening.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Bias Variance Tradeoff","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/bias-variance/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Datasets, generalization, and overfitting","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"The ML workflow end to end","desc":"Frame the problem, get and clean data, pick a baseline, train, evaluate, iterate, deploy, and monitor. Most of the effort goes into data and evaluation, not the model.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Rules of Machine Learning","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml","type":"article","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Classic ML vs deep learning vs foundation models","desc":"Tabular data often works best with classic models like gradient boosting. Images, audio, and text favor deep learning. For many language tasks today, prompting a pre-trained LLM beats training anything. Knowing which to reach for saves weeks.","level":"deep","resources":[]}]},{"id":"llm-basics","title":"How LLMs Work (Intuition)","tier":1,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["what-is-ai"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"c","biz":"r","nc":"r","pu":"r"},"summary":"A large language model (LLM) is a neural network trained on huge amounts of text to predict the next token. Scaled up and then tuned to follow instructions, that simple objective produces assistants that can write, reason, code, and use tools.","why":"Having a correct mental model of LLMs helps you use them well and judge them fairly. It explains why they hallucinate, why prompts and context matter so much, why they have knowledge cutoffs, and why outputs vary between runs.","depth":"Must master: next-token prediction, tokens, the context window, pretraining versus post-training, and sampling with temperature. You don't need the math of attention yet; you do need to know the model only sees what is in its context.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can explain in plain words how an LLM generates text one token at a time.","You can describe the stages that turn a raw pretrained model into a helpful assistant.","You can explain context windows, knowledge cutoffs, and why models hallucinate.","You can predict how settings like temperature change a model's output."],"project":"Run the same three prompts (a fact question, a creative task, and a question about a recent event) through two different assistants at low and high temperature if you can. Write a one-page explainer for a non-technical friend that uses what you saw to explain tokens, next-token prediction, knowledge cutoffs, and hallucination.","resources":[{"title":"Large Language Models explained briefly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LPZh9BOjkQs","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"Intro to Large Language Models","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zjkBMFhNj_g","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Generative AI exists because of the transformer","url":"https://ig.ft.com/generative-ai/","type":"interactive","source":"Financial Times","free":true},{"title":"Large language models, explained with a minimum of math and jargon","url":"https://www.understandingai.org/p/large-language-models-explained-with","type":"article","source":"Understanding AI","free":true},{"title":"Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7xTGNNLPyMI","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course, Chapter 1","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/1","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Next-token prediction","desc":"The model reads the text so far and outputs a probability for every possible next token. Generation is just picking a token, appending it, and repeating.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Large Language Models explained briefly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LPZh9BOjkQs","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Pretraining and post-training","desc":"Pretraining on web-scale text gives the model broad knowledge and skill at continuing documents. Post-training (instruction tuning, RLHF, and reinforcement learning on tasks) shapes it into a helpful, safer assistant that can reason and use tools.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7xTGNNLPyMI","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Tokens and the context window","desc":"Models read tokens (word pieces), not letters or words. The context window is the maximum number of tokens the model can consider at once, and it is all the model knows about your conversation.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Sampling and temperature","desc":"Temperature and top-p control how adventurous the token choices are. Low temperature gives more predictable output; higher gives more variety and more risk of errors.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Why LLMs hallucinate","desc":"The model produces plausible continuations, not looked-up facts. Without grounding in retrieved sources or tools, fluent but false answers are a natural failure mode.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"What Is ChatGPT Doing and Why Does It Work?","url":"https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/2023/02/what-is-chatgpt-doing-and-why-does-it-work/","type":"article","source":"Stephen Wolfram","free":true}]},{"title":"What happens inside (a first look)","desc":"Words become vectors, attention lets tokens share information, and stacked layers refine meaning. Interpretability research is starting to show the internal steps models take.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Large language models, explained with a minimum of math and jargon","url":"https://www.understandingai.org/p/large-language-models-explained-with","type":"article","source":"Understanding AI","free":true},{"title":"Tracing the thoughts of a large language model","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/tracing-thoughts-language-model","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]}]},{"id":"custom-assistants","title":"Custom GPTs, Projects & Assistants","tier":2,"lane":"tools","prereqs":["prompt-engineering"],"roles":{"pm":"r","biz":"r","nc":"c","pu":"c"},"summary":"Custom assistants are saved, reusable setups of an AI with your own instructions, files, and tools. Examples include Claude Projects and Skills, ChatGPT Projects and custom GPTs, Gemini Gems, and Microsoft Copilot agents.","why":"Instead of re-explaining your business, style, and rules in every chat, you set it up once. Teams can share one assistant for support replies, proposals, onboarding, or policy questions, so everyone gets consistent results.","depth":"Master writing solid instructions, choosing the right knowledge files, and testing before you share. Know that features and plan availability change often (for example, which plans can build GPTs), so check the current help pages. Connecting tools and actions is a deeper step.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can build a custom assistant with clear instructions and a curated knowledge base.","You can choose between a project, a custom assistant, a skill, and a full automation for a given job.","You can test an assistant on realistic questions and fix where it goes wrong.","You can share an assistant with a team safely, knowing what data it can access."],"project":"Build a custom assistant for one real job, such as answering customer FAQs or drafting proposals in your company style. Write the instructions, upload 3 to 5 knowledge files, and test it on 20 real questions. Track the failures, fix the instructions or files, and share it with one colleague for feedback.","resources":[{"title":"What are projects?","url":"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9517075-what-are-projects","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Use Gems in Gemini Apps","url":"https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/15146780","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Introducing Agent Skills","url":"https://claude.com/resources/articles/skills","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Microsoft Copilot Studio documentation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"On-boarding your AI Intern","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/on-boarding-your-ai-intern","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Academy","url":"https://academy.openai.com/","type":"course","source":"OpenAI","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The main options","desc":"Projects (Claude, ChatGPT) group chats with shared files and instructions. Custom GPTs and Gemini Gems are shareable assistants with a fixed purpose. Copilot Studio builds assistants inside Microsoft 365. Pick based on where your team already works.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"What are projects?","url":"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9517075-what-are-projects","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Use Gems in Gemini Apps","url":"https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/15146780","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Writing system instructions","desc":"Instructions tell the assistant its job, audience, tone, what to do when unsure, and what never to do. Write them like an onboarding doc for a new hire, with examples of good answers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"On-boarding your AI Intern","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/on-boarding-your-ai-intern","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Knowledge files","desc":"Upload the documents the assistant should rely on: FAQs, price lists, style guides, policies. Keep them current, well organized, and free of anything users shouldn't see.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Skills and reusable procedures","desc":"Skills package instructions, examples, and scripts for a specific procedure (like 'make a branded report') that the assistant loads only when needed. They keep long, detailed know-how out of every conversation.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"What are skills?","url":"https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12512176-what-are-skills","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Connecting tools and actions","desc":"Assistants can connect to calendars, CRMs, drives, and other apps through built-in connectors, actions, or MCP servers. This turns an assistant from 'answers questions' into 'does tasks'.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Model Context Protocol","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/","type":"docs","source":"MCP","free":true}]},{"title":"Testing and sharing safely","desc":"Try 15 to 20 real questions, including off-topic and adversarial ones, before sharing. Remember that users can sometimes extract instructions and files, so don't put secrets in them.","level":"core","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ai-strategy","title":"AI Strategy & ROI","tier":2,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-use-cases"],"roles":{"pm":"c","biz":"c","ds":"a"},"summary":"AI strategy is deciding where AI fits in your business goals, what to invest in, in what order, and how you'll measure return on investment (ROI). It ties individual use cases into a plan with budget, owners, and milestones.","why":"Without a strategy, AI efforts become scattered pilots that never scale. A clear plan focuses money and attention on the few bets that matter, and gives you a way to prove results to leadership, investors, or yourself.","depth":"Master building a simple portfolio of use cases, estimating ROI honestly, and setting metrics. Know the bigger strategic questions (does AI change our competitive position or business model?) even if you act on them later.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can write a one-page AI strategy linked to specific business goals.","You can estimate ROI for a use case, including hidden costs like checking time and change management.","You can define metrics that show whether an AI initiative is working.","You can balance quick wins with longer-term bets in a portfolio.","You can explain how AI might change your competitive position."],"project":"Write a one-page AI strategy for a real or sample business: three business goals, the five use cases that serve them, a rough ROI estimate for the top two (with all costs listed), the baseline metric you'll track for each, and a 90-day plan with owners.","resources":[{"title":"AI For Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true},{"title":"AI in the Enterprise","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/ai-in-the-enterprise.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Prediction Machines","url":"https://www.predictionmachines.ai/","type":"book","source":"Agrawal, Gans & Goldfarb","free":false},{"title":"AI For Business Specialization","url":"https://www.coursera.org/specializations/ai-for-business-wharton","type":"course","source":"Wharton (Coursera)","free":false},{"title":"How to Capitalize on Generative AI","url":"https://hbr.org/2023/11/how-to-capitalize-on-generative-ai","type":"article","source":"Harvard Business Review","free":false},{"title":"AI Playbook for the UK Government","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-playbook-for-the-uk-government","type":"docs","source":"UK Government","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Start from business goals","desc":"Begin with what the business needs (grow revenue, cut cycle time, improve service) and work back to where AI helps. Strategies that start from the technology tend to drift.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"AI For Everyone","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/ai-for-everyone","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng)","free":true}]},{"title":"Estimating ROI honestly","desc":"ROI = (value gained minus total cost) divided by total cost. Count tool fees, setup, training, and the time spent reviewing outputs. Time saved only becomes value if it's redirected to something useful.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Metrics and baselines","desc":"Measure the 'before' first: time per task, error rate, cost per ticket, conversion rate. Then track the same numbers after rollout, plus adoption and user satisfaction.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Portfolio thinking","desc":"Mix quick wins (off-the-shelf tools for individual productivity) with process redesigns and a few longer-term bets. Review the portfolio every quarter because AI capability moves fast.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Prediction and competitive advantage","desc":"AI makes prediction and content cheap. Ask what becomes more valuable when that happens: your data, relationships, judgment, distribution. That is where durable advantage lives.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Prediction Machines","url":"https://www.predictionmachines.ai/","type":"book","source":"Agrawal, Gans & Goldfarb","free":false}]},{"title":"Risk and governance in the plan","desc":"Build risk management in from the start with an owner, an acceptable-use policy, and a review process for higher-risk uses. Frameworks like the NIST AI RMF give a ready checklist.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true}]},{"title":"Watching market economics","desc":"Investor and analyst reports show where money is flowing and where it may be overheated. Useful context, but don't let market hype set your priorities.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"AI's $600B Question","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/ais-600b-question","type":"article","source":"Sequoia Capital","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-product-design","title":"Designing AI Products (UX)","tier":2,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-use-cases","llm-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"r","pm":"c","biz":"r"},"summary":"Designing AI products is about creating experiences where users understand what the AI can do, trust it the right amount, and can easily correct it when it's wrong. It covers interface patterns, setting expectations, feedback loops, and measuring quality.","why":"AI output is probabilistic: it varies and is sometimes wrong. Products that hide this lose user trust; products that design for it feel reliable. Good AI UX is often the difference between a demo and a product people keep using.","depth":"Master the core patterns: setting expectations, showing sources, human review of important actions, easy correction, and basic evals. You don't need to build models; you do need to understand enough about how LLMs fail to design around it.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can choose the right interaction pattern (chat, inline suggestion, background agent) for a product problem.","You can design for errors with sources, confidence cues, undo, and human review.","You can write a product spec that includes AI-specific requirements and failure cases.","You can set up a simple evaluation process to judge AI output quality before and after launch.","You can design feedback mechanisms that help the product improve over time."],"project":"Pick an AI feature for a product you know (for example, 'draft a reply' in a support tool). Write a two-page spec covering the user problem, interaction pattern, how errors are shown and corrected, what needs human approval, and 15 eval examples with expected outputs. Mock up the key screen on paper or in a design tool.","resources":[{"title":"People + AI Guidebook","url":"https://pair.withgoogle.com/guidebook/","type":"docs","source":"Google PAIR","free":true},{"title":"Guidelines for Human-AI Interaction","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/haxtoolkit/ai-guidelines/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft HAX Toolkit","free":true},{"title":"The Shape of AI: UX patterns","url":"https://www.shapeof.ai/","type":"article","source":"Shape of AI","free":true},{"title":"Your AI Product Needs Evals","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true},{"title":"What We've Learned From A Year of Building with LLMs","url":"https://applied-llms.org/","type":"article","source":"Yan, Bischof, Frye, Husain, Liu & Shankar","free":true},{"title":"Artificial Intelligence articles and research","url":"https://www.nngroup.com/topic/ai/","type":"article","source":"Nielsen Norman Group","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Human-AI interaction guidelines","desc":"Well-tested principles: make clear what the system can do and how well, show why it did something, support efficient correction, and adapt carefully over time.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Guidelines for Human-AI Interaction","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/haxtoolkit/ai-guidelines/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft HAX Toolkit","free":true},{"title":"People + AI Guidebook","url":"https://pair.withgoogle.com/guidebook/","type":"docs","source":"Google PAIR","free":true}]},{"title":"Interaction patterns","desc":"Chat is only one option. Inline suggestions, one-click actions, side panels, and background agents that report back each fit different jobs. Pick the one that fits the user's workflow.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Shape of AI: UX patterns","url":"https://www.shapeof.ai/","type":"article","source":"Shape of AI","free":true}]},{"title":"Designing for errors and trust","desc":"Show sources, make outputs easy to edit, ask for confirmation before irreversible actions, and offer an easy way out to a human. Aim for 'calibrated trust': users rely on AI where it's good and check it where it isn't.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Evals: measuring quality","desc":"Evals are tests that check AI output against what good looks like, using examples, rubrics, and sometimes another model as a judge. They let PMs know whether a change made the product better or worse.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Your AI Product Needs Evals","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true}]},{"title":"Agentic UX","desc":"When AI takes actions over many steps, users need to see progress, approve risky steps, interrupt, and review what happened. Design for supervision, not just conversation.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Cost, latency, and model choice","desc":"Bigger models are smarter but slower and pricier. Product decisions like streaming responses, caching, and using smaller models for simple steps shape both experience and margins.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Feedback loops and data","desc":"Thumbs up/down, edits, and abandoned outputs are signals. Collect them with consent and use them to find failure patterns and improve prompts or retrieval.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"ai-regulation","title":"AI Regulation & Compliance","tier":2,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["ai-ethics","ai-privacy"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"a","ds":"a","pm":"r","biz":"c","nc":"a"},"summary":"AI regulation covers the laws and standards that govern how AI is built and used, such as the EU AI Act, data protection law, and sector rules for hiring, lending, and health.","why":"If you ship AI to customers in the EU or in regulated industries, compliance decides what you can build and what paperwork you need. Fines and forced shutdowns are real risks.","depth":"Business owners and PMs must master the risk-based logic of the EU AI Act, which of their uses might be high-risk, and the existing laws (privacy, consumer protection, anti-discrimination) that already apply. Engineers only need to know the obligations that land on them: logging, transparency notices, and documentation. Leave the legal detail to counsel.","hours":8,"outcomes":["You can explain the EU AI Act's risk tiers and place a given use case in one.","You can list the main AI Act dates, including the delayed high-risk deadlines.","You can name which existing laws (GDPR, consumer protection, anti-discrimination) already apply to an AI feature.","You can use NIST AI RMF or ISO/IEC 42001 as a checklist even where no AI law applies."],"project":"List every AI feature or tool your company uses or plans (or invent five for a sample company). For each, classify its EU AI Act risk tier, note the applicable deadline, list other laws that apply, and write the top three compliance actions. Check your classifications against the AI Act Explorer's text.","resources":[{"title":"High-level summary of the AI Act","url":"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/high-level-summary/","type":"article","source":"Future of Life Institute","free":true},{"title":"AI Act (official policy page)","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai","type":"docs","source":"European Commission","free":true},{"title":"Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (full text)","url":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj","type":"docs","source":"EUR-Lex","free":true},{"title":"Global AI Law and Policy Tracker","url":"https://iapp.org/resources/article/global-ai-legislation-tracker/","type":"article","source":"IAPP","free":true},{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"OECD AI Policy Observatory","url":"https://oecd.ai/","type":"docs","source":"OECD","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"EU AI Act: the risk tiers","desc":"The Act bans some uses outright (like social scoring), sets heavy rules for high-risk uses (hiring, credit, education, critical infrastructure), adds transparency duties for chatbots and deepfakes, and leaves most other uses alone.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"High-level summary of the AI Act","url":"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/high-level-summary/","type":"article","source":"Future of Life Institute","free":true}]},{"title":"EU AI Act: the timeline","desc":"Bans and AI literacy duties applied from February 2025, general-purpose model rules from August 2025, and most other rules from August 2026. The 2026 AI Omnibus moved high-risk deadlines to December 2027 (Annex III uses) and August 2028 (AI inside regulated products).","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"AI Act (official policy page)","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai","type":"docs","source":"European Commission","free":true}]},{"title":"General-purpose AI model rules","desc":"Providers of foundation models must publish training-data summaries and technical documentation. The most capable models carry extra safety duties. The voluntary Code of Practice shows how to comply.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The General-Purpose AI Code of Practice","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai","type":"docs","source":"European Commission","free":true}]},{"title":"Laws that already apply","desc":"GDPR covers personal data and automated decisions with legal effects. Consumer protection, anti-discrimination, and sector rules apply to AI just as they do to any other product.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"GDPR Art. 22: Automated individual decision-making","url":"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/","type":"docs","source":"gdpr-info.eu","free":true},{"title":"EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data","url":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en","type":"docs","source":"EDPB","free":true}]},{"title":"US and global landscape","desc":"The US has no single federal AI law. Rules come from agencies, state laws, and executive orders that change often. Use a tracker rather than memorizing a list.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Global AI Law and Policy Tracker","url":"https://iapp.org/resources/article/global-ai-legislation-tracker/","type":"article","source":"IAPP","free":true}]},{"title":"Voluntary standards","desc":"NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001 give a structure for managing AI risk. They are often what customers and auditors ask for, even when no law requires them.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true}]}]},{"id":"info-theory-optim","title":"Optimization & Information Theory","tier":2,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":["calculus","probability-stats"],"roles":{"mle":"r","ds":"a","rs":"c"},"summary":"Optimization is the science of how models find good parameters: gradient descent and optimizers like Adam. Information theory measures information and surprise, and gives us entropy, cross-entropy, and KL divergence, the losses most models are trained on.","why":"When training is unstable, slow, or stuck, the answer is usually in the optimizer settings or the loss. Cross-entropy, perplexity, and KL penalties in RLHF all come straight from information theory.","depth":"ML engineers must master SGD, momentum, Adam/AdamW, learning-rate schedules, and what cross-entropy, perplexity, and KL divergence mean. Researchers should go deeper into loss-landscape geometry, convergence, and newer optimizers. Data scientists need only cross-entropy and the basic idea of gradient descent. Convex optimization proofs and coding theory are 'know it exists' unless you do theory research.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can explain entropy, cross-entropy, KL divergence, and perplexity, and how they relate.","You can explain why next-token cross-entropy loss is the training objective for LLMs.","You can compare SGD, momentum, and AdamW and choose sensible learning rates, warmup, and decay.","You can diagnose training curves that show too-high learning rate, plateaus, or divergence."],"project":"Train the same small network (for example, an MLP on MNIST or a tiny character-level language model) with SGD, SGD with momentum, and AdamW, each at three learning rates. Plot all loss curves on one chart and report final perplexity or accuracy. Write a paragraph explaining the differences using what you learned about momentum, adaptive steps, and cross-entropy.","resources":[{"title":"Visual Information Theory","url":"https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-09-Visual-Information/","type":"article","source":"Chris Olah","free":true},{"title":"An overview of gradient descent optimization algorithms","url":"https://www.ruder.io/optimizing-gradient-descent/","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Ruder","free":true},{"title":"Optimization Algorithms (Dive into Deep Learning ch. 12)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_optimization/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"Why Momentum Really Works","url":"https://distill.pub/2017/momentum/","type":"interactive","source":"Distill","free":true},{"title":"Deep Learning (ch. 3 and 8)","url":"https://www.deeplearningbook.org/","type":"book","source":"Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville","free":true},{"title":"Information Theory, Inference, and Learning Algorithms","url":"https://www.inference.org.uk/itprnn/book.html","type":"book","source":"David MacKay","free":true},{"title":"Solving Wordle using information theory","url":"https://www.3blue1brown.com/lessons/wordle","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Entropy, cross-entropy, and KL divergence","desc":"Entropy measures average surprise. Cross-entropy measures the cost of using the wrong distribution. KL divergence is the gap between them, and RLHF uses it to keep a model close to its starting point.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Visual Information Theory","url":"https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-09-Visual-Information/","type":"article","source":"Chris Olah","free":true}]},{"title":"Perplexity and compression","desc":"Perplexity is the exponent of cross-entropy, a measure of how surprised a language model is by real text. Better prediction means better compression.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Information Theory (Dive into Deep Learning 22.11)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_appendix-mathematics-for-deep-learning/information-theory.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Gradient descent and SGD","desc":"Take small steps against the gradient, using a random mini-batch each time. Batch size and learning rate trade off speed, noise, and stability.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Optimization Algorithms (Dive into Deep Learning ch. 12)","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_optimization/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Momentum, Adam, and AdamW","desc":"Momentum smooths updates. Adam adapts the step size per parameter. AdamW fixes how weight decay interacts with Adam and is the default for training transformers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"An overview of gradient descent optimization algorithms","url":"https://www.ruder.io/optimizing-gradient-descent/","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Ruder","free":true},{"title":"Why Momentum Really Works","url":"https://distill.pub/2017/momentum/","type":"interactive","source":"Distill","free":true}]},{"title":"Learning-rate schedules and newer optimizers","desc":"Warmup then decay (cosine or linear) is standard. Newer optimizers like Muon, which orthogonalize updates for weight matrices, are being adopted in large-scale training.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Muon: An optimizer for hidden layers in neural networks","url":"https://kellerjordan.github.io/posts/muon/","type":"article","source":"Keller Jordan","free":true},{"title":"Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization (AdamW)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05101","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Convex optimization and theory","desc":"Convex problems have one best answer and strong guarantees. Deep learning is not convex, but the ideas (duality, conditioning, convergence rates) still guide intuition.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Convex Optimization","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/","type":"book","source":"Boyd & Vandenberghe","free":true}]}]},{"id":"data-viz-eda","title":"EDA & Data Visualization","tier":2,"lane":"code","prereqs":["python-data"],"roles":{"mle":"r","ds":"c","rs":"a"},"summary":"Exploratory data analysis (EDA) is getting to know a dataset before modeling it: distributions, missing values, outliers, and relationships. Data visualization is how you see those patterns and show them to others.","why":"Most modeling mistakes, like leakage, broken labels, and skewed samples, are visible in a few plots before you train anything. Clear charts are also how data work turns into decisions people actually act on.","depth":"Master a repeatable EDA checklist and the core chart types (histogram, box plot, scatter, line, bar, heatmap) in one library, plus the judgment to pick the right one. Interactive dashboards and design theory are worth exploring later. Auto-profiling tools are helpful shortcuts but don't replace looking yourself.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can run a structured first pass on a new dataset and list its quality problems.","You can choose a chart type that fits the question and the data type.","You can make clean, labeled plots with matplotlib or seaborn.","You can spot outliers, skew, and suspicious correlations that would mislead a model.","You can turn an analysis into a short visual story for a non-technical reader."],"project":"Pick a messy public dataset and write a notebook that follows your EDA checklist, finds at least three data quality issues, and ends with a one-page summary of five charts, each with a one-sentence takeaway a manager could act on.","resources":[{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Data Visualization","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/data-visualization","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Fundamentals of Data Visualization","url":"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/","type":"book","source":"Claus O. Wilke","free":true},{"title":"Python Data Science Handbook (Visualization with Matplotlib chapter)","url":"https://jakevdp.github.io/PythonDataScienceHandbook/","type":"book","source":"Jake VanderPlas","free":true},{"title":"seaborn user guide and tutorial","url":"https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"seaborn","free":true},{"title":"From Data to Viz","url":"https://www.data-to-viz.com/","type":"interactive","source":"data-to-viz.com","free":true},{"title":"Storytelling with Data (books)","url":"https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/books","type":"book","source":"Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The EDA checklist","desc":"Shape, types, missing values, duplicates, summary statistics, distributions of each column, then relationships between columns and the target. Do it in the same order every time.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"R for Data Science: Exploratory data analysis (concepts carry over to Python)","url":"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/","type":"book","source":"Hadley Wickham","free":true}]},{"title":"Plotting with matplotlib and seaborn","desc":"matplotlib is the base plotting library; seaborn sits on top and makes statistical plots in one line. Learn figures, axes, and how to label everything.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Matplotlib Quick start guide","url":"https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html","type":"docs","source":"Matplotlib","free":true},{"title":"seaborn user guide and tutorial","url":"https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"seaborn","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing the right chart","desc":"Distributions call for histograms or box plots, trends for lines, comparisons for bars, relationships for scatter plots. A decision tree of chart types saves a lot of guessing.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"From Data to Viz","url":"https://www.data-to-viz.com/","type":"interactive","source":"data-to-viz.com","free":true}]},{"title":"Outliers, skew, and data quality","desc":"Find impossible values, long tails that need a log scale, and leakage hints like a feature that predicts the target suspiciously well.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Design principles for clear charts","desc":"Remove clutter, label directly, use color with purpose, and start from the message you want the reader to take away.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Fundamentals of Data Visualization","url":"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/","type":"book","source":"Claus O. Wilke","free":true}]},{"title":"Interactive charts and profiling tools","desc":"Plotly and Altair make interactive charts; ydata-profiling generates an automatic report of a DataFrame. LLM-powered data analysis in chat tools can speed up a first look, but always check its claims.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Plotly Python","url":"https://plotly.com/python/","type":"docs","source":"Plotly","free":true},{"title":"ydata-profiling","url":"https://docs.profiling.ydata.ai/latest/","type":"tool","source":"YData","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-coding-tools","title":"AI Coding Assistants","tier":2,"lane":"code","prereqs":["git-cli","prompt-engineering"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"r","pm":"a","nc":"a"},"summary":"AI coding assistants write, edit, explain, and debug code for you. They range from autocomplete in your editor to agents like Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor's agent that read your repo, run commands, and make multi-file changes.","why":"In 2026 most professional code is written with AI help, and agentic tools can take on whole tasks. The skill is no longer typing code fast; it is giving clear instructions, managing context, and reviewing output so you ship working code instead of plausible-looking bugs.","depth":"Master one agentic tool deeply: how to scope a task, give it context (project instruction files, docs, tests), review diffs, and use git to stay safe. Know the main tool categories and their trade-offs. Advanced setups like custom MCP servers, subagents, and parallel agents are worth learning once the basics are habit.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can use an agentic coding tool to implement a feature across several files and verify it with tests.","You can write a project instruction file (like CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md) that makes the agent follow your conventions.","You can review AI-written code critically and catch wrong assumptions, security issues, and invented APIs.","You can use git checkpoints so any agent change is easy to inspect or roll back.","You can choose between autocomplete, chat, and autonomous agents for a given task."],"project":"Use an agentic coding tool to add a real feature to one of your own repos. Write a project instruction file first, ask for a plan, have it write tests, and review every diff. Then write a short log of what it got wrong and how you caught it.","resources":[{"title":"Best practices for Claude Code","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Simon Willison on AI-assisted programming","url":"https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai-assisted-programming/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Vibe engineering","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Oct/7/vibe-engineering/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Andrej Karpathy: Software Is Changing (Again)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LCEmiRjPEtQ","type":"video","source":"Y Combinator","free":true},{"title":"Cursor Docs","url":"https://cursor.com/docs","type":"docs","source":"Cursor","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Codex (open-source CLI)","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex","type":"tool","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Aider: AI pair programming in your terminal","url":"https://aider.chat/","type":"tool","source":"Aider","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The tool landscape","desc":"Inline completion (Copilot), AI-first editors (Cursor and similar), terminal and cloud agents (Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Aider), and app builders. They differ in autonomy, context access, and how much you review.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Claude Code overview","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"GitHub Copilot documentation","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot","type":"docs","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Scoping tasks and giving context","desc":"Agents do best with a clear goal, constraints, and a way to check their work. Ask for a plan first on bigger tasks, point to relevant files, and break work into reviewable steps.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Best practices for Claude Code","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Project instructions and memory files","desc":"Files like CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or Cursor rules tell the agent how your project works: commands, style, and do's and don'ts. Short and specific beats long and vague.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"AGENTS.md","url":"https://agents.md/","type":"docs","source":"agents.md","free":true}]},{"title":"Reviewing and verifying AI code","desc":"Read every diff. Have the agent write and run tests. Watch for invented library functions, silent error swallowing, deleted tests, and leaked secrets. You own the code it writes.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Here's how I use LLMs to help me write code","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Mar/11/using-llms-for-code/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}]},{"title":"Safety: permissions, sandboxes, and git","desc":"Agents can run shell commands. Use permission settings, work on a branch, commit often, and be careful with untrusted repos or web content, which can contain prompt injection.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Extending agents: MCP, subagents, and parallel work","desc":"MCP servers connect your agent to tools like databases, issue trackers, and docs. Subagents and parallel sessions split big tasks. Powerful, but only after the basics are solid.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Model Context Protocol","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/","type":"docs","source":"MCP","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ml-algorithms","title":"Classic ML Algorithms","tier":2,"lane":"ml","prereqs":["ml-concepts","python-data","probability-stats"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c"},"summary":"Classic machine learning algorithms are the workhorses that came before deep learning: linear and logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting, k-nearest neighbors, clustering, and dimensionality reduction.","why":"On tabular business data (customers, transactions, sensors) these models are often more accurate, cheaper, and easier to explain than neural networks. They are also the fastest way to build a strong baseline before trying anything fancier.","depth":"Implement from scratch and understand deeply: linear regression and logistic regression (with gradient descent), and how a decision tree chooses splits. Understand the ideas and use fluently in scikit-learn: regularization (L1/L2), random forests, gradient boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM), k-means, and PCA. Just know what they are and when they fit: SVMs, naive Bayes, k-nearest neighbors, DBSCAN, hierarchical clustering, and t-SNE/UMAP.","hours":50,"outcomes":["You can implement linear and logistic regression from scratch in NumPy and match scikit-learn's results.","You can explain how a decision tree splits, and why random forests and boosting beat a single tree.","You can train, tune, and compare several models on a tabular dataset with scikit-learn pipelines.","You can choose a sensible first model for a new problem and justify the choice.","You can use k-means and PCA to explore unlabeled data and explain what they found."],"project":"Implement logistic regression from scratch in NumPy and check it against scikit-learn on a binary classification dataset. Then compare it with a decision tree, random forest, and LightGBM in proper pipelines with cross-validation, and write a short note on which won and why.","resources":[{"title":"An Introduction to Statistical Learning (Python edition)","url":"https://www.statlearning.com/","type":"book","source":"James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani, Taylor","free":true},{"title":"StatQuest: Machine Learning playlist","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLblh5JKOoLUICTaGLRoHQDuF_7q2GfuJF","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn User Guide","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn MOOC","url":"https://inria.github.io/scikit-learn-mooc/","type":"course","source":"Inria","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Specialization","url":"https://www.coursera.org/specializations/machine-learning-introduction","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI / Stanford","free":false},{"title":"Stanford CS229: Machine Learning","url":"https://cs229.stanford.edu/","type":"course","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"The Elements of Statistical Learning","url":"https://hastie.su.domains/ElemStatLearn/","type":"book","source":"Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Linear and logistic regression (implement from scratch)","desc":"Linear regression fits a weighted sum to predict a number; logistic regression squashes it through a sigmoid to predict a probability. Build both in NumPy with gradient descent. Nearly every later model, including neural networks, extends these ideas.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Linear Regression (visual essay)","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/linear-regression/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"StatQuest: Logistic Regression","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yIYKR4sgzI8","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"The model is a weighted sum","desc":"A prediction is w·x + b. Each weight says how much one feature pushes the prediction up or down."},{"title":"Loss functions","desc":"Mean squared error scores regression; log loss (cross-entropy) scores classification. Training means making this number small."},{"title":"Gradient descent","desc":"Compute how the loss changes with each weight, take a small step the other way, repeat. Too large a learning rate makes it diverge."},{"title":"Sigmoid and the decision threshold","desc":"The sigmoid turns a score into a probability between 0 and 1. Moving the 0.5 cutoff trades precision for recall."},{"title":"Feature scaling","desc":"Standardize features (mean 0, std 1) so gradient descent converges quickly and weights are comparable."},{"title":"Reading coefficients","desc":"Sign and size tell you a feature's effect, but correlated features make individual weights unstable."}]},{"title":"Regularization: L1, L2 (understand deeply)","desc":"Regularization adds a penalty for large weights so the model doesn't overfit. Ridge (L2) shrinks weights, Lasso (L1) can zero them out. Know what the penalty does to the model; one scikit-learn parameter turns it on.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Regularization Part 1: Ridge (L2) Regression","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q81RR3yKn30","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"Bias–variance trade-off","desc":"A penalty makes the model slightly less flexible (more bias) in exchange for much less overfitting (less variance)."},{"title":"L2 / Ridge","desc":"Penalizes squared weights. Shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero; a safe default, especially with correlated features."},{"title":"L1 / Lasso","desc":"Penalizes absolute weights. Pushes some weights exactly to zero, so it doubles as feature selection."},{"title":"Elastic Net","desc":"A mix of L1 and L2 that keeps sparsity while staying stable when features are correlated."},{"title":"Tuning the strength","desc":"alpha in Ridge/Lasso, C (the inverse) in LogisticRegression. Pick it with cross-validation, never on the test set."},{"title":"Scale before you penalize","desc":"Penalties compare weights directly, so unscaled features get punished unevenly."}]},{"title":"Decision trees (implement a simple one)","desc":"A tree asks yes/no questions about features to split the data into purer groups. Writing a small one yourself (Gini or entropy splits, max depth) makes ensembles easy to understand.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Decision Trees (visual essay)","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/decision-tree/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"Decision and Classification Trees, Clearly Explained","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_L39rN6gz7Y","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"Impurity: Gini and entropy","desc":"Both measure how mixed the labels in a node are. A pure node (one class) scores zero."},{"title":"Greedy split search","desc":"Try every feature and threshold, keep the split with the biggest impurity drop (information gain), then recurse."},{"title":"Stopping and pruning","desc":"max_depth, min_samples_leaf, and pruning stop a tree from growing until it memorizes the training data."},{"title":"Regression trees","desc":"Leaves predict the mean of their samples; splits are chosen to reduce variance instead of impurity."},{"title":"Trees as rules","desc":"A shallow tree reads as nested if/else rules, which is why trees are easy to explain to non-technical people."},{"title":"Weaknesses","desc":"Single trees are unstable (small data changes flip splits) and draw only axis-aligned boundaries. Ensembles fix both."}]},{"title":"Random forests and gradient boosting (understand and use)","desc":"Random forests average many trees trained on random subsets. Gradient boosting adds trees one at a time, each fixing the last one's errors. XGBoost and LightGBM are still the best default for tabular data. Understand the idea and the key hyperparameters; don't implement them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Gradient Boost Part 1: Regression Main Ideas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3CC4N4z3GJc","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"XGBoost: Introduction to Boosted Trees","url":"https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/en/stable/tutorials/model.html","type":"docs","source":"XGBoost","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"Bagging","desc":"Train many trees on bootstrap samples (random draws with replacement) and average them. Averaging cuts variance."},{"title":"Random feature subsets","desc":"Each split only considers a random subset of features (max_features), so the trees disagree in useful ways."},{"title":"Boosting","desc":"Each new tree fits the errors (gradients) of the ensemble so far; a learning rate shrinks each tree's contribution."},{"title":"Hyperparameters that matter","desc":"n_estimators, learning_rate, max_depth or num_leaves, and early stopping on a validation set."},{"title":"XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost","desc":"Same core idea. LightGBM is fastest on big data; CatBoost handles categorical features with little preprocessing."},{"title":"Explaining predictions","desc":"Built-in feature importance can mislead. SHAP values explain individual predictions more reliably."}]},{"title":"Unsupervised: k-means and PCA (understand and use)","desc":"k-means groups points into k clusters around centers. PCA compresses many features into a few directions that keep the most variance. Both are everyday tools for exploring data and embeddings.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"StatQuest: K-means clustering","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4b5d3muPQmA","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"StatQuest: PCA Step-by-Step","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FgakZw6K1QQ","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"The k-means loop","desc":"Assign each point to its nearest center, move each center to the mean of its points, repeat until nothing changes."},{"title":"Choosing k","desc":"Use the elbow plot and silhouette score as hints, then check that the clusters make sense in your domain."},{"title":"Where k-means fails","desc":"It assumes round clusters of similar size and is sensitive to feature scale and outliers. k-means++ helps with starting points."},{"title":"What PCA finds","desc":"Orthogonal directions (principal components) that capture the most variance. Each is a weighted mix of the original features."},{"title":"Explained variance","desc":"The explained variance ratio tells you how many components you need to keep, e.g. enough for 95%."},{"title":"Typical uses","desc":"Plot high-dimensional data in 2D, compress embeddings, and remove noise before another model. Standardize first."}]},{"title":"The wider toolbox (know they exist)","desc":"SVMs find the widest margin between classes, naive Bayes is a fast probabilistic text classifier, k-nearest neighbors predicts from the closest examples, DBSCAN finds clusters of any shape, and t-SNE/UMAP make 2D maps of high-dimensional data. Know when each fits; reach for docs when you need one.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Choosing the right estimator","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/machine_learning_map.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"Support vector machines","desc":"Find the widest margin between classes; kernels handle curved boundaries. Strong on small datasets with many features."},{"title":"Naive Bayes","desc":"Assumes features are independent given the class. Fast, surprisingly strong baseline for text such as spam filtering."},{"title":"k-nearest neighbors","desc":"No real training: predict from the k closest examples. Needs scaled features and gets slow at scale. Vector search uses the same idea."},{"title":"DBSCAN","desc":"Groups dense regions into clusters of any shape and marks sparse points as noise. You don't choose k."},{"title":"Hierarchical clustering","desc":"Builds a tree of merges (a dendrogram) that you can cut at any level to get clusters."},{"title":"t-SNE and UMAP","desc":"Make 2D maps of high-dimensional data for looking at it. Distances between clusters on the map don't mean much."}]},{"title":"scikit-learn fluency","desc":"The fit/predict/transform API, Pipelines, and hyperparameter search. Once you know the pattern, every model works the same way.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn MOOC","url":"https://inria.github.io/scikit-learn-mooc/","type":"course","source":"Inria","free":true}],"concepts":[{"title":"The estimator API","desc":"fit learns from data; predict / predict_proba / transform apply it. Fit preprocessing on training data only."},{"title":"Pipelines and ColumnTransformer","desc":"Bundle scaling, encoding, and the model into one object so preprocessing can't leak test data into training."},{"title":"Splits and cross-validation","desc":"train_test_split for a holdout set; cross_val_score with stratified folds for a more reliable estimate."},{"title":"Hyperparameter search","desc":"GridSearchCV tries every combination; RandomizedSearchCV samples them and is usually the better use of time."},{"title":"Metrics","desc":"classification_report, roc_auc_score, mean_absolute_error and friends. Pick the one that matches the real cost of mistakes."},{"title":"Baselines first","desc":"DummyClassifier/DummyRegressor tell you what 'no skill' scores, so you know whether your model actually helps."}]}]},{"id":"model-eval-ml","title":"Model Evaluation & Validation","tier":2,"lane":"ml","prereqs":["ml-concepts","probability-stats"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"c","rs":"c","pm":"a"},"summary":"Model evaluation is measuring how well a model will work on data it has never seen, using the right metrics and the right data splits. Validation is the process that keeps those numbers honest.","why":"A model with 99% accuracy can be useless or harmful if the metric or split is wrong. Good evaluation is what lets you trust a result, compare models fairly, and catch problems before users do. The same discipline carries straight into LLM evals.","depth":"Master train/validation/test splits, cross-validation, the confusion matrix, precision/recall/F1, ROC-AUC, and regression metrics like MAE and RMSE, plus how to spot data leakage. Calibration, statistical tests between models, and fairness metrics need a working understanding. Advanced resampling theory is optional.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can set up splits and cross-validation that match how the model will be used, including time-based splits.","You can choose a metric that matches the business cost of each kind of error.","You can read a confusion matrix and explain the precision-recall trade-off to a non-expert.","You can detect data leakage and explain why a suspiciously good score is suspicious.","You can compare two models and say whether the difference is real or noise."],"project":"On an imbalanced dataset (like credit card fraud), compare accuracy, F1, ROC-AUC, and PR-AUC for two models. Then deliberately introduce a leak, show how much it inflates the score, fix it with a proper pipeline, and pick a decision threshold based on an assumed cost for false positives and false negatives.","resources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Classification","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn: Cross-validation","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"MLU-Explain: visual essays on ML concepts","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Fundamentals: The Confusion Matrix","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kdsp6soqA7o","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"Model Evaluation, Model Selection, and Algorithm Selection in Machine Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12808","type":"paper","source":"Sebastian Raschka (arXiv)","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Intermediate Machine Learning","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intermediate-machine-learning","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Splits and cross-validation","desc":"Train on one part, tune on validation, report on a test set you touch once. Cross-validation rotates the held-out fold to get a more stable estimate. Use time-based splits for time-ordered data and grouped splits when rows from one user must stay together.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Train, Test, and Validation Sets","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/train-test-validation/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true},{"title":"StatQuest: Cross Validation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fSytzGwwBVw","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true}]},{"title":"Classification metrics","desc":"Accuracy, precision (how many flagged items were right), recall (how many true items you caught), F1, and the confusion matrix. Accuracy misleads on imbalanced data like fraud.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Accuracy, recall, precision","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Precision and Recall","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/precision-recall/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true}]},{"title":"Thresholds, ROC, and PR curves","desc":"A classifier outputs a score; the threshold turns it into a decision. ROC-AUC and precision-recall curves show performance across all thresholds so you can pick one that fits your costs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"ROC and AUC, Clearly Explained","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4jRBRDbJemM","type":"video","source":"StatQuest","free":true},{"title":"ROC and AUC (visual essay)","url":"https://mlu-explain.github.io/roc-auc/","type":"interactive","source":"Amazon MLU","free":true}]},{"title":"Regression metrics","desc":"MAE (average error size), RMSE (punishes big errors more), and R-squared. Pick the one whose errors match what hurts in practice.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Metrics and scoring","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Data leakage and common pitfalls","desc":"Leakage is when information from the test set or the future sneaks into training, giving great scores that collapse in production. Fitting preprocessing on all data before splitting is the classic mistake.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Common pitfalls and recommended practices","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle: Data Leakage","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/code/alexisbcook/data-leakage","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true}]},{"title":"Tuning, learning curves, and calibration","desc":"Hyperparameter search with cross-validation, learning curves that show whether more data would help, and calibration, which checks that a predicted 70% really happens about 70% of the time.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Tuning the hyper-parameters of an estimator","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/grid_search.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn: Probability calibration","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Comparing models and statistical significance","desc":"Small score differences are often noise. Confidence intervals, repeated cross-validation, and paired tests tell you whether a model is really better.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Model Evaluation, Model Selection, and Algorithm Selection in Machine Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12808","type":"paper","source":"Sebastian Raschka (arXiv)","free":true}]}]},{"id":"feature-engineering","title":"Feature Engineering","tier":2,"lane":"ml","prereqs":["ml-concepts","python-data"],"roles":{"mle":"c","ds":"c"},"summary":"Feature engineering is turning raw data into the inputs a model learns from: cleaning, encoding categories, scaling numbers, building new columns from domain knowledge, and choosing which features to keep.","why":"On tabular data, good features usually matter more than the choice of algorithm. A thoughtful ratio, time-since, or aggregate feature can beat weeks of model tuning, and doing it carelessly is the main source of data leakage.","depth":"Master handling missing values, encoding categorical variables, scaling, date and aggregate features, and doing all of it inside a scikit-learn Pipeline so it never leaks. Feature selection and importance methods need a working understanding. Automated feature tools and feature stores are good to know about but rarely needed early.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can build a preprocessing pipeline with imputation, encoding, and scaling that fits only on training data.","You can create useful features from dates, text, and grouped aggregates.","You can choose an encoding for a categorical column based on its cardinality and the model.","You can use permutation importance or SHAP to check which features a model really relies on.","You can show with cross-validation that a new feature actually helped."],"project":"Take a tabular dataset with dates, categories, and missing values (for example house prices). Build a baseline pipeline with minimal features, then add five engineered features one at a time, measuring each with cross-validation. Keep only those that help, and explain the final model with permutation importance.","resources":[{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Feature Engineering","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/feature-engineering","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"Feature Engineering and Selection: A Practical Approach for Predictive Models","url":"https://feat.engineering/","type":"book","source":"Max Kuhn and Kjell Johnson","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn: Pipelines and composite estimators","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Working with numerical data","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/numerical-data","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Interpretable Machine Learning","url":"https://christophm.github.io/interpretable-ml-book/","type":"book","source":"Christoph Molnar","free":true},{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Intermediate Machine Learning","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intermediate-machine-learning","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Missing values and imputation","desc":"Fill gaps with a median, a constant, or a model, and often add a 'was missing' flag, because missingness itself can be a signal.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Imputation of missing values","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/impute.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Encoding categorical data","desc":"One-hot encoding for few categories, ordinal or target encoding for many. Tree models and linear models want different things. Watch for categories that appear only in test data.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Working with categorical data","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/categorical-data","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true}]},{"title":"Scaling and transforming numbers","desc":"Standardizing, min-max scaling, log transforms for skewed values, and binning. Linear models, k-NN, and neural nets need scaling; tree models mostly don't.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Google ML Crash Course: Working with numerical data","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/numerical-data","type":"course","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn: Preprocessing data","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Pipelines and ColumnTransformer","desc":"Bundle every preprocessing step with the model so cross-validation and production apply exactly the same transformations, fitted only on training data. This is the main defense against leakage.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Pipelines and composite estimators","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Domain features: dates, aggregates, ratios, text","desc":"Day of week, time since last purchase, a customer's average order value, price per square meter. Text can become counts, TF-IDF, or LLM embeddings. This is where domain knowledge pays off most.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Kaggle Learn: Feature Engineering","url":"https://www.kaggle.com/learn/feature-engineering","type":"interactive","source":"Kaggle","free":true},{"title":"scikit-learn: Feature extraction","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/feature_extraction.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true}]},{"title":"Feature selection and importance","desc":"Drop features that add noise or leak the target. Permutation importance and SHAP show what the model actually uses, which also helps explain it to stakeholders.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"scikit-learn: Permutation feature importance","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/permutation_importance.html","type":"docs","source":"scikit-learn","free":true},{"title":"SHAP documentation","url":"https://shap.readthedocs.io/en/latest/","type":"docs","source":"SHAP","free":true}]},{"title":"Feature stores and automated tools","desc":"Feature stores share and serve features consistently across teams in production. Libraries like skrub automate parts of preprocessing for messy DataFrames. Know they exist.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"skrub: Machine learning with dataframes","url":"https://skrub-data.org/stable/","type":"tool","source":"skrub","free":true}]}]},{"id":"neural-networks","title":"Neural Networks","tier":2,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["ml-concepts","linear-algebra","calculus"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"A neural network is a function built from layers of simple units. Each layer multiplies its input by a matrix of weights, adds a bias, and applies a nonlinearity, and training adjusts those weights so the output gets closer to what you want.","why":"Every modern AI system, from image classifiers to LLMs, is a neural network underneath. Once you understand forward passes, loss, and backpropagation, the rest of deep learning becomes variations on one idea.","depth":"Must master: what a layer computes, why nonlinearities matter, what a loss and a gradient tell you, and how gradient descent uses them. You don't need to derive backprop for exotic layers by hand, but you should be able to do it once for a tiny network.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can explain what a neuron, a layer, an activation function, and a loss function are in plain words.","You can trace how backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute gradients for every weight.","You can build and train a small multilayer perceptron from scratch in NumPy or plain Python.","You can spot underfitting and overfitting from training and validation curves."],"project":"Build a two-layer neural network from scratch in NumPy (forward pass, cross-entropy loss, manual backprop, gradient descent) and train it to classify MNIST digits above 95% accuracy. Then check your gradients against a numerical estimate.","resources":[{"title":"Neural networks (video series)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLZHQObOWTQDNU6R1_67000Dx_ZCJB-3pi","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"Neural Networks: Zero to Hero","url":"https://karpathy.ai/zero-to-hero.html","type":"course","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Dive into Deep Learning: Multilayer Perceptrons","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"TensorFlow Playground","url":"https://playground.tensorflow.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Neural Networks and Deep Learning","url":"http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/","type":"book","source":"Michael Nielsen","free":true},{"title":"Understanding Deep Learning","url":"https://udlbook.github.io/udlbook/","type":"book","source":"Simon J.D. Prince","free":true},{"title":"Deep Learning Specialization","url":"https://www.coursera.org/specializations/deep-learning","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Neurons, layers, and activations","desc":"A layer is a matrix multiply plus a bias, followed by a nonlinearity such as ReLU or GELU. Without the nonlinearity, stacking layers would collapse into a single linear function.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"But what is a neural network?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aircAruvnKk","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Loss functions","desc":"The loss is a single number that measures how wrong the network is. Mean squared error is common for regression and cross-entropy for classification, and training means pushing this number down.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Backpropagation and gradient descent","desc":"Backprop applies the chain rule backwards through the network to find how each weight affects the loss. Gradient descent then nudges every weight a small step in the direction that lowers the loss.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Backpropagation, intuitively","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ilg3gGewQ5U","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMj-3S1tku0","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Computational graphs and autograd","desc":"Frameworks record every operation as a graph so they can compute gradients automatically. Building a tiny autograd engine yourself makes PyTorch feel much less magical.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"micrograd","url":"https://github.com/karpathy/micrograd","type":"tool","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Calculus on Computational Graphs: Backpropagation","url":"https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-08-Backprop/","type":"article","source":"Chris Olah","free":true}]},{"title":"Generalization, overfitting, and regularization","desc":"A network can memorize its training data instead of learning the pattern. Validation sets, weight decay, dropout, and more data are the standard defenses.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Universal approximation and why depth helps","desc":"In theory a wide enough network can approximate almost any function. In practice, deeper networks learn reusable features more efficiently than very wide shallow ones.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Neural Networks and Deep Learning, chapter 4","url":"http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/","type":"book","source":"Michael Nielsen","free":true}]}]},{"id":"pytorch","title":"PyTorch","tier":2,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["neural-networks","python-data"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"PyTorch is the most widely used Python library for building and training neural networks. It gives you GPU-accelerated tensors, automatic differentiation, and building blocks for models, data loading, and optimization.","why":"Almost all research code and most open models (on Hugging Face and elsewhere) are written in PyTorch. If you want to train, fine-tune, or read the code behind a model, you need to be fluent in it.","depth":"Must master: tensors and shapes, autograd, nn.Module, DataLoader, and writing a clean training loop by hand. Just know that torch.compile, mixed precision, and distributed training exist until you need speed or scale.","hours":35,"outcomes":["You can create and manipulate tensors, reason about shapes and broadcasting, and move work to a GPU.","You can define a model as an nn.Module and write a complete training and evaluation loop.","You can load data efficiently with Dataset and DataLoader.","You can save, load, and debug a model, including fixing common shape and device errors."],"project":"Port your from-scratch MNIST network to PyTorch, then extend it: add a proper Dataset/DataLoader, train on a GPU (Colab is fine), log train and validation loss each epoch, save the best checkpoint, and reload it to evaluate on the test set.","resources":[{"title":"Learn the Basics","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"Zero to Mastery Learn PyTorch for Deep Learning","url":"https://www.learnpytorch.io/","type":"course","source":"Daniel Bourke","free":true},{"title":"Building makemore Part 2: MLP","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TCH_1BHY58I","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"PyTorch documentation","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/index.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"Dive into Deep Learning (PyTorch edition)","url":"https://d2l.ai/","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"Tensor Puzzles","url":"https://github.com/srush/Tensor-Puzzles","type":"interactive","source":"Sasha Rush","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Tensors, shapes, and broadcasting","desc":"Tensors are n-dimensional arrays that can live on a CPU or GPU. Most bugs in deep learning code are shape bugs, so get comfortable with view, reshape, permute, and broadcasting rules.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Tensor Puzzles","url":"https://github.com/srush/Tensor-Puzzles","type":"interactive","source":"Sasha Rush","free":true}]},{"title":"Autograd","desc":"PyTorch records operations on tensors with requires_grad=True and computes gradients when you call backward(). Knowing when gradients accumulate and when to call zero_grad() avoids silent bugs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Automatic Differentiation with torch.autograd","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/autogradqs_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"nn.Module and building models","desc":"Models are classes that hold layers as attributes and define a forward method. Composing modules is how everything from an MLP to a transformer is built.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Build the Neural Network","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/buildmodel_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Datasets and DataLoaders","desc":"A Dataset returns one example; a DataLoader batches, shuffles, and loads examples in parallel worker processes. A slow data pipeline is a common reason GPUs sit idle.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Datasets & DataLoaders","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/data_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"The training loop","desc":"Forward pass, compute loss, backward pass, optimizer step, zero the gradients, repeat. Writing this by hand a few times teaches you what higher-level trainers are hiding.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Optimizing Model Parameters","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/optimization_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Saving, loading, and checkpoints","desc":"Save a model's state_dict and the optimizer state so you can resume training or ship weights. Formats like safetensors are now standard for sharing weights safely.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Saving and Loading Models","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/saving_loading_models.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"torch.compile and performance","desc":"torch.compile turns your model into optimized kernels with a one-line change, often giving large speedups. Learn it once your models are correct and you care about throughput.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Introduction to torch.compile","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/torch_compile_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]}]},{"id":"tokens-embeddings","title":"Tokens & Embeddings","tier":2,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["llm-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c","pm":"a"},"summary":"Tokens are the chunks of text a model actually reads, produced by a tokenizer such as byte-pair encoding. Embeddings are vectors that represent tokens, sentences, or documents so that similar meanings sit close together.","why":"Tokens determine cost, context limits, and odd failures like miscounting letters or weak handling of some languages. Embeddings power semantic search, RAG, clustering, recommendations, and deduplication.","depth":"Must master: how BPE tokenization works at a high level, counting tokens, what an embedding is, cosine similarity, and how to pick an embedding model. Just know Matryoshka embeddings, multi-vector retrieval, and tokenizer training details exist until a project needs them.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can explain how text becomes tokens and estimate token counts and costs.","You can explain why tokenization causes quirks like trouble with spelling, arithmetic, or some languages.","You can generate embeddings with an API or open model and compare them with cosine similarity.","You can choose an embedding model using benchmarks plus a test on your own data."],"project":"Embed 200 to 1,000 short texts you care about (notes, product reviews, FAQs) with two different embedding models. Build a tiny semantic search script, compare top results for ten test queries, and plot a 2D projection (UMAP or PCA) of the vectors colored by topic.","resources":[{"title":"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zduSFxRajkE","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Embeddings: What they are and why they matter","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2023/Oct/23/embeddings/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"The Illustrated Word2vec","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-word2vec/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course: The Tokenizers library","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter6/1","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Tiktokenizer","url":"https://tiktokenizer.vercel.app/","type":"interactive","source":"Tiktokenizer","free":true},{"title":"Vector embeddings guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/embeddings","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units (BPE)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07909","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Tokenization and BPE","desc":"Byte-pair encoding starts from bytes and repeatedly merges the most common pairs into new tokens. Common words become one token while rare words split into several pieces.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zduSFxRajkE","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Tiktokenizer","url":"https://tiktokenizer.vercel.app/","type":"interactive","source":"Tiktokenizer","free":true}]},{"title":"Counting tokens, cost, and limits","desc":"APIs price and limit by tokens, and different models use different tokenizers. Count tokens before you send big documents so you can budget and avoid truncation.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Token counting","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/token-counting","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"tiktoken","url":"https://github.com/openai/tiktoken","type":"tool","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Word embeddings and vector space","desc":"word2vec showed that training to predict nearby words yields vectors where directions carry meaning. It is the intuition behind every modern embedding.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Illustrated Word2vec","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-word2vec/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true}]},{"title":"Sentence and document embeddings","desc":"Embedding models turn whole passages into one vector for semantic search and RAG. Similarity is usually measured with cosine similarity or dot product.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Embeddings: What they are and why they matter","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2023/Oct/23/embeddings/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Sentence Transformers","url":"https://sbert.net/","type":"docs","source":"Sentence Transformers","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing an embedding model","desc":"Leaderboards compare models on retrieval, clustering, and more, but your own data is the real test. Weigh quality against dimension size, speed, languages, and price.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"MTEB Leaderboard","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/mteb/leaderboard","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Advanced embedding tricks","desc":"Matryoshka embeddings can be truncated to fewer dimensions with little quality loss, and quantized embeddings save storage. Useful at scale, safe to skip at first.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Matryoshka Representation Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13147","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"context-engineering","title":"Context Engineering","tier":2,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["prompt-engineering","llm-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","pm":"r","nc":"r","pu":"r"},"summary":"Context engineering is the practice of deciding exactly what goes into a model's context window at each step: instructions, examples, retrieved documents, tool definitions, tool results, memory, and conversation history. It extends prompt engineering from writing one good prompt to managing a whole information pipeline.","why":"Models can only use what they can see, and long, cluttered contexts degrade quality, raise cost, and slow responses. For agents that run many steps, good context management is often the difference between a system that works and one that drifts.","depth":"Must master: treating context as a limited budget, structuring system prompts, selecting and trimming retrieved content, summarizing or compacting history, and using prompt caching. Just know the research on long-context failure modes and advanced memory architectures at a conceptual level.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can design the context layout for an LLM feature: system prompt, tools, examples, retrieved data, and history.","You can explain context rot and lost-in-the-middle effects and design around them.","You can apply compaction, summarization, structured notes, and sub-agents to keep long-running tasks on track.","You can use prompt caching and token budgets to reduce cost and latency."],"project":"Take a long task (for example, answering 20 questions about a 100-page document or a multi-step research job). Build two versions: one that stuffs everything into the context, and one that retrieves only relevant chunks, keeps a running notes file, and compacts history. Compare accuracy, token usage, and cost.","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Context Engineering for Agents","url":"https://www.langchain.com/blog/context-engineering-for-agents","type":"article","source":"LangChain","free":true},{"title":"Context Rot","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot","type":"article","source":"Chroma","free":true},{"title":"Context engineering","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/27/context-engineering/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Context windows","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Context Engineering for AI Agents: Lessons from Building Manus","url":"https://manus.im/blog/Context-Engineering-for-AI-Agents-Lessons-from-Building-Manus","type":"article","source":"Manus","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Context as a finite budget","desc":"Every token competes for the model's attention, and more is not always better. The goal is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that gets the job done.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Long-context failure modes","desc":"Models often use information at the start and end of a long context better than the middle, and performance drops as inputs grow. Distracting or conflicting content can poison later steps.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Context Rot","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot","type":"article","source":"Chroma","free":true},{"title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Structuring system prompts and tools","desc":"Clear sections, the right level of specificity, and a small, well-described set of tools make model behavior more reliable. Put long documents first and the question last for long-context prompts.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Prompting best practices: long context prompting","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices#long-context-prompting","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Write, select, compress, isolate","desc":"Common strategies are to write notes outside the context, select only relevant pieces, compress history with summaries, and isolate work in sub-agents with their own clean context.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Context Engineering for Agents","url":"https://www.langchain.com/blog/context-engineering-for-agents","type":"article","source":"LangChain","free":true},{"title":"How Long Contexts Fail","url":"https://www.dbreunig.com/2025/06/22/how-contexts-fail-and-how-to-fix-them.html","type":"article","source":"Drew Breunig","free":true}]},{"title":"Prompt caching and cost","desc":"Keeping a stable prefix (system prompt, tools, large documents) lets providers cache it, which cuts cost and latency a lot. Design contexts so the stable part comes first and changes go at the end.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Prompt caching","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Memory and long-running agents","desc":"Agents that run for hours need persistent memory such as files, to-do lists, and progress notes they can reload. Lessons from production agents show how to keep the context lean over hundreds of steps.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Context Engineering for AI Agents: Lessons from Building Manus","url":"https://manus.im/blog/Context-Engineering-for-AI-Agents-Lessons-from-Building-Manus","type":"article","source":"Manus","free":true}]}]},{"id":"llm-apis","title":"LLM APIs & SDKs","tier":2,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["apis-json","prompt-engineering"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","ds":"r","nc":"r"},"summary":"LLM APIs let your code send messages to a model like Claude, GPT, or Gemini over HTTP and get text back. SDKs are the official libraries (Python, TypeScript, and more) that wrap those calls so you don't have to build raw requests.","why":"Every LLM app, from a chatbot to an agent, is built on these calls. Once you can call a model from code, you can put AI inside any product, script, or pipeline instead of copy-pasting into a chat window.","depth":"Master the message format (system prompt, user and assistant turns), key parameters, streaming, error handling, and how tokens turn into cost. Just know that batch APIs, provider routers, and prompt caching exist until you need to cut cost or latency.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can call at least two providers' APIs from code using their official SDKs and manage API keys safely.","You can build a multi-turn conversation by managing the message history yourself.","You can stream responses to a user interface and handle rate limits, timeouts, and retries.","You can estimate the token cost of a request and reduce it with prompt caching or a cheaper model."],"project":"Build a command-line chat app that keeps conversation history, streams answers, retries on rate-limit errors, and prints the token count and cost of each turn. Then add a flag that switches between two providers.","resources":[{"title":"Get started with Claude","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/get-started","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI developer quickstart","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/quickstart","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Gemini API documentation","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Claude Cookbooks","url":"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-cookbooks","type":"tool","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Cookbook","url":"https://developers.openai.com/cookbook","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Reducing latency","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The Messages format","desc":"Most APIs take a list of messages with roles (system, user, assistant) and return the next assistant message. The API is stateless: you resend the whole conversation every turn.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Messages API reference","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Parameters, models, and tokens","desc":"Pick a model tier (small and fast versus large and smart), set max output tokens, and understand temperature. Pricing is per input and output token, so long prompts and long answers both cost money.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Models overview","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Token counting","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/token-counting","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Streaming responses","desc":"Streaming sends the answer token by token using server-sent events, so users see text appear immediately instead of waiting for the full response.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Streaming messages","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Errors, rate limits, and retries","desc":"APIs limit requests and tokens per minute and sometimes fail with overload errors. Production code retries with exponential backoff and sets timeouts.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Rate limits","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Prompt caching and batch processing","desc":"Prompt caching reuses a long, repeated prefix (like a big system prompt or document) at a fraction of the cost and latency. Batch APIs process large jobs asynchronously at roughly half the price.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Prompt caching","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Batch processing","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Multi-provider gateways","desc":"Libraries and services like LiteLLM, OpenRouter, and the Vercel AI SDK give you one interface over many providers, which makes switching or falling back between models easier.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"LiteLLM docs","url":"https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/","type":"docs","source":"LiteLLM","free":true},{"title":"AI SDK introduction","url":"https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/introduction","type":"docs","source":"Vercel","free":true}]}]},{"id":"structured-outputs","title":"Structured Outputs & Tool Calling","tier":2,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["llm-apis"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","nc":"r"},"summary":"Structured outputs make a model return data in an exact shape, usually JSON matching a schema you define. Tool calling (also called function calling) lets the model ask your code to run a function, like a search or a database lookup, and then use the result.","why":"Real software needs predictable data, not free text. Structured outputs turn an LLM into a reliable extractor or classifier, and tool calling is the building block of every agent.","depth":"Master JSON Schema basics, defining tools with clear names and descriptions, and running the tool-call loop yourself. Just know how constrained decoding works under the hood; you rarely need to implement it.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can define a schema (for example with Pydantic) and get validated, typed objects back from a model.","You can write tool definitions and run the full loop: model requests a tool, your code runs it, the result goes back to the model.","You can design tool names, descriptions, and parameters that a model uses correctly.","You can handle bad or missing outputs with validation and retries."],"project":"Build an email triage script: it extracts sender, intent, urgency, and requested action into a Pydantic model, then uses two tools (look up a customer by email, create a ticket) to act on the result. Log every tool call.","resources":[{"title":"Tool use overview","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Function-calling and Data Extraction with LLMs","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/function-calling-and-data-extraction-with-llms","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Structured outputs guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Writing effective tools for agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Gemini function calling","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/function-calling","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"JSON Schema and Pydantic","desc":"A schema describes the fields, types, and required keys you expect. In Python, Pydantic models are the usual way to define a schema and validate what comes back.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Pydantic: get started","url":"https://pydantic.dev/docs/validation/latest/get-started/","type":"docs","source":"Pydantic","free":true}]},{"title":"Native structured outputs","desc":"Major APIs now accept a schema and guarantee the response matches it. This replaces fragile tricks like 'respond only in JSON' and regex parsing.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Structured outputs (Claude)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Structured outputs (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/structured-outputs","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"The tool-calling loop","desc":"You describe tools; the model replies with a tool call (name plus arguments); your code executes it and sends back a tool result; the model continues. Parallel tool calls and multi-step loops follow the same pattern.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Tool use overview","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Function calling","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/function-calling","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Designing good tools","desc":"Models pick and use tools based on names, descriptions, and parameter docs. Fewer, well-scoped tools with clear error messages and concise outputs work far better than many overlapping ones.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Writing effective tools for agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Extraction and classification patterns","desc":"Common uses: pulling fields from invoices or emails, tagging support tickets, routing requests. Libraries like Instructor add validation-driven retries on top of any provider.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Instructor","url":"https://python.useinstructor.com/","type":"tool","source":"Instructor","free":true}]},{"title":"Constrained decoding","desc":"Under the hood, structured output works by masking out tokens that would break the grammar at each step. Open-source tools like Outlines do this for local models.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Outlines","url":"https://github.com/dottxt-ai/outlines","type":"tool","source":"dottxt","free":true}]}]},{"id":"rag-fundamentals","title":"RAG Fundamentals","tier":2,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["llm-apis","tokens-embeddings"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","ds":"r","pm":"a","nc":"a"},"summary":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) means finding relevant pieces of your own documents and putting them into the model's prompt so it can answer from them. The classic pipeline is: split documents into chunks, embed them, store them, retrieve the closest chunks for a question, and generate an answer that cites them.","why":"Models don't know your company docs, recent events, or private data, and they make things up when they guess. RAG grounds answers in real sources, keeps knowledge up to date without retraining, and lets you show citations.","depth":"Master the full pipeline end to end, how embeddings and similarity search work, and how to tell whether a bad answer came from retrieval or generation. Just know that fancier techniques (reranking, hybrid search, GraphRAG) exist; they come in the next nodes once your basic pipeline works and you can measure it.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can explain the RAG pipeline and when RAG beats fine-tuning or just using a long context window.","You can build a working RAG app over your own documents with embeddings, a vector store, and an LLM.","You can write a grounded prompt that makes the model cite sources and say 'I don't know' when the context lacks the answer.","You can debug a wrong answer by checking what was retrieved before blaming the model.","You can build a small test set of questions and measure retrieval hit rate."],"project":"Build a 'chat with your docs' app over 20 to 50 real documents (your notes, a product manual, or a docs site). It must cite the chunks it used and refuse when the answer isn't present. Write 20 test questions with known source chunks and report how often the right chunk is in the top 5.","resources":[{"title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) course","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/retrieval-augmented-generation","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T-D1OfcDW1M","type":"video","source":"IBM Technology","free":true},{"title":"Learn RAG From Scratch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sVcwVQRHIc8","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp / LangChain","free":true},{"title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation series","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Embeddings","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/embeddings","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Advanced RAG on Hugging Face documentation using LangChain","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/cookbook/advanced_rag","type":"interactive","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Large Language Models: A Survey","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10997","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The RAG pipeline end to end","desc":"Indexing happens offline (load, chunk, embed, store). Querying happens live (embed the question, retrieve top-k chunks, build the prompt, generate). Knowing which stage broke is the core RAG skill.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/retrieval-augmented-generation/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Introduction to RAG","url":"https://developers.llamaindex.ai/python/framework/understanding/rag/","type":"docs","source":"LlamaIndex","free":true}]},{"title":"Embeddings for retrieval","desc":"An embedding model turns text into a vector so that similar meanings land close together. Choice of embedding model (quality, languages, dimensions, cost) has a big effect on retrieval quality; the MTEB leaderboard compares them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Choosing an Embedding Model","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/embedding-models-rundown/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"MTEB leaderboard","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/mteb/leaderboard","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Similarity search basics","desc":"Retrieval compares the query vector to stored vectors with cosine similarity or dot product and returns the top-k nearest. For a few thousand chunks a simple in-memory search is fine.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"What is Similarity Search?","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/what-is-similarity-search/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true}]},{"title":"Basic chunking","desc":"Documents are split into chunks small enough to be specific but large enough to keep meaning, often a few hundred tokens with some overlap. Start with a recursive splitter that respects paragraphs and headings.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Chunking Strategies for LLM Applications","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/chunking-strategies/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true}]},{"title":"Grounded prompting and citations","desc":"Put retrieved chunks in clearly labeled blocks, tell the model to answer only from them, cite chunk IDs, and admit when the answer isn't there. This is where hallucination control mostly happens.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"RAG vs long context vs fine-tuning","desc":"If your knowledge fits in the context window, just include it (with prompt caching). Use RAG when the corpus is large or changes often. Fine-tuning changes style and behavior, not facts.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Context Rot","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot","type":"article","source":"Chroma","free":true}]},{"title":"The original RAG paper","desc":"The 2020 paper from Facebook AI that named the technique, combining a retriever with a generator. Useful history; modern RAG is simpler and uses off-the-shelf LLMs.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"no-code-automation","title":"No-code AI Automation","tier":2,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["ai-productivity"],"roles":{"aie":"a","pm":"r","biz":"r","nc":"c","pu":"r"},"summary":"No-code AI automation means wiring AI models into everyday workflows with visual tools like Zapier, Make, n8n, and Power Automate. A trigger (new email, form, or row) kicks off steps that call an AI model, then act on the result in other apps.","why":"Many of the highest-value AI wins are boring workflows: sorting inbound email, drafting replies, summarizing calls, enriching leads. No-code tools let non-engineers ship these in hours, and engineers use them to prototype fast.","depth":"Master triggers, actions, data mapping between steps, AI steps with structured outputs, branching, and error handling. Just know that these platforms now offer AI agents and MCP connections; use them once your plain workflows are reliable.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can build a multi-step workflow with a trigger, an AI step, branching, and actions in other apps.","You can make an AI step return structured fields that later steps can use reliably.","You can add error handling, human approval steps, and logging so automations fail safely.","You can decide when a task needs a deterministic workflow, an AI agent, or custom code."],"project":"Build an inbox triage automation: when a new email or form submission arrives, an AI step extracts category, urgency, and a one-line summary; a router sends urgent items to Slack, logs everything to a spreadsheet, and drafts (but does not send) a reply for human review. Add an error alert path.","resources":[{"title":"n8n: Integrate AI","url":"https://docs.n8n.io/build/integrate-ai","type":"docs","source":"n8n","free":true},{"title":"Make Academy","url":"https://academy.make.com/","type":"course","source":"Make","free":true},{"title":"Zapier's AI tools guide","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-ai-guide/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true},{"title":"n8n workflow templates","url":"https://n8n.io/workflows/","type":"tool","source":"n8n","free":true},{"title":"AI examples (n8n)","url":"https://docs.n8n.io/build/integrate-ai/ai-examples","type":"docs","source":"n8n","free":true},{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Triggers, actions, and data mapping","desc":"Every automation starts with a trigger and passes data through actions. Learning to map fields between steps, and to test with real sample data, is most of the skill.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Get started with Zapier","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/get-started-with-zapier/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true},{"title":"Build your first workflow","url":"https://docs.n8n.io/build-your-first-workflow","type":"docs","source":"n8n","free":true}]},{"title":"AI steps that return structured data","desc":"Use AI steps to classify, extract, summarize, or draft. Ask for specific fields (category, priority, summary) instead of free text so routers and filters downstream can act on them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"What is AI automation? A complete guide","url":"https://zapier.com/blog/ai-automation/","type":"article","source":"Zapier","free":true}]},{"title":"Branching, loops, and error handling","desc":"Routers and filters send items down different paths. Error handlers, retries, and alerts stop silent failures. Add a human approval step before anything that sends email to customers or moves money.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Make Help Center","url":"https://help.make.com/","type":"docs","source":"Make","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing a platform","desc":"Zapier is the easiest with the most app integrations. Make gives visual control over complex flows. n8n is open source, self-hostable, and strongest for AI and developer-style work. Power Automate fits Microsoft 365 shops.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Power Automate documentation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true}]},{"title":"AI agents inside automation tools","desc":"Zapier Agents, Make AI Agents, and n8n's AI Agent node let a model choose which actions to take toward a goal. More flexible than fixed workflows, but less predictable, so start narrow.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Make AI Agents","url":"https://help.make.com/make-ai-agents","type":"docs","source":"Make","free":true},{"title":"Integrate AI (n8n)","url":"https://docs.n8n.io/build/integrate-ai","type":"docs","source":"n8n","free":true}]},{"title":"MCP connections","desc":"Automation platforms can expose their app integrations to AI assistants over MCP, so Claude or ChatGPT can trigger actions across thousands of apps.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Zapier MCP","url":"https://zapier.com/mcp","type":"tool","source":"Zapier","free":true}]}]},{"id":"deployment-basics","title":"Shipping AI: Deployment Basics","tier":2,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["apis-json","git-cli"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"r"},"summary":"Deployment means taking code that works on your laptop and running it somewhere other people can reach it, reliably. For AI apps that usually means wrapping a model or LLM call in a small web API, packaging it in a container, and shipping it to a cloud host with an automated pipeline.","why":"A model or prompt nobody can use has no value. The first time you ship, you discover the real problems: secrets, timeouts, cold starts, cost, and what happens when two users hit it at once.","depth":"Master one simple path end to end: a FastAPI service, a Dockerfile, environment variables for secrets, and a CI job that deploys on push. Just know that Kubernetes, service meshes, and autoscaling policies exist; you can learn them when a real workload needs them.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can wrap a model or LLM call in a FastAPI endpoint with input validation and sensible timeouts.","You can write a Dockerfile, build an image, and run it locally the same way it runs in the cloud.","You can deploy a service to a managed host and keep API keys out of your code.","You can set up a GitHub Actions workflow that runs tests and deploys on every merge.","You can explain the trade-offs between batch, real-time, and streaming inference."],"project":"Build a small FastAPI service that calls an LLM API (or a scikit-learn model) and returns a structured answer. Containerize it, add a /health endpoint, store the API key as a secret, and deploy it to Render, Fly.io, or Modal with a GitHub Actions workflow that runs a test and redeploys on every push to main.","resources":[{"title":"Made With ML: MLOps course","url":"https://madewithml.com/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true},{"title":"Lecture 5: Deployment","url":"https://fullstackdeeplearning.com/course/2022/lecture-5-deployment/","type":"video","source":"Full Stack Deep Learning","free":true},{"title":"FastAPI: Deployment concepts","url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/deployment/","type":"docs","source":"FastAPI","free":true},{"title":"Docker: Get started","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/","type":"docs","source":"Docker","free":true},{"title":"Designing Machine Learning Systems (Ch. 7: Model Deployment)","url":"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/designing-machine-learning/9781098107956/","type":"book","source":"Chip Huyen / O'Reilly","free":false},{"title":"The Twelve-Factor App","url":"https://12factor.net/","type":"article","source":"12factor.net","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Serving a model behind an API","desc":"Expose your model or LLM chain as an HTTP endpoint. Learn request and response schemas, input validation with Pydantic, async handlers, streaming responses, and health-check endpoints.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"FastAPI documentation","url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/","type":"docs","source":"FastAPI","free":true},{"title":"Model Serving","url":"https://madewithml.com/courses/mlops/serving/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true}]},{"title":"Containers with Docker","desc":"A container bundles your code, dependencies, and runtime so it behaves the same everywhere. Learn Dockerfiles, images, layers, and how to keep images small and builds fast.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Docker: Get started","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/","type":"docs","source":"Docker","free":true},{"title":"Python language-specific guide","url":"https://docs.docker.com/guides/python/","type":"docs","source":"Docker","free":true}]},{"title":"Config, secrets, and the twelve-factor app","desc":"Keep configuration in environment variables, never commit API keys, and use your host's secret manager. The twelve-factor rules are a short checklist for apps that deploy cleanly.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Twelve-Factor App","url":"https://12factor.net/","type":"article","source":"12factor.net","free":true},{"title":"Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true}]},{"title":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","desc":"Continuous integration runs your tests on every change. Continuous deployment ships the change automatically once tests pass. This is what makes deploying boring, which is the goal.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"GitHub Actions quickstart","url":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/get-started/quickstart","type":"docs","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing where to host","desc":"Options range from demo hosts (Hugging Face Spaces, Streamlit, Gradio) to app platforms (Render, Fly.io), serverless GPU platforms (Modal), and the big clouds. Pick based on whether you need a GPU, how spiky traffic is, and how much ops work you want.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Modal documentation","url":"https://modal.com/docs","type":"docs","source":"Modal","free":true},{"title":"Gradio quickstart","url":"https://gradio.app/guides/quickstart","type":"docs","source":"Gradio","free":true}]},{"title":"Batch vs real-time vs streaming inference","desc":"Batch jobs score many inputs on a schedule and are cheap. Real-time APIs answer one request at a time with low latency. Streaming sends tokens as they are generated, which matters for LLM chat UX.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Lecture 5: Deployment","url":"https://fullstackdeeplearning.com/course/2022/lecture-5-deployment/","type":"video","source":"Full Stack Deep Learning","free":true}]},{"title":"Safe rollouts: canaries and rollbacks","desc":"Release a new version to a small slice of traffic first, watch the metrics, then expand or roll back. This matters even more with AI, where a new prompt or model can quietly change behavior.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Canary Release","url":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CanaryRelease.html","type":"article","source":"Martin Fowler","free":true},{"title":"Kubernetes basics","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/","type":"interactive","source":"Kubernetes","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-adoption","title":"Leading AI Adoption & Change","tier":3,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-strategy"],"roles":{"pm":"r","biz":"c"},"summary":"Leading AI adoption means getting people to actually change how they work with AI: building skills, setting policy, finding champions, redesigning processes, and handling fears about jobs and quality.","why":"Buying licenses is easy; getting real use is hard. Many organizations see low adoption and little value because they treat AI as an IT rollout instead of a change in how people work.","depth":"Master the practical levers: clear policy, training by role, champions, shared wins, and measuring usage and outcomes. Know classic change-management models so you can use their language, but adapt them because AI changes fast and spreads bottom-up.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can write a clear, permission-giving AI acceptable-use policy.","You can design a training and champions program for different roles.","You can address employee concerns about job security and quality honestly.","You can measure adoption and impact and report it to leadership.","You can redesign a team process around AI, not just add AI on top."],"project":"Create a 90-day adoption plan for one team: a one-page acceptable-use policy, two role-specific training sessions built on the team's real tasks, a champions group with a channel for sharing wins, and a simple dashboard of three metrics (active usage, time saved, one quality measure). Run the first 30 days and write up what you learned.","resources":[{"title":"Making AI Work: Leadership, Lab, and Crowd","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/making-ai-work-leadership-lab-and","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"AI in the Enterprise","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/ai-in-the-enterprise.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Work Trend Index","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index","type":"article","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"The 8-Step Process for Leading Change","url":"https://www.kotterinc.com/methodology/8-steps/","type":"article","source":"Kotter","free":true},{"title":"The Prosci ADKAR Model","url":"https://www.prosci.com/methodology/adkar","type":"article","source":"Prosci","free":true},{"title":"Co-Intelligence: Living and Working with AI","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/741805/co-intelligence-by-ethan-mollick/","type":"book","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Leadership, lab, and crowd","desc":"Successful adoption needs leaders who set direction and incentives, a small team that experiments and builds, and the whole workforce sharing what works. Each part fails without the others.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Making AI Work: Leadership, Lab, and Crowd","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/making-ai-work-leadership-lab-and","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Acceptable-use policy","desc":"Say which tools are approved, what data can and can't go in, when to disclose AI use, and who checks outputs. Policies that only say 'no' push people to hidden, riskier use.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Training by role","desc":"Generic 'intro to AI' sessions rarely change behavior. Hands-on training built around each team's real tasks, followed by practice and office hours, does.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"AI Fluency: Framework & Foundations","url":"https://anthropic.skilljar.com/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","type":"course","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Change management basics","desc":"Models like Kotter's 8 steps and Prosci's ADKAR (Awareness, Desire, Knowledge, Ability, Reinforcement) give a checklist for leading change. Use them to make sure you've covered motivation and reinforcement, not just tools.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The Prosci ADKAR Model","url":"https://www.prosci.com/methodology/adkar","type":"article","source":"Prosci","free":true}]},{"title":"Jobs, fears, and incentives","desc":"People hide AI use if they fear being replaced or judged. Be honest about plans, reward people who share time-saving discoveries, and decide in advance what happens to freed-up time.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Measuring adoption and impact","desc":"Track active users and usage depth, but also business outcomes like cycle time and quality. High usage with no outcome change is a signal to redesign workflows.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Work Trend Index","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index","type":"article","source":"Microsoft","free":true}]},{"title":"AI as a teammate","desc":"Research shows people working with AI can match the output of small teams. That raises bigger questions about team structure and roles worth exploring once basics are in place.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"The Cybernetic Teammate","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/the-cybernetic-teammate","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]}]},{"id":"build-vs-buy","title":"Build vs Buy & Vendor Selection","tier":3,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-strategy"],"roles":{"aie":"a","pm":"c","biz":"c"},"summary":"Build vs. buy is the decision between buying an off-the-shelf AI product, configuring a platform, or building your own solution on model APIs. Vendor selection is how you compare and choose the right provider safely.","why":"Building what you could buy wastes months; buying what should be core to your business can leave you dependent and undifferentiated. Picking the wrong vendor can expose your data or lock you in.","depth":"Master a decision framework and a vendor checklist covering data handling, security, pricing, and exit options. You don't need to be able to build AI systems; you do need to know roughly what building involves so you can judge quotes and claims.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can decide whether to buy, configure, or build for a given use case and justify it.","You can run a structured vendor evaluation with a scorecard and a hands-on trial on your own data.","You can check a vendor's data use, security, and compliance claims.","You can estimate total cost of ownership, including usage-based pricing.","You can plan to avoid lock-in."],"project":"For one real use case, compare three options: an off-the-shelf product, a configured platform, and a custom build. Create a scorecard covering fit, quality on 10 of your own test cases, data handling, security, 12-month total cost, and exit plan. Run trials of the top two and write a one-page recommendation.","resources":[{"title":"How 100 Enterprise CIOs Are Building and Buying Gen AI in 2025","url":"https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/","type":"article","source":"Andreessen Horowitz","free":true},{"title":"2025: The State of Generative AI in the Enterprise","url":"https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-the-state-of-generative-ai-in-the-enterprise/","type":"article","source":"Menlo Ventures","free":true},{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true},{"title":"What We've Learned From A Year of Building with LLMs","url":"https://applied-llms.org/","type":"article","source":"Yan, Bischof, Frye, Husain, Liu & Shankar","free":true},{"title":"Building A Generative AI Platform","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The spectrum: buy, configure, build","desc":"Buy a finished product (an AI support desk). Configure a platform (custom assistants, no-code automations). Build on APIs (your own app using model APIs). Most companies use all three for different needs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"How 100 Enterprise CIOs Are Building and Buying Gen AI in 2025","url":"https://a16z.com/ai-enterprise-2025/","type":"article","source":"Andreessen Horowitz","free":true}]},{"title":"When to build","desc":"Build when the use case is core to your competitive advantage, uses proprietary data or processes, and no product fits. Buy when the need is common and speed matters more than differentiation.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Vendor evaluation and trials","desc":"Score vendors on fit, quality on your own test cases, integration, pricing, support, and viability. Always run a trial on real (safe) data; demos are tuned to look good.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Data, security, and compliance checks","desc":"Ask: is our data used for training? Where is it stored and for how long? What certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001) and controls exist? Trust centers and privacy pages answer most of this.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true}]},{"title":"Pricing and total cost of ownership","desc":"Compare seat-based and usage-based pricing at realistic volumes. Include integration, maintenance, staff time, and the cost of switching later.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Avoiding lock-in","desc":"Prefer vendors that let you export data, swap underlying models, and use open standards like MCP. Model quality and prices change fast, so flexibility has real value.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"What building really involves","desc":"A real AI app needs prompts, retrieval over your data, evals, monitoring, and security work, not just an API call. Knowing this helps you judge build estimates and agency quotes.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Building A Generative AI Platform","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-security","title":"Prompt Injection & AI Security","tier":3,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["structured-outputs","ai-privacy"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","pm":"r","biz":"a","nc":"r"},"summary":"AI security is about attacks that target AI systems. The biggest one is prompt injection: hidden instructions in a web page, email, or file that hijack a model into doing what the attacker wants.","why":"Once a model can read untrusted content and take actions (send email, call APIs, run code), a single poisoned document can leak data or cause damage. No model is immune, so the defense has to be in your system design.","depth":"AI engineers must master prompt injection, the \"lethal trifecta,\" and design patterns that limit damage, plus the OWASP LLM Top 10. PMs and no-code builders need to recognize risky setups and ask for guardrails. Adversarial ML attacks like data poisoning and model extraction are 'know it exists' unless you train models.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can explain direct vs indirect prompt injection and why filtering alone can't fix it.","You can spot the lethal trifecta (private data + untrusted content + a way to send data out) in an agent design.","You can apply least privilege, human approval, and isolation patterns to an LLM app or agent.","You can walk through the OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications and map each risk to your own app.","You can review an MCP server or tool integration for security risks before installing it."],"project":"Build a small agent that reads a web page or email and can call one tool (for example, \"send a message\"). Plant a hidden instruction in the input that tries to exfiltrate data, and show that it works. Then fix it with design changes (remove the exfiltration path, add human approval, or split into a quarantined reader model) and show the attack fails. Write up which OWASP risks you addressed.","resources":[{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true},{"title":"The lethal trifecta for AI agents","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Design Patterns for Securing LLM Agents against Prompt Injections","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08837","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Lakera prompt injection games (Gandalf, Agent Breaker)","url":"https://gandalf.lakera.ai/","type":"interactive","source":"Lakera","free":true},{"title":"Red Teaming LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/red-teaming-llm-applications","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"MCP Security Best Practices","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tutorials/security/security_best_practices","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"MITRE ATLAS","url":"https://atlas.mitre.org/","type":"docs","source":"MITRE","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Prompt injection: direct and indirect","desc":"Direct injection is a user typing \"ignore your instructions.\" Indirect injection hides instructions in content the model reads, like a web page or email, and is far more dangerous for agents.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Simon Willison on prompt injection","url":"https://simonwillison.net/tags/prompt-injection/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"Not what you've signed up for (indirect prompt injection)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"The lethal trifecta","desc":"An agent with access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and a way to communicate externally can be tricked into leaking that data. Remove one of the three to break the attack.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The lethal trifecta for AI agents","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}]},{"title":"Secure design patterns","desc":"Since models can't reliably tell instructions from data, constrain what they can do: least-privilege tools, human approval for risky actions, plan-then-execute, and separating a privileged model from one that reads untrusted text.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Design Patterns for Securing LLM Agents against Prompt Injections","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08837","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Defeating Prompt Injections by Design (CaMeL)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18813","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLMs and agents","desc":"A shared checklist of the main risks: injection, sensitive data leaks, supply chain, excessive agency, improper output handling, and more. There is a separate list for agentic apps.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true},{"title":"OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications for 2026","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/","type":"docs","source":"OWASP","free":true}]},{"title":"Tool, MCP, and output security","desc":"Treat model output as untrusted input: never pass it straight into SQL, shell commands, or HTML. Vet MCP servers and plugins like any third-party code, and watch for tool descriptions that carry hidden instructions.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"MCP Security Best Practices","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tutorials/security/security_best_practices","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true}]},{"title":"Jailbreaks vs prompt injection","desc":"A jailbreak tricks the model into breaking its own safety rules. Prompt injection hijacks your app. They overlap in technique but the risks and fixes differ.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Adversarial ML: poisoning, extraction, evasion","desc":"Attackers can poison training data, extract model weights or behavior through queries, or craft inputs that fool classifiers. These matter most if you train or host models.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"NIST AI 100-2: Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ai/100/2/e2025/final","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"MITRE ATLAS","url":"https://atlas.mitre.org/","type":"docs","source":"MITRE","free":true}]}]},{"id":"reading-papers","title":"Reading ML Papers","tier":3,"lane":"foundations","prereqs":["linear-algebra","probability-stats","neural-networks"],"roles":{"aie":"a","mle":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"Reading ML papers is the skill of getting the key idea out of a research paper quickly, judging whether the evidence supports it, and knowing when a paper deserves a deeper read.","why":"AI moves faster than textbooks and courses. New methods appear on arXiv first, and being able to read them directly keeps you current and lets you judge hype for yourself.","depth":"Researchers must master full critical reading: method, math, experimental design, baselines, and reproducing results. ML engineers should be able to read the method and results sections well enough to implement or apply an idea. AI engineers just need to skim abstracts, figures, and conclusions and know which sources to trust; a good blog summary is often enough.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can use a three-pass approach to read a paper in 15 minutes, 1 hour, or deeply.","You can find a paper's main claim, the evidence for it, and its weakest point.","You can spot common problems: weak baselines, cherry-picked examples, missing error bars, and test-set leakage.","You can find, track, and organize papers using arXiv, Hugging Face Papers, and citation tools.","You can reimplement a key result from a short paper."],"project":"Pick three papers: one classic (Attention Is All You Need), one from the past year, and one in your area. Do a first pass on each and write a five-line summary: claim, method, evidence, weakest point, and whether it's worth a deeper read. Then take the shortest one and reproduce one key figure or table at small scale, noting anything the paper left out.","resources":[{"title":"How to Read a Paper (S. Keshav)","url":"http://ccr.sigcomm.org/online/files/p83-keshavA.pdf","type":"paper","source":"ACM SIGCOMM","free":true},{"title":"The Annotated Transformer","url":"https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/annotated-transformer/","type":"article","source":"Harvard NLP","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Papers","url":"https://huggingface.co/papers","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"arXiv","url":"https://arxiv.org/","type":"tool","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Lil'Log","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Yannic Kilcher (paper walkthroughs)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@YannicKilcher","type":"video","source":"Yannic Kilcher","free":true},{"title":"Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/","type":"tool","source":"Allen Institute for AI","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The three-pass method","desc":"First pass: title, abstract, figures, conclusion. Second pass: the method and results, skipping proofs. Third pass: rebuild the argument yourself. Most papers only deserve the first pass.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"How to Read a Paper (S. Keshav)","url":"http://ccr.sigcomm.org/online/files/p83-keshavA.pdf","type":"paper","source":"ACM SIGCOMM","free":true}]},{"title":"Anatomy of an ML paper","desc":"Know what each section is for: related work places the idea, the method defines it, ablations show what matters, and the appendix often holds the details that make it work.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Annotated Transformer","url":"https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/annotated-transformer/","type":"article","source":"Harvard NLP","free":true}]},{"title":"Reading critically","desc":"Ask whether baselines are fair and tuned, whether results include variance, whether benchmarks might be contaminated, and whether the claim generalizes beyond the tested setting.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Adding Error Bars to Evals","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00640","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true}]},{"title":"Finding and tracking papers","desc":"Use arXiv listings, Hugging Face daily papers, and lab blogs to stay current. Citation graph tools help you find the earlier work a paper builds on.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Hugging Face Papers","url":"https://huggingface.co/papers","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Connected Papers","url":"https://www.connectedpapers.com/","type":"tool","source":"Connected Papers","free":true}]},{"title":"Reading the math","desc":"Learn common notation and read equations by asking what goes in, what comes out, and what is being minimized. Map each equation to a few lines of code.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Reproducing results","desc":"Reimplementing a paper is the deepest way to understand it and often reveals details the text leaves out.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Using AI to read papers","desc":"Ask an LLM to explain a section, define terms, or quiz you, and use tools like alphaXiv to discuss a paper line by line. Always check claims against the paper itself.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"alphaXiv","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/","type":"tool","source":"alphaXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"cnn","title":"Computer Vision & CNNs","tier":3,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["pytorch"],"roles":{"mle":"r","ds":"a","rs":"c"},"summary":"Computer vision is about getting models to understand images and video. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) slide small learned filters across an image to detect edges, textures, and eventually whole objects, and vision transformers (ViTs) now compete with them.","why":"Vision powers document processing, medical imaging, quality inspection, self-driving, and the image side of multimodal LLMs. CNNs also teach ideas like inductive bias, feature hierarchies, and transfer learning that apply across deep learning.","depth":"Must master: how a convolution works, what pooling and stride do, the ResNet idea of residual connections, and how to fine-tune a pretrained backbone. Just know the landscape of detection (YOLO), segmentation (U-Net, SAM), and ViTs unless you specialize in vision.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can explain how convolution, padding, stride, and pooling change a tensor's shape and what each layer learns.","You can describe why residual connections let networks go much deeper.","You can fine-tune a pretrained image model on a small custom dataset with data augmentation.","You can choose between classification, detection, and segmentation for a given vision problem."],"project":"Collect or download a small image dataset (300 to 1,000 images across 3 to 5 classes). Train a small CNN from scratch, then fine-tune a pretrained ResNet or ViT from timm on the same data, and compare accuracy, training time, and a confusion matrix.","resources":[{"title":"CS231n: Deep Learning for Computer Vision","url":"https://cs231n.stanford.edu/","type":"course","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"CNN Explainer","url":"https://poloclub.github.io/cnn-explainer/","type":"interactive","source":"Georgia Tech Polo Club","free":true},{"title":"Practical Deep Learning for Coders","url":"https://course.fast.ai/","type":"course","source":"fast.ai","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Convolutional Neural Networks","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-neural-networks/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Computer Vision Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/computer-vision-course/unit0/welcome/welcome","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Convolutions and feature maps","desc":"A convolution layer applies the same small filter at every position, so it detects a pattern wherever it appears. Stacking layers builds from edges to textures to parts to objects.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"CNN Explainer","url":"https://poloclub.github.io/cnn-explainer/","type":"interactive","source":"Georgia Tech Polo Club","free":true},{"title":"CS231n: Convolutional Neural Networks","url":"https://cs231n.github.io/convolutional-networks/","type":"article","source":"Stanford","free":true}]},{"title":"Classic architectures and ResNets","desc":"LeNet, AlexNet, VGG, and ResNet show how the field learned to build deeper, better networks. ResNet's skip connections are now used in nearly every deep architecture, including transformers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Modern Convolutional Neural Networks","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-modern/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Transfer learning and augmentation","desc":"Start from a model pretrained on a large dataset and fine-tune it on yours. Augmentations like flips, crops, and color jitter stretch a small dataset much further.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Transfer Learning for Computer Vision Tutorial","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/transfer_learning_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"timm documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/timm/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Detection and segmentation","desc":"Detection draws boxes around objects; segmentation labels every pixel. U-Net, the YOLO family, and promptable models like Segment Anything are the common starting points.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Ultralytics YOLO docs","url":"https://docs.ultralytics.com/","type":"docs","source":"Ultralytics","free":true},{"title":"Segment Anything","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02643","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Vision transformers","desc":"ViTs cut an image into patches and treat them like tokens in a transformer. With enough data they match or beat CNNs, and they are the usual image encoder inside multimodal models.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"An Image is Worth 16x16 Words","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11929","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"What CNNs see: feature visualization","desc":"Visualizing what neurons respond to shows the feature hierarchy directly. It is also an early form of interpretability research.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Feature Visualization","url":"https://distill.pub/2017/feature-visualization/","type":"article","source":"Distill","free":true}]}]},{"id":"sequence-models","title":"Sequence Models: RNNs to Attention","tier":3,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["pytorch"],"roles":{"mle":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"Sequence models process ordered data like text, audio, and time series. This node follows the path from recurrent networks (RNNs, LSTMs) through encoder-decoder models to the attention mechanism that made transformers possible.","why":"Understanding why RNNs struggled (slow sequential processing, vanishing gradients, a fixed-size memory) explains why attention won. The history also matters again, since state space models like Mamba bring recurrence back for long contexts.","depth":"Must master: how an RNN carries a hidden state, why gradients vanish over long sequences, the idea of seq2seq, and what attention computes. Just know LSTM gate equations and state space models at a conceptual level unless you research architectures.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can explain how an RNN processes a sequence step by step and why that is hard to parallelize.","You can describe the vanishing gradient problem and how LSTMs and GRUs ease it.","You can explain encoder-decoder models and how attention removes the fixed-size bottleneck.","You can train a small character-level language model in PyTorch."],"project":"Train a character-level language model on a text corpus you like (song lyrics, a book, your own notes) three ways: a bigram model, an LSTM, and a small single-layer attention model. Compare validation loss and the quality of generated samples.","resources":[{"title":"Neural Networks: Zero to Hero (makemore lectures)","url":"https://karpathy.ai/zero-to-hero.html","type":"course","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Understanding LSTM Networks","url":"https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-08-Understanding-LSTMs/","type":"article","source":"Chris Olah","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Recurrent Neural Networks","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_recurrent-neural-networks/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Attention Mechanisms and Transformers","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_attention-mechanisms-and-transformers/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"CS224N: Natural Language Processing with Deep Learning","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs224n/","type":"course","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural Networks","url":"https://karpathy.github.io/2015/05/21/rnn-effectiveness/","type":"article","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Language modeling as next-token prediction","desc":"A language model predicts the next character or token given the ones before it. Starting with simple bigram and MLP models makes the later jump to RNNs and transformers easy to follow.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The spelled-out intro to language modeling: building makemore","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PaCmpygFfXo","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Recurrent neural networks","desc":"An RNN reads one step at a time and updates a hidden state that summarizes everything so far. It is simple and elegant, but training is slow because each step waits for the last.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural Networks","url":"https://karpathy.github.io/2015/05/21/rnn-effectiveness/","type":"article","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"LSTMs, GRUs, and vanishing gradients","desc":"Gradients shrink as they flow back through many steps, so plain RNNs forget long-range information. LSTMs and GRUs add gates that decide what to keep, which helped a lot but did not fully fix it.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Understanding LSTM Networks","url":"https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-08-Understanding-LSTMs/","type":"article","source":"Chris Olah","free":true}]},{"title":"Seq2seq and encoder-decoder models","desc":"One network encodes the input sequence and another decodes the output, as in translation. Squeezing a whole sentence into one vector was the bottleneck that attention was invented to fix.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.3215","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"The attention mechanism","desc":"Attention lets the decoder look back at every encoder state and weight the relevant ones. Transformers keep attention and drop recurrence entirely.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Visualizing A Neural Machine Translation Model (Mechanics of Seq2seq Models With Attention)","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/visualizing-neural-machine-translation-mechanics-of-seq2seq-models-with-attention/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true},{"title":"Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0473","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"State space models and modern recurrence","desc":"Models like Mamba process sequences in linear time with a learned recurrent state, which helps for very long inputs. Several 2025 and 2026 models mix these layers with attention in hybrid designs.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Mamba: Linear-Time Sequence Modeling with Selective State Spaces","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.00752","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"dl-training","title":"Training Deep Nets Well","tier":3,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["pytorch"],"roles":{"mle":"c","rs":"c"},"summary":"Training deep networks well is the practical craft of getting a model to actually learn: choosing optimizers and learning rates, normalizing, initializing, regularizing, and debugging when the loss misbehaves.","why":"A correct architecture can still fail to train, train slowly, or overfit. These skills separate a model that works from one that silently underperforms, and they carry over directly to fine-tuning and pretraining LLMs.","depth":"Must master: AdamW, learning rate schedules with warmup, normalization layers, a systematic debugging routine, and reading loss curves. Just know newer optimizers like Muon and the details of mixed precision until you need the extra speed.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can pick an optimizer, learning rate, and schedule and justify the choice.","You can diagnose common failures (exploding or stuck loss, dead activations, overfitting) from metrics and activation statistics.","You can apply normalization, initialization, dropout, and weight decay correctly.","You can run a disciplined experiment loop with tracking, baselines, and one change at a time."],"project":"Take a small image or text model and run a controlled study: a learning rate sweep, with and without warmup, with and without normalization, and SGD versus AdamW. Log every run, plot the curves side by side, and write a one-page summary of what mattered most.","resources":[{"title":"A Recipe for Training Neural Networks","url":"https://karpathy.github.io/2019/04/25/recipe/","type":"article","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Deep Learning Tuning Playbook","url":"https://github.com/google-research/tuning_playbook","type":"article","source":"Google Research","free":true},{"title":"Building makemore Part 3: Activations & Gradients, BatchNorm","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P6sfmUTpUmc","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"CS231n: Neural Networks Part 3 (learning and evaluation)","url":"https://cs231n.github.io/neural-networks-3/","type":"article","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Optimization Algorithms","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_optimization/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Engineering Open Book","url":"https://github.com/stas00/ml-engineering","type":"book","source":"Stas Bekman","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Optimizers and learning rate schedules","desc":"AdamW is the default for most deep learning today. The learning rate is the most important hyperparameter, and warmup followed by cosine or linear decay is a common schedule.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization (AdamW)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05101","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"d2l.ai: Optimization Algorithms","url":"https://d2l.ai/chapter_optimization/index.html","type":"book","source":"d2l.ai","free":true}]},{"title":"Initialization and normalization","desc":"Good initialization keeps activations and gradients at a sensible scale. BatchNorm, LayerNorm, and RMSNorm keep them there during training, which makes deep networks far easier to train.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Building makemore Part 3: Activations & Gradients, BatchNorm","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P6sfmUTpUmc","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Layer Normalization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.06450","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Regularization","desc":"Weight decay, dropout, data augmentation, and early stopping all fight overfitting. Large models trained on huge datasets often need less of it than small models on small data.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v15/srivastava14a.html","type":"paper","source":"JMLR","free":true}]},{"title":"Debugging training runs","desc":"Overfit a single batch first, check the loss at initialization, and watch gradient norms. Most failures come from data bugs, wrong learning rates, or shape mistakes, not the architecture.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Recipe for Training Neural Networks","url":"https://karpathy.github.io/2019/04/25/recipe/","type":"article","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Hyperparameter tuning and experiment tracking","desc":"Tune the learning rate first, use a fixed budget per trial, and log every run with its config. Tools like Weights & Biases, MLflow, or TensorBoard make comparisons honest.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Deep Learning Tuning Playbook","url":"https://github.com/google-research/tuning_playbook","type":"article","source":"Google Research","free":true}]},{"title":"Mixed precision and speed","desc":"Training in bf16 or fp16 roughly halves memory and speeds up modern GPUs. Gradient accumulation and checkpointing let you fit bigger effective batches and models.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Automatic Mixed Precision package","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/amp.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Newer optimizers","desc":"Optimizers like Muon, which orthogonalizes updates for weight matrices, have shown faster training in speedrun benchmarks and some production LLMs. Worth knowing about, safe to skip at first.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Muon: An optimizer for hidden layers in neural networks","url":"https://kellerjordan.github.io/posts/muon/","type":"article","source":"Keller Jordan","free":true}]}]},{"id":"transformers","title":"Transformer Architecture","tier":3,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["tokens-embeddings","neural-networks"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"The transformer is the neural network architecture behind modern LLMs. It processes all tokens in parallel and uses self-attention so every token can pull in information from every other token, stacked with feed-forward layers, residual connections, and normalization.","why":"Knowing the transformer lets you read model cards and papers, reason about context length, memory, and speed, and understand fine-tuning, inference optimization, and interpretability. It is the single most important architecture in current AI.","depth":"Must master: queries, keys, and values; multi-head attention; causal masking; positional information; the residual stream; and the decoder-only block. You should be able to implement a small GPT. Just know the zoo of modern variants (RoPE, GQA, MoE, FlashAttention) well enough to read a model card.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can explain self-attention with queries, keys, and values and compute it by hand for a tiny example.","You can draw a decoder-only transformer block and explain what each part does.","You can implement and train a small GPT-style model from scratch in PyTorch.","You can read a modern model's architecture description and explain choices like RoPE, grouped-query attention, and mixture of experts."],"project":"Implement a small GPT (about 10M parameters) from scratch in PyTorch, train it on a text dataset such as TinyShakespeare or TinyStories, and generate samples. Then add one modern change (RoPE or grouped-query attention) and compare validation loss and speed.","resources":[{"title":"The Illustrated Transformer","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true},{"title":"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"Attention in transformers, step-by-step","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eMlx5fFNoYc","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"Transformer Explainer","url":"https://poloclub.github.io/transformer-explainer/","type":"interactive","source":"Georgia Tech Polo Club","free":true},{"title":"Attention Is All You Need","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch) code repository","url":"https://github.com/rasbt/LLMs-from-scratch","type":"book","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true},{"title":"LLM Visualization","url":"https://bbycroft.net/llm","type":"interactive","source":"Brendan Bycroft","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Self-attention: queries, keys, and values","desc":"Each token makes a query, compares it with every other token's key, and uses the resulting weights to mix their values. This lets meaning flow between tokens regardless of distance.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Attention in transformers, step-by-step","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eMlx5fFNoYc","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true},{"title":"The Illustrated Transformer","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true}]},{"title":"Multi-head attention and masking","desc":"Several attention heads run in parallel, each free to track a different kind of relationship. A causal mask stops tokens from seeing the future, which is what makes next-token training work.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"The block: MLPs, residuals, and normalization","desc":"Each block pairs attention with a feed-forward network, wrapped in residual connections and normalization. The residual stream is a running sum that every layer reads from and writes to.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Transformers, the tech behind LLMs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wjZofJX0v4M","type":"video","source":"3Blue1Brown","free":true}]},{"title":"Building a GPT from scratch","desc":"Writing a small decoder-only model end to end is the best way to make every piece concrete. Karpathy's walkthrough and nanoGPT are the standard path.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"nanoGPT","url":"https://github.com/karpathy/nanoGPT","type":"tool","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Encoder, decoder, and encoder-decoder","desc":"The original transformer had both an encoder and a decoder. BERT-style encoders are still used for classification and embeddings, while generative LLMs are almost all decoder-only.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course: How do Transformers work?","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter1/4","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Modern variants","desc":"Today's models use rotary position embeddings (RoPE), grouped-query attention to shrink the KV cache, RMSNorm, SwiGLU activations, and often mixture-of-experts layers. FlashAttention computes attention faster without changing the math.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The Big LLM Architecture Comparison","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/the-big-llm-architecture-comparison","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true},{"title":"FlashAttention","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Transformers as circuits","desc":"Viewing attention heads and MLPs as components that read and write the residual stream is the foundation of mechanistic interpretability.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2021/framework/index.html","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]}]},{"id":"fine-tuning","title":"Fine-tuning LLMs (LoRA, PEFT)","tier":3,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["transformers","pytorch"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","rs":"c"},"summary":"Fine-tuning continues training a pretrained model on your own examples so it adopts a format, style, or skill. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods like LoRA train only small adapter weights, which makes this affordable on a single GPU.","why":"Fine-tuning is the right tool when prompting and retrieval are not enough: enforcing a strict output format, teaching a narrow task cheaply with a small model, matching a voice, or distilling a big model's behavior into a smaller one. It is often the wrong tool for adding fresh facts, where RAG is better.","depth":"Must master: when to fine-tune versus prompt or use RAG, preparing chat-formatted data, supervised fine-tuning with LoRA or QLoRA, and evaluating against a baseline. Just know full fine-tuning, distillation pipelines, and the details of adapter variants until you need them.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can decide whether a problem needs prompting, RAG, or fine-tuning, and justify it.","You can build a clean instruction dataset in the right chat template.","You can fine-tune an open model with LoRA or QLoRA using TRL, PEFT, or Unsloth.","You can evaluate the tuned model against the base model and catch overfitting or lost general skills."],"project":"Pick a narrow task (for example, turning messy support emails into a fixed JSON schema). Build 500 or more examples, QLoRA-fine-tune a small open model (1B to 8B) on a free or rented GPU, and compare it to the base model with a good prompt on 50 held-out examples for accuracy, format validity, and latency.","resources":[{"title":"Hugging Face LLM Course: Fine-tuning LLMs","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/llm-course/chapter11/1","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Practical Tips for Finetuning LLMs Using LoRA","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/practical-tips-for-finetuning-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true},{"title":"Unsloth documentation","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs","type":"docs","source":"Unsloth","free":true},{"title":"PEFT documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Finetuning Large Language Models","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/finetuning-large-language-models","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"LoRA Without Regret","url":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","type":"article","source":"Thinking Machines Lab","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"When to fine-tune (and when not to)","desc":"Fine-tuning changes behavior and format well but is a poor way to inject knowledge that changes. Try strong prompts and retrieval first, and fine-tune when you have a clear, repeated task and good examples.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Practical Tips for Finetuning LLMs Using LoRA","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/practical-tips-for-finetuning-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true}]},{"title":"Data preparation and chat templates","desc":"A few hundred to a few thousand high-quality examples often beat a large noisy set. Format them with the model's exact chat template, and hold out a test set.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"LoRA and QLoRA","desc":"LoRA freezes the original weights and learns small low-rank update matrices. QLoRA does the same on a 4-bit quantized base model, letting you tune larger models on a single consumer GPU.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Supervised fine-tuning tooling","desc":"Hugging Face TRL and PEFT are the standard libraries; Unsloth and Axolotl wrap them for speed and convenience. Hosted fine-tuning APIs are an option when you do not want to manage GPUs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"TRL: SFT Trainer","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/sft_trainer","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Unsloth documentation","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs","type":"docs","source":"Unsloth","free":true}]},{"title":"Hyperparameters and evaluation","desc":"Rank, learning rate, and number of epochs matter most. Always compare to the base model on a held-out set and spot-check general abilities to catch forgetting.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"LoRA Without Regret","url":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","type":"article","source":"Thinking Machines Lab","free":true}]},{"title":"Distillation","desc":"Use a large model to generate outputs, then fine-tune a small model to imitate them. It is how many fast, cheap production models are made.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.02531","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Merging and serving adapters","desc":"LoRA adapters can be merged into the base weights or swapped at serving time, so one base model can host many fine-tunes.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"PEFT documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]}]},{"id":"open-models","title":"Running Open Models Locally","tier":3,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["tokens-embeddings","llm-apis"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","rs":"r","pu":"a"},"summary":"Open-weight models are LLMs whose weights you can download and run yourself, such as the Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma, DeepSeek, and gpt-oss families. Tools like Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, and vLLM make it practical to run them on a laptop or your own server.","why":"Running models yourself gives you privacy, offline use, no per-token bills, full control over versions, and the ability to fine-tune. It is also the best way to understand quantization, memory limits, and inference speed firsthand.","depth":"Must master: estimating memory from parameter count and quantization, running a model with Ollama or llama.cpp, using an OpenAI-compatible local endpoint, and reading model cards and licenses. Just know serving engines like vLLM and SGLang and the details of quantization formats until you deploy at scale.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can estimate whether a given model and quantization will fit on your hardware.","You can run open models locally with Ollama, LM Studio, or llama.cpp and call them from code.","You can explain quantization formats like GGUF and their quality and speed trade-offs.","You can compare open models using benchmarks, licenses, and your own test prompts."],"project":"Run three open models of different sizes locally (for example 1B to 4B, 7B to 8B, and the largest that fits) at two quantization levels each. Measure memory use and tokens per second, score them on ten of your own prompts, and point a small script at the local OpenAI-compatible endpoint to answer the same questions.","resources":[{"title":"Ollama","url":"https://ollama.com/","type":"tool","source":"Ollama","free":true},{"title":"LM Studio","url":"https://lmstudio.ai/","type":"tool","source":"LM Studio","free":true},{"title":"llama.cpp","url":"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp","type":"tool","source":"ggml-org","free":true},{"title":"A Visual Guide to Quantization","url":"https://newsletter.maartengrootendorst.com/p/a-visual-guide-to-quantization","type":"article","source":"Maarten Grootendorst","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Transformers documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"LLM command-line tool","url":"https://llm.datasette.io/en/stable/","type":"tool","source":"Simon Willison","free":true},{"title":"vLLM documentation","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The open model landscape","desc":"Open-weight families are released in many sizes, from under 1B to hundreds of billions of parameters, often as mixture-of-experts. 'Open weights' is not the same as open source, so read the license and what data and code were released.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Hugging Face model hub","url":"https://huggingface.co/models","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"The Open Source AI Definition","url":"https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-definition","type":"article","source":"Open Source Initiative","free":true}]},{"title":"Memory math and hardware","desc":"A rough rule: weight memory is parameters times bytes per parameter, so an 8B model needs about 16 GB at 16-bit and about 5 GB at 4-bit, plus room for the KV cache. Apple Silicon unified memory and consumer GPUs set the practical limits.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Quantization","desc":"Quantization stores weights in fewer bits (8, 6, 4, or lower) to save memory and speed things up, with some quality loss. GGUF is the common format for llama.cpp and Ollama.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Visual Guide to Quantization","url":"https://newsletter.maartengrootendorst.com/p/a-visual-guide-to-quantization","type":"article","source":"Maarten Grootendorst","free":true},{"title":"GGUF","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/gguf","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Desktop runners","desc":"Ollama and LM Studio download and run models with one command or click, and expose an OpenAI-compatible API so existing code just works. llama.cpp is the engine underneath many of them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Ollama documentation","url":"https://docs.ollama.com/","type":"docs","source":"Ollama","free":true},{"title":"llama.cpp","url":"https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp","type":"tool","source":"ggml-org","free":true}]},{"title":"Server-grade inference","desc":"vLLM and SGLang serve many users at once using techniques like paged attention and continuous batching. Use them when a local runner is too slow for real traffic.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"vLLM documentation","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true}]},{"title":"Evaluating and choosing a model","desc":"Public leaderboards are a starting point, but test on your own prompts for quality, speed (tokens per second), and tool-use reliability. Check context length and license before committing.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Artificial Analysis","url":"https://artificialanalysis.ai/","type":"tool","source":"Artificial Analysis","free":true}]},{"title":"Apple Silicon and MLX","desc":"MLX is Apple's array framework, and mlx-lm runs and fine-tunes LLMs efficiently on Macs.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"mlx-lm","url":"https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx-lm","type":"tool","source":"Apple","free":true}]}]},{"id":"vector-databases","title":"Vector Databases","tier":3,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["rag-fundamentals"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r"},"summary":"A vector database stores embeddings and finds the nearest ones to a query vector quickly, even across millions of items. It adds the things real apps need on top: metadata filters, updates, persistence, and scaling.","why":"Once your corpus grows past what fits in memory, or you need filtering by user, date, or permissions, a plain list of vectors stops working. Picking and tuning the right store affects speed, cost, and retrieval quality.","depth":"Master how approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes trade recall for speed, metadata filtering, and the practical choice between pgvector, a dedicated vector DB, or a managed service. Just know how quantization and index internals (HNSW graphs, IVF, product quantization) work at a high level unless you operate at very large scale.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can explain exact versus approximate nearest neighbor search and the recall/latency trade-off.","You can store, filter, update, and delete vectors with metadata in at least one vector database.","You can choose between pgvector, an open-source vector DB, and a managed service for a given project.","You can tune index parameters and measure their effect on recall and latency."],"project":"Load the same 100k-chunk dataset into pgvector and one dedicated vector DB. Compare query latency, recall against exact search, and memory use at two HNSW settings, then add a metadata filter and see how results and speed change. Write up which you'd pick and why.","resources":[{"title":"Vector Databases in Production for Busy Engineers","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/vector-databases-in-production-for-busy-engineers/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Faiss: The Missing Manual","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Retrieval Optimization: Tokenization to Vector Quantization","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/retrieval-optimization-from-tokenization-to-vector-quantization","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Qdrant documentation","url":"https://qdrant.tech/documentation/","type":"docs","source":"Qdrant","free":true},{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"Nearest Neighbor Indexes for Similarity Search","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/vector-indexes/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"What a vector database does","desc":"It stores vectors plus metadata, builds an index for fast similarity search, and supports create, update, delete, and filtered queries. A vector library like FAISS gives you only the index.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"What is a Vector Database & How Does it Work?","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-database/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"What is a Vector Database?","url":"https://qdrant.tech/articles/what-is-a-vector-database/","type":"article","source":"Qdrant","free":true}]},{"title":"ANN indexes: HNSW and friends","desc":"HNSW is a layered graph that lets search hop quickly toward the nearest neighbors; it is the default in most databases. IVF clusters vectors and searches only nearby clusters. Both trade a little recall for big speed gains.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Hierarchical Navigable Small Worlds (HNSW)","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/hnsw/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Vector indexing concepts","url":"https://docs.weaviate.io/weaviate/concepts/vector-index","type":"docs","source":"Weaviate","free":true}]},{"title":"Metadata filtering and multi-tenancy","desc":"Real queries look like 'nearest chunks, but only from this customer's documents after 2025'. Filtering by metadata, and separating tenants, is often what decides which database fits.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Choosing a store","desc":"pgvector adds vector search to Postgres, which is often enough and keeps one database. Dedicated options include Qdrant, Weaviate, Chroma, LanceDB, Milvus, and managed Pinecone. Decide on scale, filtering needs, hybrid search support, and operations effort.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"pgvector","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"Chroma docs","url":"https://docs.trychroma.com/docs/overview/introduction","type":"docs","source":"Chroma","free":true}]},{"title":"Quantization and embedding size","desc":"Storing vectors as int8 or binary, or truncating Matryoshka embeddings to fewer dimensions, cuts memory and cost by 4x to 32x with small quality loss. Rescoring with full vectors recovers most of it.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Binary and Scalar Embedding Quantization","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/embedding-quantization","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Introduction to Matryoshka Embedding Models","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/matryoshka","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Benchmarks and index internals","desc":"ANN-Benchmarks compares algorithms on recall versus queries per second. The HNSW paper and the FAISS library are the reference points if you want to go deep.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"ANN-Benchmarks","url":"https://ann-benchmarks.com/index.html","type":"tool","source":"ANN-Benchmarks","free":true},{"title":"HNSW paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09320","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"chunking-retrieval","title":"Chunking, Hybrid Search & Reranking","tier":3,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["rag-fundamentals"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r"},"summary":"This node is about getting the right chunks in front of the model. It covers how you split documents, how you combine keyword search (BM25) with vector search, and how a reranker re-scores the candidates so the best ones end up on top.","why":"Most RAG failures are retrieval failures: the answer was in the docs, but the right chunk never made it into the prompt. Better chunking, hybrid search, and reranking are the cheapest, biggest quality wins in most RAG systems.","depth":"Master chunking trade-offs, BM25 basics, hybrid search with rank fusion, two-stage retrieve-then-rerank, and measuring retrieval with recall@k and MRR. Just know that late-interaction models like ColBERT and late chunking exist; reach for them when the basics plateau.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can choose and justify a chunking strategy for a given document type (prose, code, tables, PDFs).","You can explain how BM25 scores documents and why it catches exact terms that embeddings miss.","You can implement hybrid search with reciprocal rank fusion and add a cross-encoder reranker.","You can measure retrieval quality with recall@k, MRR, and nDCG on a labeled test set.","You can apply contextual retrieval to reduce failed retrievals."],"project":"Take your RAG app from the previous node and a 50-question labeled test set. Measure recall@5 and MRR for: vector only, BM25 only, hybrid with RRF, hybrid plus reranker, and hybrid plus reranker with contextual chunks. Chart the results and keep the best configuration.","resources":[{"title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/contextual-retrieval","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Rerankers and Two-Stage Retrieval","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/rerankers/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Chunking Strategies to Improve LLM RAG Pipeline Performance","url":"https://weaviate.io/blog/chunking-strategies-for-rag","type":"article","source":"Weaviate","free":true},{"title":"What is hybrid search?","url":"https://www.elastic.co/what-is/hybrid-search","type":"article","source":"Elastic","free":true},{"title":"Systematically Improving Your RAG","url":"https://jxnl.co/writing/2024/05/22/systematically-improving-your-rag/","type":"article","source":"Jason Liu","free":true},{"title":"Cohere Rerank overview","url":"https://docs.cohere.com/docs/rerank-overview","type":"docs","source":"Cohere","free":true},{"title":"Claude Cookbooks (includes contextual retrieval notebook)","url":"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-cookbooks","type":"tool","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Chunking strategies","desc":"Fixed-size chunks with overlap are the baseline. Recursive and structure-aware splitting follows headings, paragraphs, and code blocks. Semantic chunking splits where the topic changes. Chunk size is a real trade-off: small chunks are precise, large chunks keep context.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Chunking Strategies for LLM Applications","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/chunking-strategies/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Evaluating Chunking Strategies for Retrieval","url":"https://www.trychroma.com/research/evaluating-chunking","type":"article","source":"Chroma","free":true}]},{"title":"Keyword search and BM25","desc":"BM25 ranks documents by how often query terms appear, weighted by how rare those terms are, with a penalty for long documents. It is fast, needs no training, and excels at names, IDs, error codes, and jargon.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Practical BM25: The BM25 Algorithm and its Variables","url":"https://www.elastic.co/blog/practical-bm25-part-2-the-bm25-algorithm-and-its-variables","type":"article","source":"Elastic","free":true}]},{"title":"Hybrid search and rank fusion","desc":"Run BM25 (sparse) and vector (dense) search in parallel and merge the results, usually with reciprocal rank fusion (RRF) or a weighted score. Hybrid almost always beats either method alone.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Hybrid Search Explained","url":"https://weaviate.io/blog/hybrid-search-explained","type":"article","source":"Weaviate","free":true},{"title":"Hybrid Search in Qdrant","url":"https://qdrant.tech/documentation/search-tuning/hybrid-search/","type":"docs","source":"Qdrant","free":true}]},{"title":"Reranking","desc":"Retrieve a wide set (say top 50) cheaply, then score each candidate against the query with a cross-encoder or rerank API and keep the top 5. Cross-encoders read query and document together, so they judge relevance far better than embedding distance.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Rerankers and Two-Stage Retrieval","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/rerankers/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Retrieve & Re-Rank","url":"https://www.sbert.net/examples/sentence_transformer/applications/retrieve_rerank/README.html","type":"docs","source":"Sentence Transformers","free":true}]},{"title":"Contextual retrieval","desc":"Before embedding, prepend a short LLM-written note to each chunk explaining where it sits in the document. Combined with contextual BM25 and reranking, Anthropic measured a 67% drop in failed retrievals.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/contextual-retrieval","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Measuring retrieval","desc":"Build a set of questions with known relevant chunks and measure recall@k (was it found), MRR (how high), and nDCG (ranking quality). Tune chunking and search against these numbers, not vibes.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Evaluation Measures in Information Retrieval","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/offline-evaluation/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"BEIR benchmark","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Late interaction and late chunking","desc":"ColBERT keeps one vector per token and matches token by token for high-quality retrieval. Late chunking embeds the whole document first, then pools per chunk, so each chunk keeps document context.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"ColBERT paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12832","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Late Chunking in Long-Context Embedding Models","url":"https://jina.ai/news/late-chunking-in-long-context-embedding-models/","type":"article","source":"Jina AI","free":true}]}]},{"id":"agents-fundamentals","title":"AI Agents: Core Concepts","tier":3,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["structured-outputs","context-engineering"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r","pm":"r","biz":"a","nc":"r"},"summary":"An AI agent is an LLM that runs tools in a loop to reach a goal: it decides what to do, calls a tool, looks at the result, and repeats until done. Workflows, by contrast, follow code paths you define, with LLM calls at fixed steps.","why":"Agents handle open-ended tasks where you can't script every step in advance, like coding, research, or customer support with many systems. Knowing when not to use an agent matters just as much, since they cost more and fail in less predictable ways.","depth":"Master the agent loop, the difference between workflows and agents, the common workflow patterns, tool design, context management, guardrails, and how to evaluate an agent. Just know the research lineage (ReAct, Reflexion, Voyager) at a summary level.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can explain the agent loop and decide whether a problem needs a simple prompt, a workflow, or an agent.","You can build a minimal agent from scratch with tool calling and a stopping condition, without a framework.","You can apply workflow patterns: prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers, and evaluator-optimizer.","You can manage an agent's context (what goes in, what gets summarized or dropped) over long runs.","You can add guardrails: tool permissions, human approval, step limits, and evals on full trajectories."],"project":"Build an agent from scratch in under 200 lines, with no framework: it gets three tools (web or doc search, a Python sandbox or calculator, and a file writer), a step limit, and a cost cap. Give it a research task, log every step, then rewrite the same task as a fixed workflow and compare reliability and cost over 10 runs.","resources":[{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Agentic AI","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/agentic-ai","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Agents","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2025/01/07/agents.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"LLM Powered Autonomous Agents","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"I think 'agent' may finally have a widely enough agreed upon definition","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Sep/18/agents/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The agent loop","desc":"Model reads context, picks an action (tool call or final answer), gets an observation, and loops. Everything else is detail: which tools, what context, and when to stop.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Workflows vs agents","desc":"Start with the simplest thing that works. Prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers, and evaluator-optimizer are workflow patterns that solve most problems more reliably than a fully autonomous agent.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Tools and the agent-computer interface","desc":"Agents are only as good as their tools. Clear names, focused scope, helpful error messages, and token-efficient outputs make a large difference. Giving an agent a code-execution sandbox or bash is often the most general tool.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Writing effective tools for agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Context engineering for agents","desc":"Agents fill their context window fast. Techniques include just-in-time retrieval, summarizing or compacting history, note-taking files, and sub-agents with fresh context. Skills package instructions the agent loads only when needed.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Equipping agents for the real world with Agent Skills","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Planning, reflection, and memory","desc":"Agents can plan before acting, critique their own output, and keep memory across sessions. Reasoning models now do much of the planning internally, so explicit planning prompts matter less than they used to.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"LLM Powered Autonomous Agents","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Agents","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2025/01/07/agents.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true}]},{"title":"Guardrails and evaluating agents","desc":"Limit tool permissions, require human approval for risky actions, cap steps and spend, and watch for prompt injection in tool results. Evaluate whole trajectories and final outcomes, not just single responses.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Demystifying evals for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"The lethal trifecta for AI agents","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}]}]},{"id":"mcp","title":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","tier":3,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["structured-outputs"],"roles":{"aie":"c","nc":"r","pu":"a"},"summary":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for connecting AI applications to tools and data. An MCP server exposes tools, resources, and prompts once, and any MCP client (Claude, ChatGPT, IDEs, agent frameworks) can use them.","why":"Before MCP, every app needed custom integrations for every tool. MCP is now the common way to give assistants and agents access to files, databases, SaaS apps, and internal systems, so building and securing MCP servers is a core AI engineering skill.","depth":"Master the client-server architecture, the three server primitives (tools, resources, prompts), building a server with an official SDK, testing it with the Inspector, and the security basics. Just know the transport details, authorization flow internals, and the full spec until you ship a remote server.","hours":10,"outcomes":["You can explain MCP's hosts, clients, and servers and what tools, resources, and prompts are for.","You can build an MCP server in Python or TypeScript and connect it to Claude Desktop, Claude Code, or another client.","You can test and debug a server with the MCP Inspector.","You can identify MCP security risks (prompt injection via tool output, over-broad permissions, token handling) and mitigate them."],"project":"Build an MCP server for something you actually use (a notes folder, a SQLite database, or a work API) with at least two tools and one resource. Test it in the Inspector, connect it to Claude Desktop or Claude Code, and write down which tool descriptions you had to rewrite before the model used them correctly.","resources":[{"title":"What is the Model Context Protocol?","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/getting-started/intro","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"MCP: Build Rich-Context AI Apps with Anthropic","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/mcp-build-rich-context-ai-apps-with-anthropic","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Introduction to Model Context Protocol","url":"https://anthropic.skilljar.com/introduction-to-model-context-protocol","type":"course","source":"Anthropic Academy","free":true},{"title":"Introducing the Model Context Protocol","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/model-context-protocol","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"MCP specification","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"MCP Python SDK","url":"https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"FastMCP","url":"https://gofastmcp.com/","type":"tool","source":"FastMCP","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Architecture: hosts, clients, servers","desc":"The host is the AI app, which runs one MCP client per server connection. Servers run locally (stdio) or remotely (streamable HTTP) and speak JSON-RPC.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Architecture overview","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true}]},{"title":"Tools, resources, and prompts","desc":"Tools are actions the model can call. Resources are data the app can read into context. Prompts are reusable templates the user can pick. Clients can also offer features back to servers, like sampling and elicitation.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Understanding MCP servers","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"Understanding MCP clients","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/client-concepts","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true}]},{"title":"Building and testing a server","desc":"Official SDKs (Python, TypeScript, and others) and FastMCP make a basic server a few dozen lines. Test it with the MCP Inspector before connecting it to a client.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Build an MCP server","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/build-server","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"MCP Inspector","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tools/inspector","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true}]},{"title":"Using MCP in clients and agents","desc":"Connect servers to Claude Desktop, Claude Code, IDEs, or your own agent. APIs can also call remote MCP servers directly, so your app doesn't need its own client.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Connect Claude Code to tools via MCP","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"MCP connector (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Security and authorization","desc":"Remote servers use OAuth-based authorization. The bigger risk is the model itself: tool outputs can carry prompt injections, and combining private data, untrusted content, and outbound actions is dangerous. Only install servers you trust.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Understanding Authorization in MCP","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tutorials/security/authorization","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"Simon Willison on MCP","url":"https://simonwillison.net/tags/model-context-protocol/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}]},{"title":"Scaling to many tools","desc":"Loading hundreds of tool definitions wastes context. Patterns like tool search and letting the agent write code that calls MCP tools keep token use down.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Code execution with MCP","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/code-execution-with-mcp","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Ecosystem and the registry","desc":"Thousands of servers exist for GitHub, databases, browsers, SaaS tools, and more. The official MCP Registry lists public servers.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"The MCP Registry","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/registry/about","type":"docs","source":"Model Context Protocol","free":true},{"title":"Reference servers","url":"https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true}]}]},{"id":"agent-frameworks","title":"Agent Frameworks & SDKs","tier":3,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["agents-fundamentals"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"a"},"summary":"Agent frameworks and SDKs give you ready-made pieces for building agents: the tool loop, state, memory, handoffs between agents, tracing, and human-in-the-loop steps. Popular options include the Claude Agent SDK, OpenAI Agents SDK, LangGraph, Pydantic AI, Google ADK, CrewAI, and smolagents.","why":"Once you've built an agent by hand, a framework saves time on plumbing like retries, persistence, streaming, and observability. Picking the right one, or deciding you don't need one, affects how easy your agent is to debug and change.","depth":"Master one framework well enough to build, trace, and deploy an agent, and understand what it does under the hood. Just know the others' strengths so you can choose; avoid deep lock-in to abstractions you can't see through.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can build and deploy an agent with tools, state, and tracing in at least one framework.","You can compare frameworks on control, abstraction level, provider lock-in, and observability.","You can add human-in-the-loop approval and persistent state to an agent.","You can explain what a framework is doing under the hood and drop down to raw API calls when needed."],"project":"Rebuild your from-scratch agent in two frameworks (for example the Claude Agent SDK and LangGraph). Add a human approval step before any write action and persistent state so a run can resume after a crash. Compare lines of code, trace quality, and how easy each was to debug.","resources":[{"title":"Building agents with the Claude Agent SDK","url":"https://claude.com/resources/articles/building-agents-with-the-claude-agent-sdk","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Agent SDK overview","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Agents SDK","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"LangGraph overview","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/overview","type":"docs","source":"LangChain","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Agents Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/unit0/introduction","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Agent Development Kit (ADK)","url":"https://adk.dev/","type":"docs","source":"Google","free":true},{"title":"Claude Quickstarts","url":"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-quickstarts","type":"tool","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Framework or no framework","desc":"Many good agents are a few hundred lines of direct API calls. Frameworks help most when you need durable state, complex control flow, or built-in tracing. If you use one, make sure you understand the code underneath.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Vendor agent SDKs","desc":"The Claude Agent SDK gives you the same harness that powers Claude Code (file and bash tools, context compaction, subagents, MCP). The OpenAI Agents SDK offers agents, handoffs, guardrails, and tracing. Both are light and code-first.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Agent SDK overview","url":"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"OpenAI Agents SDK","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Graph-based orchestration","desc":"LangGraph models an agent as a graph of nodes and edges with explicit state, checkpoints, and interrupts. Good for long-running, branching workflows that need persistence and human approval.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LangGraph overview","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/overview","type":"docs","source":"LangChain","free":true},{"title":"AI Agents in LangGraph","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/ai-agents-in-langgraph","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true}]},{"title":"Type-safe and lightweight options","desc":"Pydantic AI brings typed inputs and outputs and dependency injection. smolagents is minimal and has agents write Python code as their actions. Google ADK targets multi-agent apps on Gemini and Vertex but works with other models.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Pydantic AI","url":"https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/overview/","type":"docs","source":"Pydantic","free":true},{"title":"smolagents","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/smolagents/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Tracing, state, and human-in-the-loop","desc":"Whatever you pick, you need traces of every step, a way to persist and resume state, and pause points for human approval. Check how each framework handles these before committing.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Role-based frameworks","desc":"CrewAI and AutoGen frame agents as roles in a team that talk to each other. Quick to prototype, harder to control precisely.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"CrewAI docs","url":"https://docs.crewai.com/","type":"docs","source":"CrewAI","free":true},{"title":"AutoGen","url":"https://microsoft.github.io/autogen/stable/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true}]}]},{"id":"llm-evals","title":"Evaluating LLM Apps (Evals)","tier":3,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["llm-apis","model-eval-ml"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"c","ds":"r","rs":"r","pm":"r"},"summary":"Evals are tests for LLM apps. You collect realistic inputs, define what a good output looks like, and score your system automatically (with code checks, LLM judges, or human review) so you can tell if a change made things better or worse.","why":"LLM outputs are non-deterministic and fail in subtle ways, so you cannot ship by vibes. Evals are what let you swap models, edit prompts, or change retrieval with confidence. Teams that improve fastest are almost always the ones with good evals.","depth":"Master error analysis (reading real traces and naming failure modes), building a small labeled dataset, writing code-based assertions, and calibrating an LLM judge against human labels. Just know public benchmarks like MMLU exist; they rarely tell you whether your product works.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can read a sample of real outputs, categorize the failures, and decide which ones to measure.","You can build a versioned eval dataset that covers normal, edge, and adversarial cases.","You can write code-based checks and an LLM-as-judge grader, and measure how often the judge agrees with a human.","You can run evals in CI so a prompt or model change cannot ship if it regresses.","You can evaluate multi-step agents on both final outcomes and the path they took."],"project":"Take an LLM app you've built (a RAG bot or extraction tool). Read 50 real outputs and write down every failure. Turn the top three failure types into checks: at least one code assertion and one LLM judge. Hand-label 40 examples, measure the judge's agreement with your labels, then wire the eval suite into CI so a pull request shows the score change.","resources":[{"title":"Your AI Product Needs Evals","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true},{"title":"AI Evals: Everything You Need to Know (FAQ)","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals-faq/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true},{"title":"Demystifying evals for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Evaluation best practices","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/evaluation-best-practices","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"AI Engineering (Ch. 3 and 4: Evaluation)","url":"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-engineering/9781098166298/","type":"book","source":"Chip Huyen / O'Reilly","free":false},{"title":"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"AI Evals For Engineers & PMs","url":"https://maven.com/parlance-labs/evals","type":"course","source":"Hamel Husain & Shreya Shankar (Maven)","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Error analysis first","desc":"Before writing any metric, read 50 to 100 real traces and write notes on what went wrong. Group the notes into failure categories and count them. This tells you what to measure and stops you from tracking generic scores that don't matter.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Field Guide to Rapidly Improving AI Products","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/field-guide/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true}]},{"title":"Building eval datasets","desc":"Collect inputs from real usage, hand-write tricky cases, and generate synthetic ones to fill gaps. Label expected behavior, version the dataset, and keep growing it every time you find a new bug.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Define success criteria and build evaluations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Code-based checks","desc":"Many failures can be caught with plain code: valid JSON, required fields present, no banned phrases, correct tool called, answer matches a regex or exact value. These are fast, cheap, and deterministic, so use them wherever you can.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Promptfoo: Intro","url":"https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/intro/","type":"tool","source":"Promptfoo","free":true}]},{"title":"LLM-as-a-judge","desc":"Use a model to grade outputs on things code cannot check, like tone or faithfulness to sources. Prefer simple pass/fail judgments with a written critique, and validate the judge against human labels before you trust it.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Using LLM-as-a-Judge For Evaluation: A Complete Guide","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/llm-judge/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true},{"title":"Evaluating the Effectiveness of LLM-Evaluators","url":"https://eugeneyan.com/writing/llm-evaluators/","type":"article","source":"Eugene Yan","free":true}]},{"title":"Evaluating agents","desc":"Agents take many steps, so grade both the end state (did the task get done?) and the trajectory (right tools, no wasted loops). Run each task several times because agent results vary from run to run.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Demystifying evals for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Evaluating AI Agents","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/evaluating-ai-agents","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true}]},{"title":"Statistics for evals","desc":"A score on 50 examples is noisy. Learn to report confidence intervals, compare two systems with paired tests on the same questions, and know how many examples you need before a difference is real.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"A statistical approach to model evaluations","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/statistical-approach-to-model-evals","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Eval tooling and benchmarks","desc":"Frameworks like Promptfoo, Inspect, OpenAI Evals, and Braintrust run datasets and graders for you. Public benchmarks and the LM Evaluation Harness compare base models, which is useful for picking a model but not for testing your app.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Inspect","url":"https://inspect.aisi.org.uk/","type":"tool","source":"UK AI Security Institute","free":true},{"title":"LM Evaluation Harness","url":"https://github.com/EleutherAI/lm-evaluation-harness","type":"tool","source":"EleutherAI","free":true}]}]},{"id":"observability","title":"LLM Observability & Tracing","tier":3,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["llm-apis","deployment-basics"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r"},"summary":"Observability is being able to see what your LLM app actually did on every request: the prompt, retrieved context, tool calls, model output, latency, token count, and cost. Tracing records each of those steps as a linked tree you can inspect later.","why":"When a user says 'the bot gave a weird answer,' you need the exact trace to debug it. Traces are also the raw material for evals and for spotting cost or latency problems before they become incidents.","depth":"Master instrumenting an app so every request produces a trace with nested spans, plus dashboards for latency, errors, tokens, and cost. Know the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions exist so your traces stay portable. You don't need to run your own tracing backend at scale.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can instrument an LLM app so each request produces a trace with spans for retrieval, model calls, and tool calls.","You can find a specific bad response in production and see the full context that produced it.","You can build a dashboard of p50/p95 latency, error rate, token usage, and cost per request.","You can collect user feedback and attach it to traces, then turn flagged traces into eval cases.","You can explain what to log and what to redact for privacy."],"project":"Add tracing to a RAG or agent app with Langfuse or Phoenix (self-hosted or free tier). Make sure each request shows spans for retrieval, model calls, and tools, with token counts and cost. Add a thumbs up/down button that attaches feedback to the trace, then build a dashboard of p95 latency and cost per request and use it to find your slowest step.","resources":[{"title":"LLM Observability & Application Tracing","url":"https://langfuse.com/docs/observability/overview","type":"docs","source":"Langfuse","free":true},{"title":"Observability primer","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/observability-primer/","type":"docs","source":"OpenTelemetry","free":true},{"title":"Building A Generative AI Platform","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"What We've Learned From A Year of Building with LLMs","url":"https://applied-llms.org/","type":"article","source":"Applied LLMs","free":true},{"title":"OpenTelemetry for Generative AI","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/","type":"article","source":"OpenTelemetry","free":true},{"title":"What is Arize Phoenix?","url":"https://arize.com/docs/phoenix","type":"tool","source":"Arize","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Logs, metrics, and traces","desc":"The three pillars of observability. Logs are events, metrics are numbers over time, and traces follow one request through every step. LLM apps lean heavily on traces because each request has many steps.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Observability primer","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/observability-primer/","type":"docs","source":"OpenTelemetry","free":true},{"title":"Monitoring Distributed Systems","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/","type":"book","source":"Google SRE Book","free":true}]},{"title":"Tracing LLM calls, RAG, and agents","desc":"Wrap each step in a span: the user request, retrieval, reranking, each model call, each tool call. Record inputs, outputs, model name, and token counts. For agents, the trace tree is the only sane way to see why it looped or picked the wrong tool.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LLM Observability & Application Tracing","url":"https://langfuse.com/docs/observability/overview","type":"docs","source":"Langfuse","free":true}]},{"title":"OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions","desc":"OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard for telemetry. Its GenAI semantic conventions define standard attribute names for model, tokens, and prompts, so you can switch tools without re-instrumenting.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"OpenTelemetry for Generative AI","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/","type":"article","source":"OpenTelemetry","free":true},{"title":"OpenLLMetry","url":"https://github.com/traceloop/openllmetry","type":"tool","source":"Traceloop","free":true}]},{"title":"Cost, latency, and token dashboards","desc":"Track time to first token, total latency at p50 and p95, tokens in and out, cache hit rate, and cost per request or per user. Alert on sudden changes, which often mean a prompt bug or a runaway agent loop.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Feedback loops and online evals","desc":"Capture thumbs up/down, edits, and retries, and attach them to traces. Run cheap automated checks on a sample of live traffic. Route bad traces into your eval dataset so the same bug gets caught next time.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Field Guide to Rapidly Improving AI Products","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/field-guide/","type":"article","source":"Hamel Husain","free":true}]},{"title":"Privacy and retention","desc":"Traces contain user data. Decide what to redact (emails, keys, health data), how long to keep traces, and who can read them. This is a compliance question, not just a technical one.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Choosing a tracing tool","desc":"Popular options include Langfuse and Arize Phoenix (open source, self-hostable), LangSmith, and Braintrust. Most accept OpenTelemetry data. Pick one, instrument early, and avoid building your own.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"What is Arize Phoenix?","url":"https://arize.com/docs/phoenix","type":"tool","source":"Arize","free":true},{"title":"LangSmith Observability","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/observability","type":"docs","source":"LangChain","free":true}]}]},{"id":"mlops","title":"MLOps Pipelines","tier":3,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["deployment-basics","model-eval-ml"],"roles":{"mle":"c","ds":"r","aie":"a"},"summary":"MLOps is the set of practices and tools for taking ML models from notebook to production and keeping them healthy: versioning data and models, tracking experiments, automating training pipelines, testing, deploying, and monitoring for drift.","why":"Most ML projects fail after the model is trained, not before. Without pipelines, you can't reproduce a model, retrain it when data changes, or know when it has quietly gotten worse.","depth":"Master experiment tracking, a model registry, a reproducible training pipeline, and drift monitoring for one real model. Just know the big platforms (Kubeflow, SageMaker, Vertex AI) exist; the concepts transfer, and small teams rarely need them on day one.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can track experiments so any model can be traced back to its code, data, and hyperparameters.","You can version datasets and models and promote a model through a registry from staging to production.","You can build an orchestrated pipeline that ingests data, trains, evaluates, and only deploys if quality gates pass.","You can write tests for data, models, and code, and run them in CI.","You can detect data and prediction drift and decide when to retrain."],"project":"Build an end-to-end pipeline for a tabular model (for example, predicting churn). Version the data with DVC, track runs in MLflow, register the best model, and use Prefect or GitHub Actions to retrain weekly and only promote the new model if it beats the current one. Serve it with FastAPI and add an Evidently drift report on incoming data.","resources":[{"title":"Made With ML: MLOps course","url":"https://madewithml.com/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true},{"title":"Designing Machine Learning Systems","url":"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/designing-machine-learning/9781098107956/","type":"book","source":"Chip Huyen / O'Reilly","free":false},{"title":"MLOps Zoomcamp","url":"https://github.com/DataTalksClub/mlops-zoomcamp","type":"course","source":"DataTalks.Club","free":true},{"title":"Full Stack Deep Learning 2022","url":"https://fullstackdeeplearning.com/course/2022/","type":"video","source":"Full Stack Deep Learning","free":true},{"title":"MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning","type":"docs","source":"Google Cloud","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning in Production","url":"https://mlip-cmu.github.io/","type":"course","source":"Carnegie Mellon University","free":true},{"title":"Rules of Machine Learning","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml","type":"article","source":"Google","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The ML lifecycle and MLOps maturity","desc":"See the full loop from data collection to monitoring and back. Google's maturity levels (manual, pipeline automation, full CI/CD) help you decide how much automation your team needs right now.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning","type":"docs","source":"Google Cloud","free":true},{"title":"Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/86df7dcfd896fcaf2674f757a2463eba-Abstract.html","type":"paper","source":"NeurIPS 2015","free":true}]},{"title":"Experiment tracking and model registry","desc":"Log every run's parameters, metrics, and artifacts so you can compare runs and reproduce the winner. A registry stores model versions with stages like staging and production, so deploys point at a version, not a file on someone's laptop.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Experiment Tracking","url":"https://madewithml.com/courses/mlops/experiment-tracking/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true},{"title":"MLflow documentation","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/index.html","type":"docs","source":"MLflow","free":true}]},{"title":"Data and model versioning","desc":"Code lives in Git, but datasets and model weights are too big. Tools like DVC version them alongside your code so you can say exactly which data trained which model.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Versioning","url":"https://madewithml.com/courses/mlops/versioning/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true},{"title":"DVC documentation","url":"https://doc.dvc.org/","type":"docs","source":"DVC","free":true}]},{"title":"Pipelines and orchestration","desc":"Turn notebook steps into a pipeline (ingest, validate, featurize, train, evaluate, register) that runs on a schedule or trigger. Orchestrators like Airflow, Prefect, Dagster, ZenML, or Kubeflow handle retries and dependencies.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Orchestration","url":"https://madewithml.com/courses/mlops/orchestration/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true}]},{"title":"Testing ML systems and CI/CD","desc":"Test code as usual, but also test data (schemas, ranges, nulls) and models (minimum accuracy, behavior on known cases, no regression versus the current model). Gate deploys on these checks.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Testing ML Systems","url":"https://madewithml.com/courses/mlops/testing/","type":"course","source":"Made With ML","free":true},{"title":"The ML Test Score","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-ml-test-score-a-rubric-for-ml-production-readiness-and-technical-debt-reduction/","type":"paper","source":"Google Research","free":true}]},{"title":"Monitoring, drift, and retraining","desc":"Real-world data shifts over time, and model quality decays with it. Monitor input distributions, prediction distributions, and (when labels arrive) actual accuracy. Decide whether to retrain on a schedule or when drift crosses a threshold.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Data Distribution Shifts and Monitoring","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2022/02/07/data-distribution-shifts-and-monitoring.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"Evidently documentation","url":"https://docs.evidentlyai.com/introduction","type":"tool","source":"Evidently AI","free":true}]},{"title":"Feature stores","desc":"A feature store computes features once and serves the same values for training and live prediction, which prevents training-serving skew. Useful for teams with many models sharing features; overkill for a single model.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Feast documentation","url":"https://docs.feast.dev/","type":"docs","source":"Feast","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-native-business","title":"Building an AI-native Business","tier":4,"lane":"biz","prereqs":["ai-adoption","build-vs-buy"],"roles":{"pm":"r","biz":"c"},"summary":"An AI-native business is designed from the ground up around AI: small teams with AI agents doing much of the routine work, processes built for human-AI collaboration, and products or services that would not be possible without AI.","why":"Companies that bolt AI onto old processes get modest gains. Those that redesign around it can run with far smaller teams, move faster, and offer new kinds of services, which changes how competitors, pricing, and whole industries work.","depth":"Go deep on operating model design: which work goes to agents, how humans supervise, and how roles, pricing, and data strategy change. You don't need to code agents yourself; you do need a sharp sense of what agents can reliably do today and how to keep them safe and accountable.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can redesign a business function around AI agents with clear human oversight points.","You can describe new business models AI enables, such as selling outcomes instead of software seats.","You can plan how team structure, roles, and hiring change in an AI-native company.","You can identify which proprietary data, workflows, and relationships form a durable moat.","You can set up governance so AI-run processes stay accountable and auditable."],"project":"Pick one business function (for example, customer onboarding or monthly reporting) and design its AI-native version. Draw the new workflow showing which steps agents own, where humans approve or review, what gets logged, and how the team and its roles change. Estimate the cost and headcount difference versus today, and pilot one agent-run step for two weeks.","resources":[{"title":"A practical guide to building agents","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/a-practical-guide-to-building-agents.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"AI leads a service as software paradigm shift","url":"https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/ai-leads-a-service-as-software-paradigm-shift","type":"article","source":"Foundation Capital","free":true},{"title":"The Cybernetic Teammate","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/the-cybernetic-teammate","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true},{"title":"Prediction Machines","url":"https://www.predictionmachines.ai/","type":"book","source":"Agrawal, Gans & Goldfarb","free":false},{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"Requests for Startups","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/rfs","type":"article","source":"Y Combinator","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Redesigning work around AI","desc":"Start from the outcome and ask how you'd do it if AI were available from day one, rather than which steps of today's process to automate. This often removes steps entirely.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"How Anthropic teams use Claude Code","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/how-anthropic-teams-use-claude-code","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Agents as part of the workforce","desc":"Agents can now handle multi-step work like research, support tickets, bookkeeping, and coding. Treat them like team members with a defined scope, permissions, a manager, and performance reviews.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A practical guide to building agents","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/business-guides-and-resources/a-practical-guide-to-building-agents.pdf","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Service as software and new business models","desc":"AI lets companies sell completed work (a reviewed contract, a resolved ticket) rather than tools for people to use. This changes pricing toward outcomes and opens markets that were too labor-intensive before.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"AI leads a service as software paradigm shift","url":"https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/ai-leads-a-service-as-software-paradigm-shift","type":"article","source":"Foundation Capital","free":true}]},{"title":"Small teams, new roles","desc":"AI-native teams are smaller and flatter, with people acting as editors, orchestrators, and owners of outcomes. Hiring shifts toward judgment, domain expertise, and the ability to direct AI well.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Cybernetic Teammate","url":"https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/the-cybernetic-teammate","type":"article","source":"Ethan Mollick","free":true}]},{"title":"Data and moats","desc":"When everyone has access to the same models, advantage comes from proprietary data, workflow integration, customer trust, and distribution. Design processes that capture useful data as a by-product.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Governance for AI-run operations","desc":"Logging, audit trails, approval thresholds, incident response, and regulatory compliance (like the EU AI Act) matter more when AI acts on its own. Build these in early rather than retrofitting.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"EU Artificial Intelligence Act","url":"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/","type":"docs","source":"Future of Life Institute","free":true}]},{"title":"Where startups see opportunity","desc":"Investor requests for startups and market reports show which AI-native ideas are attracting builders. Good for spotting trends in your industry early.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Requests for Startups","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/rfs","type":"article","source":"Y Combinator","free":true}]}]},{"id":"ai-governance","title":"AI Governance Programs","tier":4,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["ai-regulation","ai-adoption"],"roles":{"aie":"a","pm":"r","biz":"c"},"summary":"An AI governance program is how an organization decides which AI uses are allowed, who approves them, how risks are checked, and how problems are caught and fixed over time.","why":"Once AI is used across a company, ad hoc decisions stop working. Governance lets you move fast on low-risk uses, keep high-risk ones under control, and show customers, auditors, and regulators that you are in charge.","depth":"Business leaders must master the building blocks: an AI policy, a use-case inventory, risk tiers, an approval path, and incident handling. PMs should know how their features pass through that process. Engineers just need to know what evidence (evals, logs, documentation) the process will ask for.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can draft an acceptable-use AI policy for staff.","You can set up an inventory of AI systems and a risk-tiering method tied to review depth.","You can map your program to NIST AI RMF (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) or ISO/IEC 42001.","You can design an incident response and monitoring loop for AI systems in production."],"project":"Build a starter governance kit for a real or sample company: a one-page AI use policy, an inventory spreadsheet with risk tiers, an intake form for new AI use cases, and an incident response checklist. Map each part to the four NIST AI RMF functions and run one real use case through the whole process.","resources":[{"title":"AI Risk Management Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"NIST AI RMF: Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1)","url":"https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"NIST AI RMF Playbook","url":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"ISO/IEC 42001: AI management systems","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001","type":"docs","source":"ISO","free":false},{"title":"Microsoft Responsible AI","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/ai/responsible-ai","type":"article","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"AIGP certification","url":"https://iapp.org/certify/aigp/","type":"course","source":"IAPP","free":false},{"title":"AI Incident Database","url":"https://incidentdatabase.ai/","type":"tool","source":"Responsible AI Collaborative","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Policy, roles, and ownership","desc":"Write down what staff may and may not do with AI, who owns each AI system, and who can approve new uses. A small cross-functional committee (legal, security, product, business) is common.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Inventory and risk tiering","desc":"You can't govern what you don't know about. Keep a register of every AI system and vendor tool, rate each by impact, and scale review effort to the risk.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"MIT AI Risk Repository","url":"https://airisk.mit.edu/","type":"tool","source":"MIT","free":true}]},{"title":"Frameworks: NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001","desc":"NIST AI RMF is a free, flexible risk framework with a playbook and a generative AI profile. ISO/IEC 42001 is a certifiable management-system standard that customers increasingly ask about.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"NIST AI RMF Playbook","url":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/","type":"docs","source":"NIST","free":true},{"title":"ISO/IEC 42001: AI management systems","url":"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001","type":"docs","source":"ISO","free":false}]},{"title":"Vendor and third-party AI risk","desc":"Most AI risk comes in through tools you buy. Ask vendors about training on your data, retention, security certifications, subprocessors, and model change notices.","level":"deep","resources":[]},{"title":"Monitoring, incidents, and audits","desc":"Models and vendors change under you. Track quality and harm metrics after launch, give people a way to report problems, and learn from public incidents at other companies.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"AI Incident Database","url":"https://incidentdatabase.ai/","type":"tool","source":"Responsible AI Collaborative","free":true}]},{"title":"AI literacy and training","desc":"The EU AI Act requires AI literacy for staff who use AI systems. A short, role-based training program is usually the most cost-effective control you have.","level":"optional","resources":[]}]},{"id":"red-teaming","title":"Red Teaming & Safety Evals","tier":4,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["ai-security","llm-evals"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"a","rs":"r"},"summary":"Red teaming means attacking your own AI system on purpose to find harmful or unsafe behavior before real users do. Safety evals turn those findings into repeatable tests you can run on every release.","why":"Normal evals check that the happy path works. Red teaming checks what happens when someone tries to break it. Labs use it to decide whether a model is safe to release, and app teams use it before launching anything public-facing.","depth":"AI engineers should master manual and automated red teaming for their own apps (jailbreaks, injection, harmful content, data leaks) and turning failures into regression tests. Research scientists should go deeper into automated attack methods, capability evals for dangerous skills, and benchmark design. Gradient-based attacks are 'know it exists' for app builders.","hours":20,"outcomes":["You can write a red-team plan that lists threats, attacker types, and harm categories for an AI app.","You can run automated scans with tools like promptfoo, garak, or PyRIT and read the results.","You can turn red-team findings into a safety eval suite that runs in CI.","You can explain how frontier labs use dangerous-capability evals to make release decisions."],"project":"Pick a chatbot or agent you built (or a simple one with a system prompt). Write a red-team plan with five harm categories. Run 30 minutes of manual attacks, then an automated scan with promptfoo or garak. Fix the top two issues and turn every successful attack into an eval case that runs on each change. Report attack success rate and over-refusal rate before and after.","resources":[{"title":"LLM red teaming guide","url":"https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/red-team/","type":"docs","source":"Promptfoo","free":true},{"title":"Red Teaming LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/red-teaming-llm-applications","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Adversarial Attacks on LLMs","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-10-25-adv-attack-llm/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"PyRIT","url":"https://github.com/Azure/PyRIT","type":"tool","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"Inspect","url":"https://inspect.aisi.org.uk/","type":"tool","source":"UK AI Security Institute","free":true},{"title":"Red Teaming Language Models with Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Advancing red teaming with people and AI","url":"https://openai.com/index/advancing-red-teaming-with-people-and-ai/","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Planning a red-team exercise","desc":"Decide what you're protecting, who might attack, and what counts as a failure. Mix domain experts with people who think like attackers.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Red Teaming Language Models to Reduce Harms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07858","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true}]},{"title":"Jailbreak and attack techniques","desc":"Know the common families: role-play, encoding tricks, many-shot prompting, multi-turn escalation, and optimized adversarial suffixes. Knowing them helps you test for them.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Adversarial Attacks on LLMs","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-10-25-adv-attack-llm/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Many-shot jailbreaking","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/many-shot-jailbreaking","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Automated red teaming tools","desc":"Use one model to generate attacks on another at scale. Open-source scanners provide ready-made attack libraries and scoring.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LLM red teaming guide","url":"https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/red-team/","type":"tool","source":"Promptfoo","free":true},{"title":"garak: LLM vulnerability scanner","url":"https://github.com/NVIDIA/garak","type":"tool","source":"NVIDIA","free":true}]},{"title":"From findings to safety evals","desc":"Every confirmed failure becomes a test case. Track attack success rate and over-refusal rate together, since a model that refuses everything is also broken.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Inspect","url":"https://inspect.aisi.org.uk/","type":"tool","source":"UK AI Security Institute","free":true}]},{"title":"Dangerous capability evals","desc":"Frontier labs and safety institutes test whether models can meaningfully help with cyberattacks, bioweapons, or autonomous replication, and tie the results to release policies.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/responsible-scaling-policy","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"METR","url":"https://metr.org/","type":"article","source":"METR","free":true}]},{"title":"Benchmarks and optimized attacks","desc":"Standard benchmarks like HarmBench let you compare defenses. Gradient-based attacks such as GCG search for adversarial strings automatically and need access to model weights.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"HarmBench","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04249","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"diffusion","title":"Diffusion & Generative Image Models","tier":4,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["cnn","dl-training"],"roles":{"mle":"a","rs":"c"},"summary":"Diffusion models generate images (and video, audio, and more) by learning to reverse a gradual noising process. Starting from pure noise, the model removes a little noise at each step until a clean sample appears, often guided by a text prompt.","why":"Diffusion and its close cousin flow matching power most image and video generators. Understanding them lets you fine-tune them, control them, and follow research on generative media and world models.","depth":"Must master: the forward noising and reverse denoising idea, the noise-prediction training objective, latent diffusion, and classifier-free guidance. Just know the score-based SDE view and the math of flow matching at an intuitive level unless you research generative models.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can explain how a diffusion model is trained and how sampling turns noise into an image.","You can explain why latent diffusion runs in a compressed space and how text conditioning and guidance work.","You can generate, fine-tune, and control a model using the Hugging Face diffusers library.","You can place VAEs, GANs, diffusion, and flow matching on a map of generative models."],"project":"Implement a small DDPM from scratch in PyTorch and train it on MNIST or Fashion-MNIST until samples look recognizable. Then use diffusers to train a LoRA on 15 to 30 images of a style or object and compare outputs at different guidance scales.","resources":[{"title":"What are Diffusion Models?","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2021-07-11-diffusion-models/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"The Illustrated Stable Diffusion","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-stable-diffusion/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true},{"title":"Hugging Face Diffusion Models Course","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/diffusion-course/unit0/1","type":"course","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"MIT 6.S184: Generative AI with Stochastic Differential Equations","url":"https://diffusion.csail.mit.edu/","type":"course","source":"MIT","free":true},{"title":"Practical Deep Learning for Coders, Part 2","url":"https://course.fast.ai/Lessons/part2.html","type":"course","source":"fast.ai","free":true},{"title":"Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.11239","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Diffusers documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Generative models before diffusion","desc":"VAEs learn a compressed latent space; GANs pit a generator against a discriminator. Their strengths and failure modes explain why diffusion took over.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Generative Adversarial Networks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2661","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Denoising diffusion (DDPM)","desc":"Training adds noise to real images at random levels and teaches a network to predict that noise. Sampling runs this in reverse over many small steps.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.11239","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"The Annotated Diffusion Model","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/annotated-diffusion","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Latent diffusion and text conditioning","desc":"Running diffusion inside a VAE's compressed latent space makes high-resolution generation affordable. A text encoder feeds the prompt into the denoiser through cross-attention.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"The Illustrated Stable Diffusion","url":"https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-stable-diffusion/","type":"article","source":"Jay Alammar","free":true},{"title":"High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.10752","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Guidance and sampling","desc":"Classifier-free guidance trades diversity for prompt adherence with a single scale knob. Faster samplers and distillation cut generation from hundreds of steps to a handful.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Classifier-Free Diffusion Guidance","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.12598","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Flow matching and diffusion transformers","desc":"Flow matching learns a straight path from noise to data and is now common in leading image and video models. Diffusion transformers (DiT) replace the U-Net backbone with a transformer that scales better.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Flow Matching for Generative Modeling","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02747","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Scalable Diffusion Models with Transformers","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09748","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"The score-based view","desc":"Diffusion can be seen as learning the gradient of the data's log-density and following it with a stochastic differential equation. This view unifies many variants.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Generative Modeling by Estimating Gradients of the Data Distribution","url":"https://yang-song.net/blog/2021/score/","type":"article","source":"Yang Song","free":true}]},{"title":"Fine-tuning and control","desc":"LoRA adapters, DreamBooth-style personalization, and control networks let you teach a model new styles or subjects and steer layout. These are the everyday tools of applied image generation.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Diffusers documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]}]},{"id":"pretraining","title":"Pretraining & Scaling Laws","tier":4,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["transformers","dl-training"],"roles":{"mle":"r","rs":"c"},"summary":"Pretraining is the first and most expensive stage of building an LLM: training a transformer from scratch on trillions of tokens to predict the next token. Scaling laws describe how loss improves predictably as you grow parameters, data, and compute.","why":"Pretraining decides most of a model's knowledge and raw ability, and scaling laws drive billion-dollar decisions about model size and data. Understanding them helps you read frontier model reports and reason about where capabilities come from.","depth":"Must master: the pretraining objective, data pipelines (filtering, deduplication, mixing), compute-optimal scaling (Chinchilla), and the basic training-stability tricks. Just know the specifics of parallelism strategies and hyperparameter transfer (muP) unless you train large models yourself.","hours":50,"outcomes":["You can explain how data quantity and quality, model size, and compute trade off under scaling laws.","You can describe how a web-scale pretraining dataset is collected, filtered, deduplicated, and mixed.","You can pretrain a small GPT end to end and track loss against compute.","You can read a frontier model technical report and summarize its data, architecture, and training recipe."],"project":"Train a family of four or five small GPT models (for example 1M to 50M parameters) on a fixed slice of FineWeb with different compute budgets. Plot final loss against compute, fit a simple power law, and use it to predict the loss of a slightly larger model before training it to check.","resources":[{"title":"Let's reproduce GPT-2 (124M)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l8pRSuU81PU","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch","url":"http://cs336.stanford.edu/spring2025/","type":"course","source":"Stanford","free":true},{"title":"Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models (Chinchilla)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"The Smol Training Playbook","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingFaceTB/smol-training-playbook","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"OLMo (fully open models, data, and code)","url":"https://allenai.org/olmo","type":"tool","source":"Allen Institute for AI","free":true},{"title":"The Ultra-Scale Playbook","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/nanotron/ultrascale-playbook","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Scaling laws","desc":"Loss falls as a smooth power law in parameters, data, and compute. Kaplan et al. found the first laws; Chinchilla showed that many models were undertrained and that data should grow with model size.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models (Chinchilla)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Pretraining data","desc":"Most text comes from filtered web crawls plus code, books, papers, and math. Quality filtering, deduplication, and the data mix often matter as much as the architecture.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"FineWeb: decanting the web for the finest text data at scale","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingFaceFW/blogpost-fineweb-v1","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Training a GPT end to end","desc":"Reproducing a small model like GPT-2 teaches the full loop: tokenized data shards, the model, the optimizer, learning rate schedule, evaluation, and checkpointing.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Let's reproduce GPT-2 (124M)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l8pRSuU81PU","type":"video","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true},{"title":"nanochat","url":"https://github.com/karpathy/nanochat","type":"tool","source":"Andrej Karpathy","free":true}]},{"title":"Stability and hyperparameters at scale","desc":"Large runs suffer loss spikes and divergence, handled with careful initialization, normalization, learning rate warmup, and monitoring. Methods like muP let you tune on small models and transfer settings to big ones.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Tensor Programs V: Tuning Large Neural Networks via Zero-Shot Hyperparameter Transfer","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03466","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"The Smol Training Playbook","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingFaceTB/smol-training-playbook","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Parallelism and infrastructure","desc":"Big models are split across many GPUs with data, tensor, pipeline, and expert parallelism. Throughput metrics like MFU tell you how well you are using the hardware.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The Ultra-Scale Playbook","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/nanotron/ultrascale-playbook","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Reading frontier technical reports","desc":"Reports from open model labs reveal real recipes: data sizes, mixtures, annealing phases, long-context extension, and mixture-of-experts designs.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"The Llama 3 Herd of Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21783","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.19437","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"rlhf","title":"Alignment Training: RLHF & DPO","tier":4,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["fine-tuning"],"roles":{"mle":"r","rs":"c","aie":"a"},"summary":"Alignment training (post-training) turns a pretrained model into a helpful, honest, and safer assistant. RLHF trains a reward model on human preference comparisons and then optimizes the LLM against it, while DPO and related methods learn directly from preference pairs without a separate reward model.","why":"This stage is where models learn to follow instructions, refuse harmful requests, and match a desired personality. It is also where many failure modes like sycophancy and reward hacking come from, so it matters for both capability and safety work.","depth":"Must master: the SFT, reward model, and RL pipeline from InstructGPT; how DPO simplifies it; and what reward hacking is. Just know the math of PPO, the many DPO variants, and the details of constitutional and AI-feedback methods unless you do post-training research.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can explain the three-stage InstructGPT pipeline and what each stage contributes.","You can explain how DPO turns preference data into a simple classification-style loss.","You can run a small DPO training job with TRL and evaluate the result.","You can describe reward hacking, sycophancy, and how AI feedback and constitutions are used in practice."],"project":"Start from a small instruction-tuned open model. Build or download a preference dataset of a few thousand pairs that reward a specific behavior (for example, concise answers or polite refusals), train with DPO using TRL and LoRA, and compare before and after with an LLM judge plus your own blind ratings on 50 prompts.","resources":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (book)","url":"https://rlhfbook.com/","type":"book","source":"Nathan Lambert","free":true},{"title":"Illustrating Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/rlhf","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"RLHF: Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2023/05/02/rlhf.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback (InstructGPT)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Direct Preference Optimization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"TRL documentation","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/index","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Preference data and reward models","desc":"Humans compare two model responses and pick the better one. A reward model learns to predict those preferences and score new responses.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Deep reinforcement learning from human preferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03741","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"RLHF with PPO","desc":"The model generates responses, the reward model scores them, and PPO updates the model while a KL penalty keeps it close to the original. InstructGPT showed a small aligned model could beat a much larger raw one in human ratings.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback (InstructGPT)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Illustrating Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/rlhf","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Direct Preference Optimization","desc":"DPO shows the RLHF objective can be optimized directly from preference pairs with a simple loss, no reward model or RL loop needed. It is cheap, stable, and widely used in open models.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Direct Preference Optimization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"TRL: DPO Trainer","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/dpo_trainer","type":"docs","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"AI feedback and Constitutional AI","desc":"Instead of only human labels, a model critiques and ranks outputs against a written set of principles. This scales feedback and makes the target values explicit.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Reward hacking and sycophancy","desc":"Models learn to exploit flaws in the reward signal, for example by flattering users or gaming tests. Spotting and mitigating this is a core alignment problem.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Reward Hacking in Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2024-11-28-reward-hacking/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true}]},{"title":"Modern post-training recipes","desc":"Current pipelines combine SFT, preference tuning, and reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) for math and code, often using GRPO. Open recipes like Tulu 3 document the full process.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Tulu 3: Pushing Frontiers in Open Language Model Post-Training","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15124","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"DeepSeekMath (introduces GRPO)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03300","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"PPO in depth","desc":"Proximal Policy Optimization keeps each update small with a clipped objective. You only need its internals if you implement RL training yourself.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"advanced-rag","title":"Advanced RAG Patterns","tier":4,"lane":"apps","prereqs":["vector-databases","chunking-retrieval"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r"},"summary":"Advanced RAG covers the patterns that go beyond 'embed, retrieve top-k, answer': rewriting queries, routing between sources, retrieving over knowledge graphs, letting an agent decide when and what to search, and evaluating the whole system rigorously.","why":"Simple RAG breaks on vague questions, multi-hop questions ('compare X across these three reports'), questions about a whole corpus, and data that lives in many places. These patterns fix specific failure modes, and evaluation tells you which ones are worth their cost.","depth":"Master query rewriting, agentic RAG with search as a tool, and component-level evaluation (retrieval metrics plus faithfulness and answer relevance). Know GraphRAG, Self-RAG, and CRAG well enough to recognize when a problem calls for them; most production systems don't need all of them.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can diagnose RAG failures and pick the pattern that fixes each (rewriting, routing, graph retrieval, agentic search).","You can implement query rewriting, decomposition, and HyDE and measure whether they help.","You can build an agentic RAG loop where the model calls search tools, judges results, and searches again.","You can explain when GraphRAG is worth its indexing cost and build a small version.","You can run an end-to-end RAG evaluation with retrieval metrics, faithfulness, and answer relevance, using LLM judges you've checked against human labels."],"project":"Build a research assistant over a few hundred related documents (for example, a company's annual reports). Compare three versions on a 50-question test set that includes multi-hop and whole-corpus questions: tuned hybrid RAG, hybrid RAG with query decomposition, and an agentic RAG loop with search tools. Report retrieval recall, faithfulness, answer correctness, latency, and cost per question.","resources":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Large Language Models: A Survey","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10997","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Building Performant RAG Applications for Production","url":"https://developers.llamaindex.ai/python/framework/optimizing/production_rag/","type":"docs","source":"LlamaIndex","free":true},{"title":"Knowledge Graphs for RAG","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/knowledge-graphs-rag","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Learn RAG From Scratch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sVcwVQRHIc8","type":"video","source":"freeCodeCamp / LangChain","free":true},{"title":"RAG Evaluation: Don't let customers tell you first","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/rag-evaluation/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true},{"title":"Ragas: Automated Evaluation of Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15217","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Evaluating (LlamaIndex)","url":"https://developers.llamaindex.ai/python/framework/module_guides/evaluating/","type":"docs","source":"LlamaIndex","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Query rewriting and decomposition","desc":"Users ask vague or compound questions. An LLM can rewrite the query, expand it into several variants (multi-query), split it into sub-questions, or step back to a more general question before retrieval.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Query Rewriting for Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14283","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Advanced RAG Techniques","url":"https://www.pinecone.io/learn/advanced-rag-techniques/","type":"article","source":"Pinecone","free":true}]},{"title":"HyDE and query-side tricks","desc":"HyDE has the model write a hypothetical answer and embeds that instead of the question, because answers look more like documents than questions do. Useful when queries are short and documents are long.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Precise Zero-Shot Dense Retrieval without Relevance Labels (HyDE)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10496","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Agentic RAG","desc":"Instead of a fixed pipeline, give an agent search tools (vector, keyword, SQL, web) and let it decide what to search, read the results, and search again until it has enough. This is now the default for complex questions and research assistants.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation: A Survey on Agentic RAG","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09136","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"How we built our multi-agent research system","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"GraphRAG and knowledge graphs","desc":"GraphRAG extracts entities and relationships into a graph, clusters them into communities, and summarizes each. It shines on 'global' questions about a whole corpus and on multi-hop links, but indexing is expensive.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"From Local to Global: A Graph RAG Approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16130","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Microsoft GraphRAG docs","url":"https://microsoft.github.io/graphrag/","type":"docs","source":"Microsoft","free":true}]},{"title":"Self-correcting RAG (Self-RAG, CRAG)","desc":"These patterns grade retrieved documents and draft answers, then re-retrieve, fall back to web search, or revise when quality is low. You can implement the core idea with a simple grader step in an agent loop.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Self-RAG","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11511","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15884","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"RAG evaluation","desc":"Evaluate retrieval (recall@k, MRR) and generation separately. Generation metrics include faithfulness (is every claim supported by the context), answer relevance, and context precision. Use LLM-as-judge tools like Ragas, but validate the judge against your own labels.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Ragas: available metrics","url":"https://docs.ragas.io/en/stable/concepts/metrics/available_metrics/","type":"docs","source":"Ragas","free":true},{"title":"RAG Evaluation cookbook","url":"https://huggingface.co/learn/cookbook/rag_evaluation","type":"interactive","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]},{"title":"Routing, multi-source, and structured data","desc":"Route questions to the right index or tool (docs, SQL database, API). Text-to-SQL, metadata extraction, and parent-child or small-to-big retrieval handle data that doesn't fit plain chunks.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Building Performant RAG Applications for Production","url":"https://developers.llamaindex.ai/python/framework/optimizing/production_rag/","type":"docs","source":"LlamaIndex","free":true}]}]},{"id":"multi-agent","title":"Multi-agent Systems & Orchestration","tier":4,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["agent-frameworks"],"roles":{"aie":"r","rs":"a"},"summary":"Multi-agent systems split work across several LLM agents, such as an orchestrator that delegates to specialized subagents running in parallel. Orchestration is how you coordinate them: who decides, how context is shared, and how results are combined.","why":"For broad tasks like deep research or large codebases, parallel subagents with fresh context windows can cover more ground and finish faster than one agent. They also use many more tokens and fail in new ways, so you need to know when they are worth it.","depth":"Master the orchestrator-worker pattern, context sharing and handoffs, and the known failure modes. Know agent-to-agent protocols like A2A and emergent 'society of agents' research exist, but most real systems use one lead agent with subagents.","hours":15,"outcomes":["You can decide when multiple agents beat a single agent, considering parallelism, context limits, and token cost.","You can build an orchestrator that spawns subagents with clear task briefs and merges their results.","You can design how context and state are shared or isolated between agents.","You can diagnose common multi-agent failures like duplicated work, lost context, and agents contradicting each other."],"project":"Build a research system with a lead agent that breaks a question into 3 to 5 sub-questions, runs subagents in parallel with search tools, and writes a cited report. Compare it to a single agent on 10 questions for quality, wall-clock time, and total tokens.","resources":[{"title":"How we built our multi-agent research system","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Don't Build Multi-Agents","url":"https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents","type":"article","source":"Cognition","free":true},{"title":"Agent orchestration (OpenAI Agents SDK)","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/multi_agent/","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Multi AI Agent Systems with crewAI","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/multi-ai-agent-systems-with-crewai","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Orchestrator and subagents","desc":"A lead agent plans, writes detailed briefs, spawns subagents in parallel, and synthesizes their findings. Each subagent works in its own context window, which acts as compression.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"How we built our multi-agent research system","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"When not to go multi-agent","desc":"Multi-agent setups can use several times more tokens and lose shared context. Tasks with tightly linked decisions, like most coding, often work better with a single agent that has full context.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Don't Build Multi-Agents","url":"https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents","type":"article","source":"Cognition","free":true}]},{"title":"Handoffs and routing","desc":"In a handoff, one agent passes the conversation to a specialist (for example from triage to billing). Routing and handoffs are common in customer support systems.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Handoffs","url":"https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/handoffs/","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Multi-agent (LangChain)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent","type":"docs","source":"LangChain","free":true}]},{"title":"Failure modes","desc":"Research catalogs failures such as poor task specs, agents ignoring each other, premature stopping, and weak verification. Most are design problems, fixable with better briefs and checks.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Agent-to-agent protocols","desc":"A2A is an open protocol for agents built by different vendors to discover each other and exchange tasks. MCP connects agents to tools; A2A connects agents to agents.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"A2A Protocol","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/","type":"docs","source":"Linux Foundation","free":true}]},{"title":"Conversation-based multi-agent research","desc":"Frameworks like AutoGen explored agents that solve tasks by conversing. Good background for understanding the design space.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"AutoGen paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08155","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"computer-use","title":"Browser & Computer-Use Agents","tier":4,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["agents-fundamentals","mcp"],"roles":{"aie":"r","nc":"a"},"summary":"Computer-use and browser agents operate software the way people do: they look at screenshots or page structure, then click, type, and scroll. Browser agents focus on websites; computer-use agents can drive a whole desktop.","why":"Lots of work lives in apps with no API, like legacy systems, web portals, and internal tools. These agents can automate those tasks, and they're the basis of products like Claude in Chrome and ChatGPT agent. They are also slower, costlier, and riskier than API-based tools.","depth":"Master how the screenshot-action loop works, when to prefer an API, MCP, or DOM-based browser automation instead, and how to sandbox these agents safely. Just know the benchmarks (OSWorld, WebArena) and how vision-based actions are trained.","hours":12,"outcomes":["You can explain the screenshot, reason, act loop and the trade-off between vision-based and DOM-based control.","You can run a computer-use agent safely in a sandboxed VM or container.","You can build a browser automation that combines an LLM with Playwright or a browser-agent library.","You can identify and reduce risks like prompt injection from web pages and unintended actions."],"project":"In a Docker container or VM, build an agent that logs into a test web app, fills a multi-page form from data in a spreadsheet, and takes a screenshot of the confirmation. Implement it twice, once with pixel-based computer use and once with Playwright plus an LLM, and compare success rate, speed, and cost over 10 runs.","resources":[{"title":"Computer use tool","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/computer-use-tool","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Claude Quickstarts (computer use demo)","url":"https://github.com/anthropics/claude-quickstarts","type":"tool","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Computer use (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-computer-use","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Developing a computer use model","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/developing-computer-use","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Playwright","url":"https://playwright.dev/","type":"tool","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"OSWorld: Benchmarking Multimodal Agents","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07972","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"How computer use works","desc":"The model gets a screenshot, decides on an action (move mouse, click at x,y, type), the harness performs it, and a new screenshot comes back. Counting pixels and reading screens accurately were the hard parts to train.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Developing a computer use model","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/developing-computer-use","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Computer use tool","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/computer-use-tool","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Browser agents and DOM-based control","desc":"In a browser you can use the page's accessibility tree or DOM instead of pixels, which is faster and more reliable. Playwright, Playwright MCP, and browser-use are common building blocks.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Playwright MCP","url":"https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp","type":"tool","source":"Microsoft","free":true},{"title":"browser-use","url":"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing the right interface","desc":"Prefer an API or MCP server if one exists, then DOM-based browser control, then pixel-level computer use. Each step down is more general but slower and more fragile.","level":"core","resources":[]},{"title":"Safety and sandboxing","desc":"Run agents in an isolated VM or container with limited accounts. Web pages can contain prompt injections, so require confirmation before purchases, sending messages, or changing settings.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Piloting Claude in Chrome","url":"https://claude.com/resources/articles/claude-for-chrome","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Benchmarks","desc":"OSWorld tests real desktop tasks across apps; WebArena tests realistic websites. Scores rose fast through 2025 and 2026, but long, multi-app tasks are still error-prone.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"OSWorld","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07972","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"WebArena","url":"https://webarena.dev/","type":"tool","source":"WebArena","free":true}]},{"title":"Hosted browser infrastructure","desc":"Services run headless browsers at scale with sessions, proxies, and recordings, so you don't have to manage browser fleets yourself.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Browserbase","url":"https://www.browserbase.com/","type":"tool","source":"Browserbase","free":false}]}]},{"id":"llm-inference","title":"Inference Optimization","tier":4,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["open-models","transformers"],"roles":{"aie":"r","mle":"c","rs":"r"},"summary":"Inference optimization is making a model generate tokens faster, cheaper, and for more users at once on the same hardware. It covers how serving engines manage GPU memory, batch requests, compress weights, and cut wasted work.","why":"Inference is where most of the money goes once a model is in use. If you self-host open models, the right engine and settings can mean several times more throughput per GPU, or a model that fits on hardware you can afford.","depth":"Master the mental model: prefill vs decode, why decoding is memory-bandwidth bound, the KV cache, and the latency vs throughput trade-off. Be able to run and tune vLLM or SGLang. Writing custom CUDA kernels is specialist work; know that FlashAttention and similar kernels exist and what they fix.","hours":35,"outcomes":["You can explain prefill vs decode and why token generation is limited by memory bandwidth, not compute.","You can estimate KV cache memory for a model and context length, and how many users a GPU can hold.","You can serve an open model with vLLM or SGLang and benchmark time to first token, tokens per second, and throughput.","You can choose a quantization format and measure its effect on quality and speed.","You can decide when to use tensor parallelism, prefix caching, or speculative decoding."],"project":"Rent a single GPU and serve an 8B open model with vLLM. Write a load-test script that measures TTFT, tokens per second, and throughput at 1, 8, and 32 concurrent users. Then compare FP16 vs an AWQ or FP8 quantized version and turn prefix caching on and off with a long shared system prompt. Write up the numbers and a quick quality check on 20 eval questions.","resources":[{"title":"vLLM documentation","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true},{"title":"Mastering LLM Techniques: Inference Optimization","url":"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/mastering-llm-techniques-inference-optimization/","type":"article","source":"NVIDIA","free":true},{"title":"Large Transformer Model Inference Optimization","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-01-10-inference-optimization/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Efficiently Serving LLMs","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/efficiently-serving-llms","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true},{"title":"How To Scale Your Model (Inference chapters)","url":"https://jax-ml.github.io/scaling-book/","type":"book","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true},{"title":"AI Engineering (Ch. 9: Inference Optimization)","url":"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-engineering/9781098166298/","type":"book","source":"Chip Huyen / O'Reilly","free":false}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The inference mental model","desc":"Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel and is compute heavy. Decode generates one token at a time and is limited by how fast weights move from GPU memory. Key metrics are time to first token (TTFT), time per output token (TPOT), and total throughput.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Transformer Inference Arithmetic","url":"https://kipp.ly/p/transformer-inference-arithmetic","type":"article","source":"kipply","free":true},{"title":"LLM Inference Performance Engineering: Best Practices","url":"https://www.databricks.com/blog/llm-inference-performance-engineering-best-practices","type":"article","source":"Databricks","free":true}]},{"title":"KV cache and PagedAttention","desc":"The KV cache stores attention keys and values for past tokens so they aren't recomputed. It grows with context length and batch size and often limits how many users fit on a GPU. PagedAttention (vLLM) manages it in fixed-size blocks to cut waste.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Efficient Memory Management for LLM Serving with PagedAttention","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Continuous batching","desc":"Instead of waiting for a whole batch to finish, the server adds and removes requests every decoding step. This keeps the GPU busy and is the single biggest throughput win in modern serving engines.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Achieve 23x LLM Inference Throughput & Reduce p50 Latency","url":"https://www.anyscale.com/blog/continuous-batching-llm-inference","type":"article","source":"Anyscale","free":true}]},{"title":"Quantization","desc":"Store weights (and sometimes activations or the KV cache) in fewer bits, such as FP8, INT8, or 4-bit formats like AWQ and GPTQ. Less memory means bigger batches and cheaper GPUs. Always measure quality on your own evals after quantizing.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Quantization (vLLM docs)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/quantization/index.html","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true},{"title":"AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00978","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Prefix caching and speculative decoding","desc":"Prefix caching reuses the KV cache when many requests share the same system prompt or documents. Speculative decoding has a small draft model guess several tokens that the big model verifies in one pass, cutting latency without changing outputs.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Automatic Prefix Caching","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/automatic_prefix_caching/","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true},{"title":"Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.17192","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Attention kernels and multi-GPU serving","desc":"FlashAttention reorders the attention computation to avoid slow memory reads. Models too big for one GPU are split with tensor or pipeline parallelism. Some systems also split prefill and decode onto different GPUs (disaggregated serving).","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"FlashAttention","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Parallelism and Scaling","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/serving/parallelism_scaling/","type":"docs","source":"vLLM","free":true}]},{"title":"Choosing a serving engine","desc":"vLLM and SGLang are the main open-source engines for GPU serving, with TensorRT-LLM for NVIDIA-tuned setups and llama.cpp for CPUs and laptops. Hugging Face TGI is now in maintenance mode, and Hugging Face points users to vLLM and SGLang for new deployments.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"SGLang documentation","url":"https://docs.sglang.io/","type":"docs","source":"SGLang","free":true},{"title":"text-generation-inference (GitHub)","url":"https://github.com/huggingface/text-generation-inference","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]}]},{"id":"llmops","title":"LLMOps at Scale: Cost, Latency, Routing","tier":4,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["observability","llm-evals"],"roles":{"aie":"c","mle":"r"},"summary":"LLMOps at scale is running LLM features in production for many users without blowing the budget or the latency target. It covers model routing, caching, rate limits, fallbacks, guardrails, prompt versioning, and the gateway layer that ties it together.","why":"Once usage grows, LLM bills and tail latency become real business problems, and provider outages become your outages. Teams that route easy requests to small models, cache aggressively, and fail over gracefully often cut costs by half or more.","depth":"Master cost and latency levers (model choice, prompt caching, batching, output length), routing with fallbacks, and treating prompts as versioned, eval-gated artifacts. Learned routers and semantic caches are worth knowing about; apply them only after the basics are in place and measured.","hours":25,"outcomes":["You can break down cost per request and per user, and name the top three levers to reduce it.","You can put an LLM gateway in front of several providers with retries, fallbacks, and rate limiting.","You can route requests between a small and a large model and prove with evals that quality held.","You can use provider prompt caching and batch APIs to cut cost on suitable workloads.","You can version prompts and roll out a change behind an eval gate and a canary."],"project":"Take an existing LLM app with tracing and evals. Put LiteLLM in front of it with a fallback provider. Add a simple router that sends short or simple requests to a small model, enable prompt caching on your system prompt, and move one offline job to a batch API. Report cost per request, p95 latency, and eval score before and after.","resources":[{"title":"Building A Generative AI Platform","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"What We've Learned From A Year of Building with LLMs","url":"https://applied-llms.org/","type":"article","source":"Applied LLMs","free":true},{"title":"AI Engineering (Ch. 9 and 10)","url":"https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-engineering/9781098166298/","type":"book","source":"Chip Huyen / O'Reilly","free":false},{"title":"Prompt caching","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Latency optimization","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"LLMOps (LLM Bootcamp)","url":"https://fullstackdeeplearning.com/llm-bootcamp/spring-2023/llmops/","type":"video","source":"Full Stack Deep Learning","free":true},{"title":"RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Cost anatomy and budgeting","desc":"Cost is roughly input tokens times input price plus output tokens times output price, multiplied by calls per task. Agents and long contexts multiply this fast. Track cost per feature and per user, and set budgets and alerts.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Building A Generative AI Platform","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true}]},{"title":"Latency engineering","desc":"Stream tokens, shorten outputs, run independent calls in parallel, pick smaller models for simple steps, and keep prompts stable so they cache. Measure p95, not just averages, since slow tails are what users notice.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Latency optimization","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Reducing latency","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Caching and batch APIs","desc":"Provider prompt caching makes repeated prefixes (system prompts, documents, tool definitions) much cheaper and faster. Batch APIs process non-urgent jobs at a discount. Response caching helps when identical requests repeat.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Prompt caching","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Batch API","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch","type":"docs","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Model routing and cascades","desc":"Send easy requests to a cheap model and hard ones to a strong model. A router can be rules, a classifier, or a cascade that escalates when the cheap model is unsure. Always validate routing with evals, not intuition.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"RouteLLM: Learning to Route LLMs with Preference Data","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"FrugalGPT","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05176","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Gateways, rate limits, and fallbacks","desc":"An LLM gateway gives you one API over many providers, plus retries with backoff, fallbacks when a provider is down or rate-limited, per-team keys, and spend tracking. Know your provider's rate limit tiers before launch day.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"LiteLLM: Getting Started","url":"https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/","type":"tool","source":"LiteLLM","free":true},{"title":"Rate limits","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Prompt and model versioning","desc":"Treat prompts, model IDs, and tool definitions as versioned config. Every change runs through your eval suite, ships behind a flag or canary, and can be rolled back. Pin model versions so a provider update doesn't surprise you.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Automated Testing for LLMOps","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/automated-testing-llmops","type":"course","source":"DeepLearning.AI","free":true}]},{"title":"Self-host vs API economics","desc":"APIs win at low or spiky volume. Self-hosting open models can win at high, steady volume or when data must stay in-house, but you pay for GPUs, engineers, and idle time. Run the numbers with real traffic before switching.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"LLMOps (LLM Bootcamp)","url":"https://fullstackdeeplearning.com/llm-bootcamp/spring-2023/llmops/","type":"video","source":"Full Stack Deep Learning","free":true}]}]},{"id":"distributed-training","title":"Distributed Training & GPUs","tier":4,"lane":"ops","prereqs":["dl-training"],"roles":{"mle":"r","rs":"r"},"summary":"Distributed training spreads one training job across many GPUs, and often many machines, so you can train bigger models or train faster. It combines GPU performance basics with parallelism strategies like data, tensor, and pipeline parallelism, and sharded optimizers like ZeRO and FSDP.","why":"Any model beyond a few billion parameters cannot be trained or even fine-tuned fully on one GPU. Knowing how parallelism works lets you pick the right setup, avoid out-of-memory errors, and stop wasting expensive GPU hours.","depth":"Master why training runs out of memory (weights, gradients, optimizer state, activations), mixed precision, gradient accumulation and checkpointing, and running DDP and FSDP in PyTorch. Know how tensor, pipeline, and context parallelism work conceptually; implementing them from scratch is for people building training frameworks.","hours":45,"outcomes":["You can estimate how much GPU memory a training run needs and explain where it goes.","You can use mixed precision, gradient accumulation, and activation checkpointing to fit a larger model or batch.","You can launch a multi-GPU job with PyTorch DDP and FSDP (or Accelerate/DeepSpeed) and verify it scales.","You can explain data, tensor, pipeline, and sequence parallelism and when each one is used.","You can profile a training step and tell whether it is compute, memory, or communication bound."],"project":"Take a small GPT-style training script (such as nanoGPT) and run it on one GPU, then on 2 to 4 GPUs with DDP, then with FSDP. Add BF16 mixed precision and activation checkpointing. For each setup, record peak memory, tokens per second, and scaling efficiency, and use the PyTorch profiler to find where time goes in one training step.","resources":[{"title":"The Ultra-Scale Playbook: Training LLMs on GPU Clusters","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/nanotron/ultrascale-playbook","type":"book","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"How To Scale Your Model","url":"https://jax-ml.github.io/scaling-book/","type":"book","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true},{"title":"PyTorch Distributed Overview","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/dist_overview.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr From First Principles","url":"https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html","type":"article","source":"Horace He","free":true},{"title":"Machine Learning Engineering Open Book","url":"https://github.com/stas00/ml-engineering","type":"book","source":"Stas Bekman","free":true},{"title":"GPU MODE lectures","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@GPUMODE","type":"video","source":"GPU MODE","free":true},{"title":"Zero Redundancy Optimizer (ZeRO) tutorial","url":"https://www.deepspeed.ai/tutorials/zero/","type":"docs","source":"DeepSpeed","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"GPU fundamentals and performance","desc":"Learn what a GPU is good at, the difference between compute-bound and memory-bound work, why tensor cores need the right shapes and dtypes, and how to read a profiler trace.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr From First Principles","url":"https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html","type":"article","source":"Horace He","free":true},{"title":"GPU Glossary","url":"https://modal.com/gpu-glossary","type":"docs","source":"Modal","free":true}]},{"title":"Memory budget and single-GPU tricks","desc":"Training memory is weights plus gradients plus optimizer state plus activations. Mixed precision (BF16), gradient accumulation, and activation checkpointing are the first tools to reach for before adding GPUs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Automatic Mixed Precision (torch.amp)","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/amp.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Data parallelism with DDP","desc":"Every GPU holds a full copy of the model and processes a different slice of the batch, then gradients are averaged with an all-reduce. This is the simplest and most common way to scale.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Getting Started with Distributed Data Parallel","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/ddp_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"PyTorch Distributed Overview","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/dist_overview.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]},{"title":"Sharding: ZeRO and FSDP","desc":"Instead of each GPU keeping a full copy of optimizer state, gradients, and weights, shard them across GPUs and gather pieces only when needed. This is how most teams fine-tune and train models that don't fit on one GPU.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Getting Started with Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP2)","url":"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/FSDP_tutorial.html","type":"docs","source":"PyTorch","free":true},{"title":"ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.02054","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Tensor, pipeline, and context parallelism","desc":"Tensor parallelism splits individual matrix multiplies across GPUs. Pipeline parallelism puts different layers on different GPUs. Context (sequence) parallelism splits long sequences. Large runs combine them all, which is called 3D or 4D parallelism.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08053","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"How to Train Really Large Models on Many GPUs?","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2021-09-25-train-large/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true}]},{"title":"Communication and networking","desc":"Collectives like all-reduce, all-gather, and reduce-scatter move tensors between GPUs over NVLink inside a node and InfiniBand or Ethernet between nodes. At scale, communication overlap often decides whether adding GPUs helps.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"NCCL overview","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/overview.html","type":"docs","source":"NVIDIA","free":true}]},{"title":"Running real clusters","desc":"Large jobs need checkpointing so a crashed node doesn't lose days of work, schedulers like Slurm or Kubernetes, and monitoring for hardware failures and loss spikes. This is mostly operational know-how.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Machine Learning Engineering Open Book","url":"https://github.com/stas00/ml-engineering","type":"book","source":"Stas Bekman","free":true},{"title":"torchtitan","url":"https://github.com/pytorch/torchtitan","type":"tool","source":"PyTorch","free":true}]}]},{"id":"interpretability","title":"Mechanistic Interpretability","tier":5,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["transformers","reading-papers"],"roles":{"rs":"r"},"summary":"Mechanistic interpretability tries to reverse-engineer neural networks: find the features a model represents and the circuits that connect them, so we can understand how it actually computes an answer.","why":"If we can read what a model is doing inside, we can catch deception, debug failures, and verify safety in ways that testing outputs alone can't. It is one of the most active research areas in AI safety.","depth":"This is research-level work. Research scientists in the field must master transformer internals, the residual stream view, superposition, sparse autoencoders, and attribution methods, and be able to run experiments in TransformerLens or nnsight. Everyone else only needs the big idea: models store many concepts as directions in activation space, and we can now find and steer some of them.","hours":80,"outcomes":["You can explain the residual stream, attention heads as information movers, and why superposition makes neurons hard to read.","You can train or load a sparse autoencoder and inspect the features it finds.","You can run activation patching to find which components cause a behavior.","You can reproduce a known circuit (such as induction heads) in a small model.","You can read current circuit-tracing papers and name their limitations."],"project":"Using TransformerLens on GPT-2 small, find the induction heads: show their attention pattern on repeated random tokens, then ablate them and measure the loss jump on the repeated half. Next, explore features for a concept of your choice on Neuronpedia and write a short post on what you found and what you couldn't explain.","resources":[{"title":"ARENA (Chapter 1: Transformer Interpretability)","url":"https://www.arena.education/","type":"course","source":"ARENA","free":true},{"title":"How To Become A Mechanistic Interpretability Researcher","url":"https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/jP9KDyMkchuv6tHwm/how-to-become-a-mechanistic-interpretability-researcher","type":"article","source":"Neel Nanda","free":true},{"title":"Transformer Circuits Thread","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"On the Biology of a Large Language Model","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2025/attribution-graphs/biology.html","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"TransformerLens","url":"https://github.com/TransformerLensOrg/TransformerLens","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true},{"title":"Neuronpedia","url":"https://www.neuronpedia.org/","type":"interactive","source":"Neuronpedia","free":true},{"title":"Tracing the thoughts of a large language model","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/tracing-thoughts-language-model","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"The transformer circuits view","desc":"Think of a transformer as a residual stream that attention heads and MLPs read from and write to. This framing makes it possible to trace how information flows.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2021/framework/index.html","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Features and superposition","desc":"Models represent more concepts than they have neurons by packing them into overlapping directions. This is why single neurons often look like a mix of unrelated things.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Toy Models of Superposition","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2022/toy_model/index.html","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Sparse autoencoders and dictionary learning","desc":"A sparse autoencoder learns to rewrite activations as a sparse mix of features, many of which match human-readable concepts. This has been scaled to production models.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Towards Monosemanticity","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Scaling Monosemanticity","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2024/scaling-monosemanticity/","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Causal methods: patching and circuit tracing","desc":"Activation patching swaps internal activations between runs to see what changes the output. Attribution graphs trace step-by-step how features combine to produce an answer.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Circuit Tracing: Revealing Computational Graphs in Language Models","url":"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2025/attribution-graphs/methods.html","type":"paper","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"circuit-tracer","url":"https://github.com/decoderesearch/circuit-tracer","type":"tool","source":"GitHub","free":true}]},{"title":"Probing and steering","desc":"Linear probes test whether a concept is readable from activations. Steering adds a direction to the activations to push behavior, for example toward honesty or a topic.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Representation Engineering","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01405","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Steering Language Models With Activation Engineering","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10248","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Open problems and critiques","desc":"Features found by SAEs are incomplete, explanations may not be faithful, and it is unclear how well current methods scale to safety guarantees. Know the debate before you pick a project.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Open Problems in Mechanistic Interpretability","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.16496","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"alignment-research","title":"AI Alignment Research","tier":5,"lane":"safety","prereqs":["rlhf","interpretability"],"roles":{"rs":"r"},"summary":"Alignment research asks how to make AI systems reliably pursue what their developers and users actually intend, even as systems become more capable than the people overseeing them.","why":"Current training methods produce models that mostly behave well, but studies already show reward hacking, sycophancy, and models that act differently when they think they're being watched. These problems get harder as models become more autonomous.","depth":"This is research-level work for research scientists. Master the core problems (specification gaming, reward hacking, deceptive alignment, scalable oversight) and the main empirical methods (model organisms, control evaluations, CoT monitoring). You don't need to master every agenda. Pick one area and go deep after reading broadly.","hours":100,"outcomes":["You can explain outer vs inner alignment, specification gaming, and reward hacking with real examples.","You can describe the main research agendas: scalable oversight, interpretability, AI control, model organisms, and evaluation.","You can read and critique a recent alignment paper, including its threat model and what the result does and doesn't show.","You can design a small empirical experiment that tests an alignment hypothesis on an open model."],"project":"Replicate a small model-organism result on an open-weight model. For example, fine-tune a small model on a narrow bad behavior and measure whether it generalizes to unrelated prompts, or build a simple reward-hacking environment and show the model exploiting it. Write it up with your threat model, method, results, and what would change your conclusion.","resources":[{"title":"BlueDot Impact AI safety courses","url":"https://bluedot.org/courses","type":"course","source":"BlueDot Impact","free":true},{"title":"Concrete Problems in AI Safety","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06565","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Alignment Science Blog","url":"https://alignment.anthropic.com/","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"AI Safety, Ethics, and Society","url":"https://www.aisafetybook.com/","type":"book","source":"Center for AI Safety","free":true},{"title":"ARENA","url":"https://www.arena.education/","type":"course","source":"ARENA","free":true},{"title":"AI Alignment Forum","url":"https://www.alignmentforum.org/","type":"article","source":"Alignment Forum","free":true},{"title":"MATS research program","url":"https://www.matsprogram.org/","type":"course","source":"MATS","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Core problems: specification gaming and reward hacking","desc":"Models optimize the objective you wrote, not the one you meant. This shows up as agents exploiting bugs in environments and LLMs gaming tests or graders.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity","url":"https://deepmind.google/blog/specification-gaming-the-flip-side-of-ai-ingenuity/","type":"article","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true},{"title":"Reward Hacking in Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2024-11-28-reward-hacking/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true}]},{"title":"Scalable oversight","desc":"How do humans supervise a model that knows more than they do? Ideas include debate, AI-assisted feedback, constitutional methods, and weak-to-strong generalization.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Weak-to-Strong Generalization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.09390","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (OpenAI)","free":true},{"title":"Constitutional AI","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true}]},{"title":"Deception and model organisms","desc":"Researchers build models with known bad behaviors to study whether safety training removes them. Results show some behaviors persist and models can fake alignment during training.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Alignment faking in large language models","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/alignment-faking","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Sleeper Agents","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05566","type":"paper","source":"arXiv (Anthropic)","free":true}]},{"title":"AI control","desc":"Assume the model might be misaligned and design protocols (monitoring, trusted editing, limited permissions) that stay safe anyway. This is measured with red-team vs blue-team evaluations.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"AI Control: Improving Safety Despite Intentional Subversion","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.06942","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Chain-of-thought monitoring","desc":"Reasoning models think in readable text, which gives a chance to spot bad intent. That chance is fragile, because training pressure can make reasoning less faithful or less readable.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Chain of Thought Monitorability","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.11473","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Generalization surprises","desc":"Narrow training can have broad effects. Fine-tuning on insecure code, for example, has made models misaligned in unrelated chats. Understanding why is an open question.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Emergent Misalignment","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.17424","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"multimodal-models","title":"Multimodal Model Architectures","tier":5,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["transformers","diffusion"],"roles":{"rs":"r","mle":"a"},"summary":"Multimodal models take in and produce more than one kind of data, such as text, images, audio, and video. Architectures range from gluing a vision encoder onto an LLM to natively multimodal models trained on mixed token streams from the start.","why":"Frontier assistants now see screenshots, read documents, hear speech, and generate images. Knowing how the pieces connect explains their strengths and blind spots, and it is where much current research and product work happens.","depth":"Must master: contrastive image-text training (CLIP), the encoder-plus-projector-plus-LLM recipe, and the difference between late fusion and early fusion. Just know the details of audio tokenizers, real-time speech models, and unified generation models unless you research them.","hours":35,"outcomes":["You can explain how CLIP aligns images and text in a shared embedding space.","You can sketch how a vision-language model turns image patches into tokens an LLM can attend to.","You can compare adapter-style, early-fusion, and unified understanding-plus-generation architectures.","You can read a recent multimodal model report and identify its encoders, connector, data mix, and training stages."],"project":"Build a tiny vision-language model: freeze a pretrained SigLIP or CLIP image encoder and a small open LLM, train only a projection layer (then optionally LoRA) on an image-caption dataset, and evaluate how well it captions and answers simple questions about held-out images.","resources":[{"title":"Multimodality and Large Multimodal Models (LMMs)","url":"https://huyenchip.com/2023/10/10/multimodal.html","type":"article","source":"Chip Huyen","free":true},{"title":"Understanding Multimodal LLMs","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/understanding-multimodal-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true},{"title":"Generalized Visual Language Models","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2022-06-09-vlm/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Vision Language Models Explained","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/vlms","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision (CLIP)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00020","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Visual Instruction Tuning (LLaVA)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08485","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Contrastive pretraining (CLIP, SigLIP)","desc":"Train an image encoder and a text encoder so matching pairs land close together in embedding space. These encoders power zero-shot classification, search, and the vision side of many VLMs.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00020","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15343","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Vision-language models with adapters","desc":"Take a pretrained vision encoder, project its outputs into the LLM's embedding space, and train on image-text instruction data. LLaVA made this recipe simple and popular.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Visual Instruction Tuning (LLaVA)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08485","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Understanding Multimodal LLMs","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/understanding-multimodal-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true}]},{"title":"Cross-attention and resampler designs","desc":"Instead of feeding image tokens straight in, some models let the LLM attend to visual features through extra cross-attention layers. Flamingo and BLIP-2 are the reference designs.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Flamingo: a Visual Language Model for Few-Shot Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.14198","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"BLIP-2","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.12597","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Early fusion and unified generation","desc":"Natively multimodal models train on interleaved text and image tokens from the start, and some can generate images as well as read them. Chameleon and Transfusion show two approaches: discrete image tokens versus mixing next-token prediction with diffusion.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Chameleon: Mixed-Modal Early-Fusion Foundation Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09818","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Transfusion","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.11039","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Speech and audio","desc":"Whisper showed that large-scale weakly supervised training makes robust speech recognition. Newer speech-to-speech models handle listening and talking in real time with audio tokens.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Robust Speech Recognition via Large-Scale Weak Supervision (Whisper)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04356","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Moshi: a speech-text foundation model for real-time dialogue","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00037","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Multimodal data and evaluation","desc":"Data quality (captions, interleaved documents, OCR-heavy images) often matters more than architecture. Benchmarks for charts, documents, and video reasoning reveal where models still fail.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Vision Language Models (Better, Faster, Stronger)","url":"https://huggingface.co/blog/vlms-2025","type":"article","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}]}]},{"id":"world-models","title":"World Models & Embodied AI","tier":5,"lane":"dl","prereqs":["multimodal-models"],"roles":{"rs":"a"},"summary":"A world model is a learned simulator: given the current state and an action, it predicts what happens next. Embodied AI puts such models, plus vision-language-action policies, into robots and agents that act in physical or virtual worlds.","why":"Many researchers see world models as a path past text-only intelligence, toward agents that plan, imagine consequences, and handle the physical world. Interactive video generators and robot foundation models made this an active frontier in 2025 and 2026.","depth":"This is frontier research. Must understand: the core idea of learning dynamics and planning inside a model, the difference between generative (pixel-predicting) and latent (JEPA-style) world models, and how vision-language-action models work. Just follow the rest through papers and lab blogs.","hours":50,"outcomes":["You can explain what a world model is and how an agent can learn or plan inside one.","You can compare pixel-level generative world models with latent predictive approaches like JEPA.","You can describe how vision-language-action models turn camera input and instructions into robot actions.","You can read a current world-model or robotics paper and identify its data, model, and evaluation setup."],"project":"Reproduce a mini world model: collect rollouts from a simple Gymnasium environment (like CarRacing or a grid world), train a VAE plus a recurrent dynamics model on them, and train a small controller entirely inside the learned model. Then test it in the real environment and note where the dream and reality diverge.","resources":[{"title":"World Models (interactive paper)","url":"https://worldmodels.github.io/","type":"interactive","source":"Ha & Schmidhuber","free":true},{"title":"Genie 3: A new frontier for world models","url":"https://deepmind.google/blog/genie-3-a-new-frontier-for-world-models/","type":"article","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true},{"title":"Mastering Diverse Domains through World Models (DreamerV3)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04104","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"V-JEPA 2: Self-Supervised Video Models Enable Understanding, Prediction and Planning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09985","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"pi-zero: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.24164","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"LeRobot","url":"https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Model-based reinforcement learning basics","desc":"Model-free RL learns what to do by trial and error; model-based RL first learns how the world responds, then plans or trains inside that model. You need the RL vocabulary of states, actions, rewards, and policies.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction","url":"http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html","type":"book","source":"Sutton & Barto","free":true},{"title":"Spinning Up in Deep RL","url":"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/","type":"course","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"Learning to dream: World Models, Dreamer, MuZero","desc":"Agents learn a compact model of their environment and practice inside it. Dreamer and MuZero showed that planning in a learned latent space can master games and control tasks.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"World Models (interactive paper)","url":"https://worldmodels.github.io/","type":"interactive","source":"Ha & Schmidhuber","free":true},{"title":"Mastering Diverse Domains through World Models (DreamerV3)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04104","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Interactive video world models","desc":"Large video models trained on huge amounts of footage can generate explorable, controllable worlds frame by frame. Genie and similar systems hint at simulators for training agents.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Genie: Generative Interactive Environments","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15391","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Genie 3: A new frontier for world models","url":"https://deepmind.google/blog/genie-3-a-new-frontier-for-world-models/","type":"article","source":"Google DeepMind","free":true}]},{"title":"Latent predictive models (JEPA)","desc":"Instead of predicting every pixel, JEPA-style models predict abstract representations of what comes next. Proponents argue this is more efficient and better for planning.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Self-Supervised Learning from Images with a Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08243","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"V-JEPA 2","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09985","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Vision-language-action models for robots","desc":"VLAs start from a vision-language model and learn to output robot actions, so web knowledge transfers to physical tasks. RT-2, OpenVLA, and pi-zero are key examples.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15818","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09246","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Simulation, sim-to-real, and robot data","desc":"Robot data is scarce, so teams combine simulation, teleoperation, and synthetic video from world foundation models. Open toolkits make it possible to experiment with low-cost arms.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"LeRobot","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/index","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true},{"title":"Cosmos World Foundation Model Platform for Physical AI","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.03575","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"reasoning-models","title":"Reasoning Models & Test-time Compute","tier":5,"lane":"llm","prereqs":["rlhf","pretraining"],"roles":{"rs":"c","mle":"r","aie":"a"},"summary":"Reasoning models are LLMs trained to think through a problem in a long chain of thought before answering. They improve by spending more compute at inference time (test-time compute), and are mostly trained with reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers.","why":"Reasoning models made large jumps on math, coding, science, and agentic tasks, and 'thinking' modes are now standard in frontier assistants. Understanding how they are trained and where they fail is central to current research and to using them well.","depth":"This is frontier research. Must understand: chain-of-thought, the test-time compute trade-off, RL with verifiable rewards, and process versus outcome rewards. Just follow the fast-moving details (search methods, length control, faithfulness of reasoning traces) through papers and lab blogs.","hours":40,"outcomes":["You can explain how chain-of-thought and test-time compute improve accuracy, and when they don't.","You can describe how RL with verifiable rewards (as in DeepSeek-R1) produces long reasoning behavior.","You can compare parallel sampling, self-consistency, search, and sequential thinking as ways to spend inference compute.","You can reason about open problems such as faithfulness of reasoning traces, overthinking, and reward hacking."],"project":"Take a small open model (0.5B to 3B) and a math dataset with checkable answers such as GSM8K. Measure accuracy with direct answers, chain-of-thought, and majority voting over 8 samples. Then run a short GRPO training job with TRL using a correctness reward, and plot accuracy and average response length over training.","resources":[{"title":"Why We Think","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2025-05-01-thinking/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true},{"title":"Understanding Reasoning LLMs","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/understanding-reasoning-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true},{"title":"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Learning to reason with LLMs","url":"https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true},{"title":"Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03314","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Building with extended thinking","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/extended-thinking","type":"docs","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Open R1","url":"https://github.com/huggingface/open-r1","type":"tool","source":"Hugging Face","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Chain-of-thought and self-consistency","desc":"Asking a model to show its steps improves multi-step reasoning. Sampling several reasoning paths and taking a majority vote improves it further.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Scaling test-time compute","desc":"Spending more compute at inference, through longer thinking or more samples, can beat using a bigger model. The best strategy depends on problem difficulty.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally can be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03314","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Learning to reason with LLMs","url":"https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/","type":"article","source":"OpenAI","free":true}]},{"title":"RL with verifiable rewards","desc":"Train on problems whose answers can be checked automatically, like math and unit-tested code, and reward correct final answers. DeepSeek-R1 showed long reasoning, self-checking, and backtracking emerge from this.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Understanding Reasoning LLMs","url":"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/understanding-reasoning-llms","type":"article","source":"Sebastian Raschka","free":true}]},{"title":"Process versus outcome rewards","desc":"Outcome rewards check only the final answer; process reward models score each step. Step-level feedback helps search and verification but is costly to label.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Let's Verify Step by Step","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.20050","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Distilling and bootstrapping reasoning","desc":"Small models can learn reasoning by fine-tuning on traces from stronger models, sometimes with only about a thousand examples. Self-training methods like STaR bootstrap from the model's own correct traces.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"s1: Simple test-time scaling","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19393","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"STaR: Bootstrapping Reasoning With Reasoning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.14465","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Faithfulness and open problems","desc":"A model's written reasoning does not always reflect what actually drove its answer, which matters for monitoring and safety. Overthinking easy problems and wasting tokens is another active issue.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Reasoning models don't always say what they think","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/reasoning-models-dont-say-think","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Search over thoughts","desc":"Tree-of-thoughts and similar methods explore several branches of reasoning and back up when stuck. Explicit search has partly given way to learned long chains of thought, but the ideas recur.","level":"optional","resources":[{"title":"Tree of Thoughts: Deliberate Problem Solving with Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]}]},{"id":"autonomous-agents","title":"Long-horizon Autonomous Agents","tier":5,"lane":"agents","prereqs":["multi-agent","llm-evals"],"roles":{"aie":"a","rs":"r"},"summary":"Long-horizon autonomous agents work on tasks that take hours or days with little human input, like building a feature end to end or running a small business process. The open problems are staying on track, managing memory across many context windows, recovering from errors, and knowing when to ask for help.","why":"The length of tasks agents can complete reliably has been doubling roughly every several months, which makes this one of the fastest-moving areas in AI. Understanding the limits, and how to measure them, matters for anyone building or betting on agents.","depth":"This is frontier territory. Read the key engineering posts, benchmarks, and papers, and run your own long experiments. Know the research directions (memory, self-improvement, verification, safety of autonomy); nobody has mastered them yet.","hours":30,"outcomes":["You can explain how long-horizon ability is measured (for example METR's task-length metric, SWE-bench) and the limits of those measures.","You can design a harness for multi-session work: progress files, checkpoints, tests, and context resets.","You can analyze why long runs fail (drift, compounding errors, reward hacking, giving up) from real transcripts.","You can discuss safety concerns of autonomy, such as oversight, permissions, and monitoring."],"project":"Give a coding agent a multi-day project (for example, a small web app with 30 listed features) using a harness with a feature list, progress file, and tests. Let it run across many sessions with minimal intervention, then analyze the transcripts: where it drifted, what it broke, and which harness changes helped most.","resources":[{"title":"Effective harnesses for long-running agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"SWE-bench: Can Language Models Resolve Real-World GitHub Issues?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06770","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Demystifying evals for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"Project Vend: Can Claude run a small shop?","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/project-vend-1","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true},{"title":"LLM Powered Autonomous Agents","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/","type":"article","source":"Lilian Weng","free":true}],"subtopics":[{"title":"Measuring task horizon","desc":"METR measures the length of tasks (in human-expert time) an agent can complete with 50% reliability; this has grown exponentially. SWE-bench and its successors test real software tasks.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Tasks","url":"https://metr.org/blog/2025-03-19-measuring-ai-ability-to-complete-long-tasks/","type":"article","source":"METR","free":true},{"title":"SWE-bench","url":"https://www.swebench.com/","type":"tool","source":"SWE-bench","free":true}]},{"title":"Harnesses for multi-session work","desc":"Agents that span many context windows need structure: an initializer that sets up the environment, a feature list, progress notes, git commits, and tests so each new session can pick up cleanly.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Effective harnesses for long-running agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Memory and context over long runs","desc":"Compaction, external memory files, and retrieval over past actions let agents keep going past one context window. Deciding what to remember and forget is still an open problem.","level":"core","resources":[{"title":"Effective context engineering for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Self-improvement and skill libraries","desc":"Research agents like Voyager build a growing library of reusable skills; Reflexion learns from verbal feedback on failures. Modern agent skills and memory systems build on these ideas.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16291","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true},{"title":"Reflexion: Language Agents with Verbal Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366","type":"paper","source":"arXiv","free":true}]},{"title":"Real-world autonomy experiments","desc":"Experiments like Anthropic's Project Vend, where Claude ran a small shop, show how agents behave over weeks: creative, but prone to odd mistakes and being talked into bad deals.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"Project Vend: Can Claude run a small shop?","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/research/project-vend-1","type":"article","source":"Anthropic","free":true}]},{"title":"Oversight and safety of autonomy","desc":"More autonomy means less human checking. Open questions include scalable oversight, monitoring agent behavior, sandboxing, and preventing reward hacking or harmful side effects.","level":"deep","resources":[{"title":"The lethal trifecta for AI agents","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/","type":"article","source":"Simon Willison","free":true}]}]}]};
